window.BiologyATAR = [{"id": "bio11-classification", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Ecosystems and biodiversity", "title": "Biodiversity, classification and evolutionary relationships", "scope": "Genetic, species and ecosystem diversity; species concepts; taxonomy; keys; evidence for relationships.", "screens": [{"title": "Biodiversity at three levels", "kind": "learn", "text": "Biodiversity describes variation in living systems at several connected scales. Genetic diversity is variation in alleles and other genetic sequences within a species or population. Species diversity concerns the number of species and their relative abundances. Ecosystem diversity concerns the variety of ecological systems, including their habitats and interactions. A reserve containing many species can still contain small, genetically restricted populations. Conversely, a single widespread species can contain substantial genetic variation across its range. State which level you are measuring before comparing sites. Counts of species alone cannot establish genetic diversity or ecosystem function. Conservation decisions need evidence at appropriate spatial and temporal scales, with comparable methods so that differences do not simply reflect sampling effort.", "diagram": "diversity"}, {"title": "Richness and evenness", "kind": "learn", "text": "Species richness is the number of species recorded in a defined area or sample. Evenness describes how similarly individuals are distributed among those species. A sample with ten individuals from each of four species has the same richness as a sample with 37 individuals of one species and one individual of each of three others, but greater evenness. The samples therefore differ in species diversity despite identical richness. More sampling often reveals additional species, so compare equal effort, comparable areas and appropriate seasons. A low recorded richness can indicate real ecological differences or incomplete detection. Explain what a measure captures and what it misses rather than claiming a single count describes every aspect of biodiversity.", "diagram": "evenness"}, {"title": "Hierarchical classification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Taxonomy places organisms in nested groups. A common hierarchy is domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species. Broader taxa contain several narrower groups, and organisms in the same species share all the higher ranks in that scheme. A taxon is a named group at any level. Binomial names use the genus followed by the specific epithet: the genus starts with a capital letter and the epithet is lower case, with both italicised when typed. International naming conventions help researchers communicate despite different common names. A classification is a scientific representation of relationships, not a permanent list immune to new evidence. Taxonomic groupings can change when molecular data reveal relationships that visible traits alone did not resolve.", "diagram": "taxonomy"}, {"title": "Physical, reproductive and molecular evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Classification uses patterns in structural features, methods of reproduction and molecular sequences. Shared inherited features can indicate common ancestry. However, similar environments can favour similar features in unrelated lineages, so superficial resemblance does not necessarily mean close relationship. DNA and protein sequences add many characters for comparison, but the chosen sequence, alignment and evolutionary model affect interpretation. Researchers combine evidence rather than treating one feature as decisive in every case. A newly obtained sequence can support moving a species into a different genus without the organism itself changing. Distinguish biological evolutionary change from revision of the human classification system. Revised classifications reflect improved understanding of ancestry, not evidence that science has no standards.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The biological species concept", "kind": "learn", "text": "The biological species concept groups populations whose members can interbreed under natural conditions and produce viable, fertile offspring, while being reproductively isolated from other groups. It is useful for many sexually reproducing organisms because it focuses on gene flow. It has limitations for asexual organisms, fossils and populations that do not meet geographically. Some recognised species hybridise, and reproductive compatibility can form complicated boundaries. A successful cross in an artificial setting is not automatically proof that two natural populations constitute one species. In an exam, state the definition and explain why the particular organism or evidence limits its application. Avoid assuming that every pair of individuals must actually mate for the concept to be useful.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Morphological and genetic species concepts", "kind": "learn", "text": "A morphological species concept uses consistent differences in observable form, while genetic approaches compare inherited molecular differences. Morphology allows classification when reproductive information is unavailable, including many fossils, but can confuse cryptic species that look alike or separate sexes and life stages of one species. Genetic evidence can reveal hidden lineages, yet the threshold used depends on the organism and evidence rather than one universal percentage difference. Some species share recent ancestry and similar sequences while remaining biologically distinct. Compare concepts using the same example, specifying what can be measured and where ambiguity remains. Species boundaries are scientifically investigated, and different lines of evidence may require a careful judgement rather than a simple rule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Using a dichotomous key", "kind": "learn", "text": "A dichotomous key identifies organisms through a series of paired alternatives. At each step, choose the statement that matches an observable feature and follow its indicated destination. Effective couplets use mutually exclusive descriptions such as wings present or wings absent. Traits should be measurable and appropriate to the specimens, not vague opinions such as attractive or large. The key is useful only for the organisms and life stages it was designed to cover. Identification using a key is different from proving evolutionary relationships: the easiest feature for recognition may not be the most informative feature for ancestry. When constructing a key, ensure every listed organism reaches a unique terminal identification without gaps, circular routes or overlapping choices.", "diagram": "key"}, {"title": "Reading a phylogenetic tree", "kind": "learn", "text": "A phylogenetic tree proposes evolutionary relationships. A branch point, or node, represents an inferred common ancestor, and a clade includes an ancestor and all its descendants. The most closely related tips share the most recent common ancestor, not necessarily the shortest printed distance between their names. Rotating branches around a node does not change the relationship. Branch lengths represent time or amount of change only when a scale or legend says so. Living species at the tips are not usually direct ancestors of other living tips. Read the branching pattern and the evidence supporting it, then state uncertainty where appropriate. A tree based on one gene may not capture every aspect of species history.", "diagram": "phylogeny"}, {"title": "Classification changes and international cooperation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Naming conventions, shared specimen collections and international sequence databases help scientists compare biodiversity across places and times. Classification revisions require evidence and discussion among researchers. Historical surveys may have used earlier names, so an apparent disappearance in a database can reflect synonymy or reclassification rather than extinction. Conservation assessments therefore need consistent identification and traceable records. Modern taxonomy draws on morphology, reproduction, genetics and ecology, and researchers should report uncertainty when evidence is incomplete. Scientific agreement is strengthened by reproducible methods and scrutiny, not by simply counting opinions. Explain how a specific technology or additional evidence changes the confidence or resolution of a classification, rather than saying computers make all identification automatically correct.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Applying biodiversity measures to conservation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose two fictional wetlands each contain six recorded bird species. One has a balanced abundance distribution, while the other is dominated by one disturbance-tolerant species. Richness alone cannot justify the conclusion that the sites are equally diverse. Add evenness, habitat information, survey effort and whether the species are native or threatened. Genetic data would be needed to compare variation within a particular bird population. Seasonal migrants can also change counts without indicating permanent habitat change. A defensible comparison identifies the measure, cites the observations and acknowledges detection limits. Conservation value may include threatened species, distinctive habitats and cultural significance as well as a diversity index. A numerical measure supports a decision but does not replace explanation of ecological context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Biodiversity and identification", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Biodiversity and identification</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">What it measures</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence or limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Genetic diversity</td><td>Inherited variation within a species</td><td>Allele/sequence sampling; counts alone cannot establish it</td></tr><tr><td>Richness</td><td>Number of species recorded</td><td>Standardise area, effort and season</td></tr><tr><td>Evenness</td><td>Similarity of relative species abundances</td><td>Identical richness can have different evenness</td></tr><tr><td>Biological species</td><td>Natural reproductive compatibility/isolation</td><td>Limited for fossils, asexuals and separated populations</td></tr><tr><td>Morphological concept</td><td>Consistent observable differences</td><td>Cryptic species and life-stage variation complicate it</td></tr><tr><td>Phylogeny</td><td>Inferred ancestry and clades</td><td>Read nodes; printed distance is not the measure</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Compare wetlands", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Compare wetlands", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Species concept", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Species concept", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Read a tree", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Read a tree", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Construct a key", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Naming evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Convergent traits", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Conservation recommendation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Compare wetlands", "prompt": "Fictional wetland A has species counts 10,10,10,10; B has 37,1,1,1. Compare richness and evenness and explain why the species counts cannot establish genetic diversity.", "steps": ["[1] Both samples contain four species, so richness is equal.", "[2] A has greater evenness because abundances are equal; B is dominated by one species.", "[1] Equal richness therefore does not establish equal species diversity.", "[2] Genetic diversity concerns inherited variation within a species; DNA or allele data from that population are required."], "allocations": [1, 2, 1, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-classification-q1"}, {"title": "Species concept", "prompt": "Two fossil shells have different shapes. Explain how morphology could support classification and why the biological species concept cannot be tested directly.", "steps": ["[2] Consistent structural differences across comparable specimens can support separate morphological species.", "[2] Fossils cannot be crossed to test natural interbreeding and production of viable fertile offspring.", "[2] Different ages, life stages or sexes could explain the shapes, so multiple specimens and independent evidence strengthen the inference."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-classification-q2"}, {"title": "Read a tree", "prompt": "A rooted tree has topology (D,(C,(A,B))). Which pair is most closely related? Explain why moving the printed labels or rotating branches does not change the conclusion.", "steps": ["[1] A and B are the closest pair.", "[2] They share a more recent common ancestor with each other than either does with C or D.", "[1] Rotation preserves connections and common ancestors; physical label distance is not a relatedness measure."], "allocations": [1, 2, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-classification-q3"}, {"title": "Construct a key", "prompt": "Four fictional insects have these traits: A wings and one antenna pair; B no wings and one antenna pair; C wings and two antenna pairs; D no wings and two antenna pairs. Construct a dichotomous key and give one limitation.", "steps": ["[2] First couplet: wings present → winged pair; wings absent → wingless pair.", "[2] In winged pair: one antenna pair → A; two → C.", "[2] In wingless pair: one antenna pair → B; two → D.", "[1] The key identifies only these stipulated specimens and relevant life stages."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 1], "marks": 7, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-classification-q4"}, {"title": "Naming evidence", "prompt": "A scientist moves a species to a new genus after comparing DNA. Explain what changed and give the correct typed form of a binomial name.", "steps": ["[2] The classification changed in response to evidence of evolutionary relationships; reclassification itself does not change the organism.", "[2] A binomial has capitalised genus and lower-case specific epithet, both italicised, such as Homo sapiens with both words in italics.", "[1] Traceable synonyms help connect older records to the revised name."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-classification-q5"}, {"title": "Convergent traits", "prompt": "Two unrelated desert plants have reduced leaves. Explain why this resemblance alone cannot demonstrate close ancestry and suggest better evidence.", "steps": ["[2] Similar selection pressures can independently favour reduced leaves that lower water loss.", "[1] This is convergence and the similar feature can be analogous.", "[2] Compare several homologous structures and aligned DNA or protein sequences to test relationship."], "allocations": [2, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-classification-q6"}, {"title": "Conservation recommendation", "prompt": "A proposed reserve contains six common bird species. Another contains four species including a threatened species and several distinct habitats. Evaluate a decision based only on richness and propose a defensible assessment. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Richness favours the first reserve but measures species number only.", "[2] Evenness and abundance can reveal domination or rare populations that richness hides.", "[2] Threatened species and population viability can make the second reserve important despite lower richness.", "[2] Habitat variety indicates ecosystem diversity and should be assessed directly.", "[2] Genetic sampling could assess variation within target populations; bird counts alone cannot.", "[2] Compare equal sampling effort and seasons and include habitat connectivity, cultural values and uncertainty before recommending management."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-classification-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Two samples each contain four species but one is dominated by a single species. What differs?", "o": ["Evenness", "Necessarily richness", "The definition of an allele", "The number of taxonomic ranks"], "a": 0, "why": "Evenness describes relative abundances; richness is four in both samples."}, {"q": "What best tests genetic diversity within a population?", "o": ["Variation in inherited sequences or alleles", "Only number of habitats", "Only number of recorded species", "Only total individuals"], "a": 0, "why": "Within-species genetic diversity requires evidence of inherited variation, not a species count alone."}, {"q": "Which rank lies between family and species in the common hierarchy?", "o": ["Genus", "Domain", "Order", "Kingdom"], "a": 0, "why": "The sequence includes order, family, genus and species, with genus containing related species."}, {"q": "Which organisms most limit direct use of the biological species concept?", "o": ["Asexual organisms and fossils", "All sexually reproducing birds", "Only organisms with cells", "Only members of one genus"], "a": 0, "why": "The concept centres on natural reproductive compatibility, which cannot be directly tested in fossils or applied in the same way to asexual lineages."}, {"q": "How are sister tips recognised in a phylogenetic tree?", "o": ["By the most recent shared node", "By nearest printed names", "By having the longest labels", "By appearing on the left"], "a": 0, "why": "Topology and common ancestry determine inferred relationship; rotating a branch does not change it."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-fieldwork", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Ecosystems and biodiversity", "title": "Ecological fieldwork, data and scientific inquiry", "scope": "Quadrats, transects, capture–recapture, sampling, abiotic measurements, uncertainty and reports.", "screens": [{"title": "From an ecological question to a hypothesis", "kind": "learn", "text": "A research question identifies the relationship being investigated. A directional hypothesis predicts the direction of the relationship and should define measurable variables. For example, increasing canopy cover is predicted to reduce the percentage cover of a light-demanding plant. Canopy cover is the explanatory variable and plant cover is the response measure. In a field survey the researcher may measure existing variation rather than manipulate it, so the result supports an association rather than automatically demonstrating cause. Operational definitions specify how each variable will be measured, such as canopy cover estimated from a consistent photographic method and plant cover from a gridded quadrat. Define the population, locations, sampling time and units before collecting data.", "diagram": "inquiry"}, {"title": "Random and systematic sampling", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random placement gives eligible locations a known chance of selection and reduces deliberate location bias. Coordinates can be generated within a defined sampling area, but inaccessible sites require a documented rule rather than choosing whichever replacement looks convenient. Systematic sampling uses regular intervals and is useful across an environmental gradient. Stratified sampling divides the area into relevant habitat strata and samples each, often in proportion to its area. A convenient sample beside a path may overrepresent disturbed conditions. The method should match the question, distribution of organisms and practical constraints. Randomness alone does not guarantee representativeness when the frame excludes important habitats. Report the number of independent samples and the area to which the conclusion applies.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Quadrats for abundance and cover", "kind": "learn", "text": "A quadrat defines a known area for sampling sessile organisms or organisms that can be counted within that boundary. Count individuals, record presence or absence, or estimate percentage cover depending on the question. Density equals the number of individuals divided by the sampled area. A grid helps estimate cover, but overlapping plant layers can complicate interpretation, so define whether cover refers to one species, ground occupancy or separate layers. Use a consistent boundary rule to avoid counting organisms differently between samples. Choose quadrat size to suit organism size and distribution. Several independent quadrats across the habitat generally provide better information than repeatedly measuring one small patch. Quadrat sampling cannot reliably estimate every mobile animal population.", "diagram": "quadrat"}, {"title": "Transects across an environmental gradient", "kind": "learn", "text": "A line transect follows a defined line across a habitat and records organisms contacting the line or observations at specified points. A belt transect samples a strip, often using quadrats at regular intervals. Transects are useful for examining changes with distance from shore, a stream or a disturbed edge. Record abiotic variables at the same locations so biological patterns can be compared with environmental conditions. Replicate the transect in independent positions rather than treating neighbouring observations on one line as independent habitats. Correlation with distance may reflect several changing variables simultaneously. Describe the sampling intervals and orientation clearly enough for another investigator to repeat the method. A transect diagram should show direction, distances, quadrat placement and units.", "diagram": "transect"}, {"title": "Capture–recapture population estimates", "kind": "learn", "text": "The basic capture–recapture estimate is N ≈ M × C ÷ R, where M is the number marked and released initially, C is the number captured in the later sample and R is the number of marked individuals recaptured. It assumes a reasonably closed population, thorough mixing, retained recognisable marks and similar capture probability for marked and unmarked organisms. Marking must not alter survival or behaviour and should meet ethical requirements. If 40 animals are marked, 50 are caught later and ten are marked, the estimate is 200 animals. This is an estimate, not a complete census. Low recapture counts can produce unstable estimates, and zero recaptures make this simple formula unusable rather than implying an infinite population.", "diagram": "recapture"}, {"title": "Measuring abiotic conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Useful abiotic measurements include temperature, light intensity, soil or water pH, moisture, salinity, dissolved oxygen and substrate characteristics. Use the correct instrument, calibrate where necessary, record units and standardise the depth, height and timing of readings. Soil temperature at midday cannot be fairly compared with an early-morning surface reading from another site without considering that difference. Repeat measurements to describe spatial and temporal variation. An instrument displaying many decimal places is not necessarily accurate to all of them. Record the resolution and relevant limitations. Avoid altering the location immediately before measuring it, for example by shading a light sensor with your body. Link each measured variable to a biological mechanism when interpreting its relationship with organisms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sample estimates, mean and spread", "kind": "learn", "text": "For counts 2, 4, 4, 5 and 10, the mean is 25 ÷ 5 = 5 and the median is the middle ordered value, 4. The minimum–maximum interval is 2–10, while the statistical range width is 10 − 2 = 8. The 2025 examiner report advises giving minimum and maximum values when an exam asks for a range of values, so make the meaning explicit. Spread matters: similar means can conceal very different distributions. Calculate density using the total sampled area or clearly report the mean per quadrat. Estimate a larger population only when the samples reasonably represent that area. An estimate with apparently precise digits can still have substantial uncertainty because organisms are clumped or detection is incomplete.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Tables and graphs that answer the question", "kind": "learn", "text": "A results table needs clear variable names, units in headings and a consistent arrangement. Use a bar or column graph for separate categories, a line or scatter representation for appropriate continuous relationships, and a histogram for frequencies across intervals of a continuous variable. Label axes, use sensible scales and include both variables in the title. Interpolate cautiously between observations and avoid confident extrapolation beyond the measured range. A trend statement should cite numerical evidence, such as a fall in mean density from 18 to six individuals per square metre. Error bars need a legend stating what they represent. A graph displays a pattern; explaining why it occurs requires biological reasoning and consideration of alternative causes.", "diagram": "graphs"}, {"title": "Reliability, validity, accuracy and precision", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reliability concerns consistency of measurements or results when procedures are repeated. Repeated independent samples help reveal variability. Validity concerns whether the procedure tests the intended relationship, including appropriate controls and a representative sampling design. Accuracy concerns closeness to the accepted or true value, while precision concerns closeness among repeated measurements or the resolving capability of a method in its context. A consistently miscalibrated probe can produce precise but inaccurate readings. Increasing replicates helps estimate variability but does not automatically remove a systematic bias. Match each suggested improvement to a stated limitation and explain how it improves the evidence. For example, calibrating the probe addresses instrument bias, while sampling more habitat patches addresses limited spatial representation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Fieldwork ethics, safety and communication", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fieldwork requires permission, a risk assessment and methods that minimise disturbance. Avoid damaging rare plants, introducing pathogens on equipment or marking animals in a way that changes survival. Replacement, reduction and refinement guide animal research: use alternatives where suitable, justify numbers and improve procedures to reduce harm. Consider cultural access and custodianship when planning work on Country. A scientific report normally connects a question and hypothesis to a reproducible method, results, analysis, conclusion and evaluation, with referenced sources. Distinguish observations from interpretations and disclose relevant limitations. Use fictional or supplied datasets when practical work cannot be safely conducted. The online lesson can prepare you for fieldwork, but reading about a technique is not evidence that you have completed a practical investigation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choosing and reporting field methods", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Choosing and reporting field methods</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Method</th><th scope=\"col\">Suitable use</th><th scope=\"col\">Critical checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Quadrat</td><td>Sessile abundance, presence or cover</td><td>Known area, boundary rules, representative independent placements</td></tr><tr><td>Transect</td><td>Change across a gradient</td><td>Consistent distances and multiple independent transects</td></tr><tr><td>Capture–recapture</td><td>Some mobile populations: N=M×C/R</td><td>Closed population, mixing, persistent harmless marks, equal capture likelihood</td></tr><tr><td>Random sampling</td><td>Reduce location-selection bias</td><td>Suitable frame; documented inaccessible-location rules</td></tr><tr><td>Systematic sampling</td><td>Regular intervals</td><td>Avoid alignment with hidden periodic habitat pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Calibration</td><td>Check measurement accuracy</td><td>A consistent offset is not cured by repetition</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Explore the numbers: capture", "kind": "interactive", "model": "capture"}, {"title": "Worked question: Capture–recapture", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Capture–recapture", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Quadrat density", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Quadrat density", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Validity or reliability", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Validity or reliability", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transect design", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Recapture uncertainty", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Graph choice", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Evaluate a survey", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Capture–recapture", "prompt": "In a fictional closed population, 40 animals are marked and released. A second sample of 50 contains 10 marked animals. Estimate population size and explain two assumptions.", "steps": ["[2] N = M × C / R = 40 × 50 / 10 = 200 animals.", "[2] The population remains closed between samples, so births, deaths and migration do not substantially alter proportions.", "[2] Marks persist without affecting capture or survival; marked animals mix with others and have comparable capture probability."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q1"}, {"title": "Quadrat density", "prompt": "Five independent 0.5 m² quadrats contain 2,4,4,5,10 plants. Calculate mean density and explain what the spread says about the estimate.", "steps": ["[1] Total count is 25 plants across 2.5 m².", "[1] Density is 25 / 2.5 = 10 plants m⁻².", "[1] Counts span 2–10; the range width is 8.", "[2] Variable counts suggest patchiness or sampling variability; more independent representative samples improve the estimate but do not remove systematic bias."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q2"}, {"title": "Validity or reliability", "prompt": "Every soil probe reads 2°C too high, but repeated readings at one site differ by less than 0.1°C. Evaluate precision and accuracy and recommend a correction.", "steps": ["[1] Repeated readings are precise because their spread is small.", "[2] They are inaccurate because a consistent calibration error shifts them away from the true temperature.", "[1] Calibrate against an appropriate standard or replace the faulty probe; repetition alone does not remove the systematic error."], "allocations": [1, 2, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q3"}, {"title": "Transect design", "prompt": "Design a field investigation of plant cover along a dune-to-inland moisture gradient. Include sampling, measurement and one limit on causal conclusions.", "steps": ["[2] Place several independent transects across the gradient using a justified location method.", "[2] Record cover in equal-area quadrats at systematic distances and measure moisture consistently at comparable depths and times.", "[2] Replicate across transects and control or record relevant soil/light differences.", "[1] Association does not alone establish moisture as the cause because other variables covary."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 1], "marks": 7, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q4"}, {"title": "Recapture uncertainty", "prompt": "A mark–recapture estimate uses M=30, C=60, R=6. Calculate N and predict the bias if marked animals avoid the trap on the second visit.", "steps": ["[2] N=30×60/6=300 animals.", "[2] Avoidance reduces R relative to expectation and inflates the estimate, assuming M and C remain fixed.", "[1] The equal-capture-probability assumption is violated."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q5"}, {"title": "Graph choice", "prompt": "A student surveys canopy cover (%) and abundance of a plant (individuals m⁻²) at 20 sites. Choose a graph and explain two reporting features.", "steps": ["[1] Use a scatterplot of the paired quantitative measurements.", "[2] Label both axes with variable names and units, using an appropriate readable scale.", "[2] Show all observations and describe direction, spread and unusual points without implying experimental causation."], "allocations": [1, 2, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q6"}, {"title": "Evaluate a survey", "prompt": "A class samples only quadrats beside a path at noon, counts leaves rather than individuals, and reports that the entire woodland has low biodiversity. Evaluate the investigation and redesign it. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Path-side convenience sampling can overrepresent disturbed habitat and exclude woodland interiors.", "[2] Leaves are not independent individuals or a measure of species richness; define the actual response measure.", "[2] Use a sampling frame spanning habitats, with random coordinates or justified stratified sampling.", "[2] Use consistent quadrat areas, boundary rules, identification and measurement times.", "[2] Collect enough independent replicates and report variation and detection limitations.", "[2] Limit inference to the surveyed scope; compare seasons or repeat surveys before generalising about the whole woodland."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-fieldwork-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What most reduces deliberate quadrat-location bias?", "o": ["Random coordinates within a suitable frame", "Choosing the greenest sites", "Choosing only beside paths", "Taking the first visible patch"], "a": 0, "why": "Random placement reduces selection bias, but the sampling frame must still include relevant habitats."}, {"q": "Which method best describes sampling at regular distances along a gradient?", "o": ["Systematic transect sampling", "Unstructured convenience sampling", "A genetic test cross", "A secretory pathway"], "a": 0, "why": "Systematic intervals along transects reveal changes across a gradient, ideally with independent transects."}, {"q": "For M=20,C=40,R=8, what is estimated population size?", "o": ["100", "68", "10", "800"], "a": 0, "why": "Capture–recapture uses M×C/R, so 20×40/8=100 under its assumptions."}, {"q": "What does repeating a miscalibrated probe fail to remove?", "o": ["Systematic bias", "All random fluctuation", "Independent replication", "Every observation"], "a": 0, "why": "A consistent offset persists through repetition; calibration is needed to address it."}, {"q": "Why is a field correlation not automatically causal?", "o": ["Other variables may covary", "All surveys are useless", "A scatterplot removes confounding", "Large samples prove causation"], "a": 0, "why": "Observational associations can reflect confounders; sampling precision alone does not establish a cause."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "23 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-interactions", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Ecosystems and biodiversity", "title": "Habitats, ecological niches and species interactions", "scope": "Biotic and abiotic factors; niches; competition, predation, symbiosis, disease and keystone species.", "screens": [{"title": "Levels of ecological organisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "An organism is an individual living thing. A population consists of individuals of one species occupying a defined place and time. A community includes populations of different species interacting in an area. An ecosystem includes that community and its abiotic environment, with transfers of energy and cycling of matter. A habitat is the place an organism lives, whereas its niche describes how it uses resources and interacts with its environment. A wetland can contain several habitats and many species occupying distinct niches. State the level being considered when explaining a change: mortality in individuals may alter population density, which may then change community interactions. The same event can have consequences at multiple levels without making the terms interchangeable.", "diagram": "ecologicallevels"}, {"title": "Biotic and abiotic components", "kind": "learn", "text": "Biotic factors involve living organisms and their activities, such as food availability, predation, competitors and pathogens. Abiotic factors are physical or chemical conditions, including temperature, water, light, pH, salinity and substrate. The distinction depends on the factor, not whether it is beneficial. Dead organic material is usually treated as a biological resource in ecological explanations, while the oxygen concentration it influences is a chemical condition. Identify the specific factor and its mechanism. For example, lower soil moisture can restrict plant water uptake, which affects growth and therefore food availability for herbivores. Multiple factors interact, so one measurement may not explain every difference between habitats.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Tolerance ranges and limiting factors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Organisms have ranges of conditions under which survival, growth and reproduction are possible. Performance may be greatest near an optimum and reduced toward tolerance limits. Different life stages can have different limits, and acclimatisation can alter short-term responses within bounds. A limiting factor is a resource or condition that restricts performance or population growth under the circumstances. Adding more of a resource that is already abundant may have little effect because another factor limits growth. A species distribution can reflect temperature tolerance, resource availability, competitors and barriers to dispersal. Do not assume a species is absent solely because one measured abiotic factor is unsuitable. Test the explanation against observations and plausible alternatives.", "diagram": "tolerance"}, {"title": "Niches and resource partitioning", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ecological niche includes resource use, environmental requirements, timing of activities and interactions with other organisms. A species' potential or fundamental niche can be broader than its realised niche when competition or other biotic influences restrict its distribution. Resource partitioning reduces overlap, for example when bird species feed at different heights or use different food sizes. It can allow coexistence without requiring the species to become entirely isolated. The competitive exclusion principle predicts that species using the same limiting resources in the same way cannot coexist indefinitely in a stable environment. Real habitats vary through space and time, so coexistence does not automatically disprove the principle. Explain which resource is limiting and how the species differ in its use.", "diagram": "niche"}, {"title": "Intraspecific and interspecific competition", "kind": "learn", "text": "Intraspecific competition occurs among members of one species, while interspecific competition occurs between species. Both can involve limited food, water, light, space or reproductive opportunities. Competition can reduce growth, survival or reproductive output even when organisms never physically fight. Plants can compete through uptake of soil water and shading. Members of the same species often have strongly overlapping needs, so intraspecific competition may be intense as density rises. A lower abundance beside another species is not alone proof of competition, because habitat differences may explain the pattern. A controlled removal or carefully designed comparison can provide stronger evidence. Link the limited resource to the observed effect rather than identifying competition solely from organisms being close together.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Predator–prey relationships", "kind": "learn", "text": "Predation transfers biomass when one organism consumes another. Predator abundance and prey abundance can influence each other, often with a time lag because changes in survival and reproduction take time. More prey can support additional predators, and increasing predation can subsequently reduce prey abundance. Other factors, including disease, alternative foods and weather, also affect the populations, so real records need not show a neat repeated cycle. Predation can alter behaviour as well as numbers: prey may avoid particular habitats or feeding times. An exam prediction should trace the causal links and acknowledge the supplied conditions. Avoid claiming every predator increase immediately causes a proportional prey decrease or that a single predator controls all features of an ecosystem.", "diagram": "predatorprey"}, {"title": "Mutualism and commensalism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Symbiosis describes close interactions between species, and examples include mutualism, commensalism and parasitism. In mutualism, both partners benefit, such as a pollinator obtaining food while transferring pollen between flowers. Benefits may depend on context and are measured relative to the alternatives. In commensalism, one partner benefits while the other has no detected substantial benefit or harm, such as an epiphyte using a tree for support without taking its nutrients. Absence of a measured effect is not proof that no effect can ever occur. Explain the resource or service gained by each species. Collaboration within a species can also improve survival or reproduction, such as cooperative defence, but it should not automatically be labelled an interspecific symbiosis.", "diagram": "symbiosis"}, {"title": "Parasitism and ecological disease", "kind": "learn", "text": "In parasitism, a parasite benefits by obtaining resources from a host, while imposing a cost on that host. Parasites can live externally or internally and may alter growth, reproduction or survival. Disease is an ecological interaction that can change population density and community structure. Transmission may increase when susceptible hosts interact more frequently, but it also depends on the pathogen's life cycle, environmental conditions and host defences. A parasite need not kill its host immediately; destroying the resource too quickly can reduce its opportunities for transmission. Distinguish the parasite, host and disease outcome. The ecological consequences extend beyond an individual infection, for example when loss of a dominant plant changes shade and resources for other species.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Keystone species and trophic effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "A keystone species has an influence on community structure that is disproportionately large relative to its abundance or biomass. It need not be the most numerous species. A predator can prevent one prey species from dominating, allowing other species to persist. Removing that predator may initiate a trophic cascade through altered consumption and competition. The effect depends on the particular ecosystem and remaining interactions. Identifying a keystone role requires evidence, such as changes after removal, not simply calling an admired species important. Single-species protection can be useful but may fail if habitat, ecological relationships or other threats are neglected. Evaluate both the evidence for its ecological role and whether conservation measures protect the processes that support the wider community.", "diagram": "keystone"}, {"title": "Explaining a food-web disturbance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose a fictional estuary loses a predator that normally consumes large herbivorous fish. Herbivore abundance may increase, leading to more grazing on aquatic vegetation. Reduced vegetation can remove habitat for juvenile animals and alter sediment stability, so consequences extend beyond a simple count of fish. The prediction relies on assumed links: alternative predators, food limitation or disease could modify the outcome. Use the supplied food web and distinguish direct effects from indirect effects. State which organisms change, the direction predicted and the mechanism connecting each change. Food webs omit interaction strength, seasonal shifts and abiotic feedbacks unless these are explicitly included. A model can guide a testable prediction without guaranteeing that every link produces the expected response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ecological relationships", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Ecological relationships</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Relationship</th><th scope=\"col\">Effect</th><th scope=\"col\">Mechanism/example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mutualism</td><td>Both benefit</td><td>Fungus gains sugars; plant gains mineral access in an appropriate mycorrhizal association</td></tr><tr><td>Commensalism</td><td>One benefits, other approximately unaffected</td><td>Assess actual costs rather than assuming none</td></tr><tr><td>Parasitism</td><td>Parasite benefits; host harmed</td><td>Resource use and damage</td></tr><tr><td>Predation</td><td>Predator consumes prey</td><td>Can cause delayed population responses</td></tr><tr><td>Competition</td><td>Shared limiting resource reduces success</td><td>Within or between species; resource partitioning may reduce overlap</td></tr><tr><td>Keystone effect</td><td>Disproportionate community impact</td><td>Abundance alone does not establish ecological importance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Interaction signs", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Interaction signs", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Niche restriction", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Niche restriction", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Predator removal", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Predator removal", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Resource partitioning", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Lagged cycles", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Limiting factors", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Keystone management", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Interaction signs", "prompt": "A fungus receives sugars from a tree and improves its mineral uptake; a tick feeds on a mammal and damages its skin. Classify both interactions and explain their effects.", "steps": ["[2] The fungus–tree interaction is mutualism: both receive a stated benefit.", "[2] The tick–mammal interaction is parasitism: the tick benefits while its host is harmed."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-interactions-q1"}, {"title": "Niche restriction", "prompt": "A plant survives across pH 5–8 in isolation but occurs only at pH 5–6 when a competitor is present. Distinguish fundamental and realised niches.", "steps": ["[2] The fundamental niche includes the tolerated conditions without the stated competitor, here pH 5–8.", "[2] The realised niche is the occupied range under biotic interactions, here pH 5–6.", "[1] Competition can restrict occupation even where abiotic conditions allow survival."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-interactions-q2"}, {"title": "Predator removal", "prompt": "In a fictional shore, a predator consumes grazers. Predator removal is followed by more grazers and fewer algae. Explain a plausible trophic cascade and one alternative explanation.", "steps": ["[2] Reduced predation allows grazer survival or abundance to increase.", "[2] Increased grazing can remove more algal biomass, reducing algae.", "[1] Changed nutrients, temperature or sampling could also alter algae; the sequence alone does not establish the predator as the sole cause."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-interactions-q3"}, {"title": "Resource partitioning", "prompt": "Two bird species feed on the same tree but one mainly uses outer branches and the other the trunk. Explain how this pattern could permit coexistence.", "steps": ["[2] Use of different parts reduces overlap in exploited food or feeding space.", "[2] Lower interspecific competition can permit coexistence if other needs are met.", "[1] The observation is consistent with partitioning but requires diet and resource evidence to establish its mechanism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-interactions-q4"}, {"title": "Lagged cycles", "prompt": "A prey population peaks in March; its predator peaks in May. Explain a plausible lag and why this does not prove an inevitable fixed cycle.", "steps": ["[2] More prey can improve predator survival and reproduction, which takes time to increase predator abundance.", "[2] Greater predation can then reduce prey, followed by reduced food for predators.", "[1] Other environmental factors and time series are needed; a single pair of peaks does not prove a repeating cause."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-interactions-q5"}, {"title": "Limiting factors", "prompt": "A waterway contains adequate light and nutrients but dissolved oxygen drops below a fish species’ tolerance. Explain why adding nutrients may not increase fish abundance.", "steps": ["[2] Oxygen is a limiting abiotic condition even when other requirements are sufficient.", "[2] Additional nutrients can increase algal growth and subsequent decomposition, worsening oxygen demand.", "[1] Survival depends on multiple interacting requirements rather than one resource in isolation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-interactions-q6"}, {"title": "Keystone management", "prompt": "A fictional predator is proposed for removal because it is uncommon. Explain why abundance alone is insufficient and design an evidence-based assessment of ecological impact. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A keystone species can have a disproportionate community effect relative to abundance.", "[2] Removing it may release prey or competitors and alter a trophic cascade.", "[2] Map feeding relationships and collect comparable abundance and habitat measurements before intervention.", "[2] Use replicated comparison sites or a justified before–after control design and monitor relevant confounders.", "[2] Measure community responses and variation rather than only counting the target species.", "[2] Weigh conservation, ethical and cultural impacts, consider reversible alternatives and report uncertainty in attribution."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-interactions-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Which sign pair represents parasitism for parasite then host?", "o": ["Benefit and harm", "Benefit and benefit", "No effect and benefit", "Harm and harm"], "a": 0, "why": "The parasite gains a resource while the host incurs a cost; mutualism benefits both."}, {"q": "What can restrict a realised niche relative to a fundamental niche?", "o": ["Competition", "Binomial spelling alone", "The printed width of a tree", "An unchanged taxonomic label"], "a": 0, "why": "Biotic interactions such as competitors can prevent occupation of otherwise physiologically suitable conditions."}, {"q": "What defines a keystone species?", "o": ["Disproportionate community influence", "Always highest abundance", "Always largest body size", "Always a producer"], "a": 0, "why": "Keystone effects are large relative to abundance and may involve cascades through community interactions."}, {"q": "How can resource partitioning reduce competition?", "o": ["By reducing overlap in resource use", "By eliminating every predator", "By making all species identical", "By abolishing limited resources"], "a": 0, "why": "Different times, places or resources can reduce direct competition while not eliminating all ecological limits."}, {"q": "Why can predator abundance peak after prey abundance?", "o": ["Predator reproduction responds with a delay", "Predators are always plants", "A graph determines breeding", "Prey deliberately schedule predator growth"], "a": 0, "why": "Improved prey availability can increase predator survival/reproduction, but demographic responses take time."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-energy-cycles", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Ecosystems and biodiversity", "title": "Energy flow, food webs and carbon and nitrogen cycles", "scope": "Trophic levels, productivity, biomass pyramids, decomposition, carbon cycling and nitrogen transformations.", "screens": [{"title": "Producers capture energy and build biomass", "kind": "learn", "text": "Most ecosystems receive their main energy input from sunlight. Photosynthetic producers convert some light energy into chemical energy in organic molecules, using inorganic carbon and other resources. Chemosynthetic producers use energy from chemical reactions in some environments, showing why sunlight is the primary source rather than the only possible source. Biomass is living biological material, commonly estimated as dry mass to reduce variation caused by water content. Producers provide organic matter that supports consumers and decomposers. Light reaching an ecosystem is not all absorbed or converted into biomass. Explain the energy conversion separately from the movement of carbon atoms: energy changes form, whereas matter is incorporated into molecules and can later cycle through the environment.", "diagram": "foodweb"}, {"title": "Trophic levels and food-web arrows", "kind": "learn", "text": "A trophic level describes an organism's feeding position. Producers form the first level; primary consumers eat producers, and secondary or higher consumers feed on other consumers. Omnivores can feed at more than one level, so a food web gives a more realistic account than a single chain. Arrows conventionally point from the food organism to the organism consuming it, showing the direction of transfer of matter and chemical energy. Decomposers receive material from multiple trophic levels, including dead organisms and wastes. Include the arrow convention in a diagram rather than assuming the reader knows it. A food web shows possible feeding relationships, but usually does not quantify rates, abundance or the strength of each interaction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Energy transfer and respiratory losses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Energy available to a higher trophic level is lower because some biomass is not eaten, some ingested material is not assimilated, and much assimilated energy supports metabolism and is released as heat through respiration. Organisms do not deliberately waste energy; it is used to maintain living processes. Heat is dispersed and does not return to producers as a usable replacement for sunlight. Ecological transfer efficiency can be estimated as energy incorporated at the higher level divided by energy at the lower level, multiplied by 100%. It varies among organisms and ecosystems. The familiar ten-percent value is an approximate teaching example, not a universal law. Always use the values supplied in the question instead of imposing that percentage.", "diagram": "energyflow"}, {"title": "Gross and net primary production", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gross primary production is the total rate at which producers fix energy in organic material. Net primary production is the portion remaining after the producers' own respiratory use: NPP = GPP − R. NPP provides new plant biomass that may support consumers or become detritus. Productivity is a rate, so include units such as kilojoules per square metre per year or grams of dry mass per square metre per year. Standing biomass is an amount measured at a time and should not be confused with productivity. A rapidly reproducing producer population can have high productivity despite low standing biomass. Distinguish energy flows, biomass stocks and rates when interpreting a pyramid, table or comparison between ecosystems.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Energy, biomass and number pyramids", "kind": "learn", "text": "An energy pyramid represents transfer through trophic levels over a stated time and area and is upright because available energy decreases through successive transfers. A biomass pyramid represents standing biomass and can be inverted in aquatic systems where rapidly reproducing phytoplankton support a larger standing biomass of consumers. A pyramid of numbers can also be inverted, for example when a few large trees support many herbivorous insects. These differences do not contradict energy conservation because the representations measure different quantities. Label the pyramid type, trophic levels and units. Do not infer a numerical energy transfer efficiency from a biomass pyramid unless the question supplies the assumptions or rates required to make that inference.", "diagram": "pyramids"}, {"title": "Detritivores, decomposers and recycling", "kind": "learn", "text": "Detritivores ingest dead organic material and physically fragment it, while decomposers such as many fungi and bacteria release enzymes and absorb products of decomposition. These activities return inorganic nutrients to forms that producers can use. Decomposer respiration also returns carbon dioxide to the atmosphere or water and releases heat. Decomposition rates depend on conditions such as temperature, moisture, oxygen and the nature of the material. Without decomposition, nutrients can remain locked in organic matter, restricting further production. Distinguish nutrient recycling from energy recycling: matter can be reused, but a continuing energy input is needed because energy is progressively dissipated as heat. Microorganisms are active components of food webs and biogeochemical cycles, not merely background organisms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Carbon cycling between stores", "kind": "learn", "text": "Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere or water and incorporates carbon into organic compounds. Feeding transfers that carbon through food webs. Respiration by plants, animals and microorganisms returns carbon dioxide, while decomposition can also transfer carbon into soils and sediments. Some carbon enters long-lived geological stores, and combustion of fossil fuels releases stored carbon into the atmosphere. A carbon store is an amount, whereas a carbon flux is a transfer per unit time. Land clearing can both release carbon and reduce future uptake, but the net effect depends on the system and timescale. Trace the carbon atoms through specific processes rather than saying carbon simply disappears when energy is used.", "diagram": "carbon"}, {"title": "Nitrogen fixation, nitrification and assimilation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Most organisms cannot directly use atmospheric nitrogen gas to build proteins and nucleic acids. Nitrogen-fixing microorganisms convert nitrogen gas into biologically usable nitrogen compounds, with some bacteria living in association with legumes. Nitrifying microorganisms oxidise ammonium to nitrite and then nitrate under suitable conditions. Plants can absorb nitrate or ammonium and assimilate nitrogen into organic molecules, which are transferred to consumers through feeding. These processes have distinct names and directions, and nitrogen fixation should not be confused with plants absorbing nitrate. Soil oxygen, pH, water availability and microbial activity affect transformations. Fertiliser additions alter nitrogen availability, but excessive additions can move beyond plant uptake and affect surrounding ecosystems.", "diagram": "nitrogen"}, {"title": "Ammonification and denitrification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Decomposition of nitrogen-containing organic material releases ammonium through ammonification. Denitrifying microorganisms convert nitrate into gaseous nitrogen forms, returning nitrogen to the atmosphere, commonly under oxygen-limited conditions. These transformations connect organic material, soil or water and the atmosphere. A complete nitrogen-cycle explanation identifies the starting and ending forms and the role of microorganisms. It should not treat all bacteria as carrying out every transformation. Runoff of nitrogen compounds can enrich aquatic environments, causing excessive growth of algae and increasing decomposer oxygen demand after that material dies. This connection shows how changes to a biogeochemical cycle can affect species distributions and survival. A diagram simplifies the chemistry, so annotate its arrows with processes rather than leaving every transfer unexplained.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Data interpretation across a trophic system", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a fictional system, producers incorporate 20,000 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹ as net production and herbivores incorporate 2,400 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹. The estimated transfer efficiency is 2,400 ÷ 20,000 × 100 = 12%. The remaining energy has several destinations, including uneaten material, unassimilated material and respiratory heat. It cannot all be described as heat from herbivore respiration. If secondary consumers incorporate 240 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹, their transfer efficiency relative to herbivores is 10%, while their energy is 1.2% of producer production. State the denominator and units clearly. Calculations support the explanation but do not substitute for identifying the biological processes that account for the pattern.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Energy and nitrogen bookkeeping", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Energy and nitrogen bookkeeping</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term/process</th><th scope=\"col\">Relationship</th><th scope=\"col\">Avoid this confusion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NPP</td><td>GPP − producer respiration</td><td>Productivity is a rate, not standing biomass</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer efficiency</td><td>Next-level production / previous-level production ×100</td><td>Not a universal fixed 10%</td></tr><tr><td>Fixation</td><td>Atmospheric N₂ → available reduced nitrogen</td><td>Plant uptake is not nitrogen fixation</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrification</td><td>Ammonium → nitrite → nitrate</td><td>Different from denitrification</td></tr><tr><td>Ammonification</td><td>Organic nitrogen → ammonium</td><td>Decomposition releases matter, not new sunlight</td></tr><tr><td>Denitrification</td><td>Nitrate → gaseous nitrogen forms</td><td>Often promoted by low-oxygen conditions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Transfer efficiency", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Transfer efficiency", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Net production", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Net production", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Nitrogen transformations", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Nitrogen transformations", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Inverted biomass", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Carbon explanation", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Decomposers", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Nutrient pollution", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Transfer efficiency", "prompt": "A fictional ecosystem stores 20,000 kJ m⁻² y⁻¹ of producer production, 2,400 in herbivore production and 240 in secondary-consumer production. Calculate both successive transfer efficiencies.", "steps": ["[2] Producer→herbivore efficiency = 2400/20000×100 = 12%.", "[2] Herbivore→secondary efficiency = 240/2400×100 = 10%.", "[1] The values are efficiencies for this dataset, not a universal 10% rule."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q1"}, {"title": "Net production", "prompt": "Gross primary productivity is 1,500 and producer respiration is 600 kJ m⁻² y⁻¹. Calculate NPP and distinguish a productivity rate from biomass.", "steps": ["[2] NPP=GPP−R=1500−600=900 kJ m⁻² y⁻¹.", "[2] Productivity is production per area per time; biomass is the amount present at a time.", "[1] Rapid turnover can sustain production even when standing biomass is small."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q2"}, {"title": "Nitrogen transformations", "prompt": "Explain the route from atmospheric N₂ to nitrogen in plant proteins, including nitrogen fixation, nitrification and assimilation.", "steps": ["[2] Nitrogen-fixing microorganisms convert N₂ into biologically available reduced nitrogen forms.", "[2] Nitrifying microorganisms oxidise ammonium to nitrite and then nitrate.", "[2] Plants take up available nitrogen compounds and assimilate nitrogen into amino acids and proteins; uptake is not fixation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q3"}, {"title": "Inverted biomass", "prompt": "A marine ecosystem has lower standing phytoplankton biomass than zooplankton biomass. Explain why this does not imply an inverted energy pyramid.", "steps": ["[2] Phytoplankton can grow and turn over rapidly, supporting consumers despite a small standing stock.", "[2] Energy is lost from transfers through respiration, heat and unconsumed material.", "[1] Biomass is a stock while energy flow must be compared over equivalent times and areas."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q4"}, {"title": "Carbon explanation", "prompt": "Trace carbon from atmospheric CO₂ into a herbivore and back to the atmosphere through respiration.", "steps": ["[2] Photosynthesis fixes carbon from CO₂ into organic compounds in producers.", "[1] Feeding transfers organic carbon to the herbivore.", "[2] Cellular respiration oxidises organic compounds and releases CO₂; carbon cycles while usable energy dissipates as heat."], "allocations": [2, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q5"}, {"title": "Decomposers", "prompt": "Explain why decomposers connect multiple trophic levels and why their role does not recycle energy back into sunlight.", "steps": ["[2] Dead material and wastes from producers and consumers supply organic substrates to decomposers.", "[2] Decomposition releases inorganic nutrients that can be taken up again by producers.", "[1] Respiration dissipates chemical energy as heat; matter cycles but energy flows through the system."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q6"}, {"title": "Nutrient pollution", "prompt": "A fictional lake receives nitrate runoff, develops an algal bloom and then loses fish after the bloom dies. Explain the causal chain and recommend two monitoring measures and a management response. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Increased available nutrients can increase algal production where nutrients limit growth.", "[2] Dead algae provide substrate for decomposers.", "[2] Increased aerobic decomposition consumes dissolved oxygen, threatening fish below their tolerance.", "[2] Monitor dissolved oxygen and nutrient concentration consistently across sites and times.", "[2] Reduce nutrient inputs using a justified catchment action such as controlling fertiliser runoff and restoring buffer vegetation.", "[2] Compare before/after and control sites, considering temperature and water flow; removing visible algae alone does not fix the nutrient source."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energy-cycles-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Which way do food-web arrows conventionally point?", "o": ["Food to consumer", "Consumer to food", "Only toward sunlight", "Only toward producers"], "a": 0, "why": "Arrows show transfer of matter and chemical energy from the eaten organism to the consumer."}, {"q": "GPP=1000 and producer respiration=400. What is NPP?", "o": ["600", "1400", "4000", "0"], "a": 0, "why": "Net primary productivity is gross production minus producer respiration over matching units/time."}, {"q": "What may allow an inverted standing-biomass pyramid?", "o": ["Rapid producer turnover", "Energy creation by consumers", "No producer respiration", "Matter becoming sunlight"], "a": 0, "why": "Rapidly replaced producers can support greater consumer standing biomass; energy flow over time still declines through transfers."}, {"q": "Which process converts nitrate to gaseous nitrogen forms?", "o": ["Denitrification", "Nitrogen fixation", "Assimilation into protein", "Photosynthesis"], "a": 0, "why": "Denitrifying microorganisms return nitrogen to gaseous forms, often in oxygen-limited conditions."}, {"q": "Why can a dying algal bloom lower dissolved oxygen?", "o": ["Aerobic decomposers consume oxygen", "All algae stop matter cycling", "Nitrate directly becomes fish", "Sunlight is consumed by microbes"], "a": 0, "why": "Dead organic material fuels decomposition, increasing oxygen demand; this is a central eutrophication mechanism."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-populations", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Ecosystems and biodiversity", "title": "Population dynamics, carrying capacity, succession and fire", "scope": "Population size, density, distribution and composition; limiting factors; growth; succession; Australian fire ecology.", "screens": [{"title": "Population size, density and distribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Population size is the number of individuals in a defined area at a stated time. Density is the number per unit area or volume. Distribution describes where individuals occur, with clumped, uniform and approximately random patterns possible at a particular scale. Clumping may reflect patches of resources, social behaviour or limited dispersal. Uniform spacing may arise from competition or territoriality. The pattern observed depends on the size of the sampled area, so a species can be clumped across a landscape while appearing evenly spaced within a patch. Composition concerns features such as age structure or sex ratio that affect future demographic change. Equal population sizes can therefore conceal important differences in density, distribution and reproductive potential.", "diagram": "distribution"}, {"title": "Births, deaths, immigration and emigration", "kind": "learn", "text": "A population changes through births and immigration adding individuals, and deaths and emigration removing them. A simple accounting model is change in N = B + I − D − E over the stated interval. For a population initially containing 120 animals, 30 births, eight immigrants, 18 deaths and ten emigrants give a net increase of ten and a final population of 130. These counts do not by themselves identify why the rates changed. Food supply, predators, disease and movement barriers can influence them. State the time interval and whether the population is open to movement. A census decline can occur through emigration without widespread mortality, so management explanations need evidence about the contributing processes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Exponential growth under favourable conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "When per-capita growth remains positive and resources do not substantially limit reproduction, a population can show approximately exponential growth. The increase becomes larger as more reproductive individuals contribute offspring, producing a J-shaped curve. This model describes a restricted set of conditions rather than an expectation that populations grow indefinitely. Introduced species may initially grow rapidly in a favourable habitat, but limiting resources and interactions usually become important. A graph of exponential growth has time on the horizontal axis and population size on the vertical axis. Distinguish the rate of increase from the population size itself. A doubling interval can be approximately constant even though the number added during each interval becomes larger.", "diagram": "growth"}, {"title": "Carrying capacity and logistic growth", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carrying capacity is the population size an environment can support over time under its existing resources and conditions. In a simple logistic model, growth slows as density approaches carrying capacity, giving an S-shaped curve. Competition, disease or other density-dependent influences can reduce per-capita growth. Carrying capacity can change with rainfall, habitat damage, food supply or management, so it is not a permanent number for a species. Populations can overshoot and subsequently decline, and real records often fluctuate rather than remaining at an exact plateau. Use the model to predict a direction under specified conditions and evaluate what it omits. A population below carrying capacity can still decline if mortality rises or reproductive conditions become unfavourable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Density-dependent and density-independent factors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Density-dependent effects tend to change with population density. Competition for food, some forms of disease transmission and interactions with predators can become stronger as density rises. Density-independent influences, such as an extreme storm, can affect populations regardless of their starting density, although the eventual impact may still depend on habitat or behaviour. The distinction concerns the relationship between effect and density, not whether the factor is biotic or abiotic in every case. A drought can reduce resources and thereby intensify density-dependent competition as well as directly affecting survival. Explain the mechanism in the particular scenario. Avoid assuming all disease or predation produces a simple proportional relationship with density in every ecological system.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Age structure and population composition", "kind": "learn", "text": "A population containing many juveniles may have future growth potential, but only if those individuals survive and reproduce. A population dominated by older non-reproductive individuals may decline even when its current count is high. Sex ratio matters when reproduction requires partners and one sex is scarce. Reproductive timing, dispersal and social organisation also affect the result. Age-structure diagrams show the distribution among defined age classes, not a guaranteed forecast. Compare proportions as well as absolute numbers and identify which classes contribute offspring. A management programme that counts every animal equally can miss the loss of breeding adults. State what additional information is needed to estimate future change rather than treating a demographic diagram as a complete prediction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Primary and secondary succession", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ecological succession describes changes in community composition and interactions over time. Primary succession begins on a substrate with little or no developed soil or prior community, such as newly exposed rock. Secondary succession follows disturbance where soil or other biological legacies remain, such as after some fires or cleared farmland. Early colonisers can alter light, nutrients and substrate conditions, influencing which organisms establish later. Succession is not always a fixed sequence leading to one permanent climax community. Disturbance, climate, species arrival and existing organisms can change its direction. Identify what remains after the disturbance before classifying the process. Changes in vegetation can affect animals and microorganisms, creating further feedbacks across the community.", "diagram": "succession"}, {"title": "Facilitation, competition and changing conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Early species can facilitate later colonists by contributing organic material, stabilising substrate or reducing harsh conditions. They can also inhibit others through resource competition or chemical effects. Later species may tolerate the changed conditions without depending directly on the pioneers. As vegetation grows, shade and soil moisture can alter the available niches. The resulting community reflects interactions and historical events, not a purposeful march toward a superior ecosystem. A successional model can support a prediction, but evidence should identify the mechanism linking earlier and later stages. Different starting conditions can produce different outcomes after the same type of disturbance. Restoration therefore considers soil, seed sources, dispersal and ongoing threats rather than assuming time alone will recover the desired community.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Fire as a dynamic Australian ecological factor", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fire influences many Australian ecosystems through changes in resources, habitat and the survival or recruitment of species. Some plants resprout from protected structures, while others rely on seed release or germination cues associated with heat or smoke. Species differ in their tolerances and recovery times. Fire frequency, intensity, season and extent all matter: repeated fires before plants mature can reduce populations, while long exclusion can change habitat for other species. Avoid claiming that all Australian plants need fire or that any burn improves biodiversity. Cultural burning knowledge is place-specific and should be treated with respect and appropriate attribution. Evaluating a fire regime requires ecological evidence, knowledge of local conditions and consideration of people and cultural responsibilities.", "diagram": "fire"}, {"title": "Predicting population responses to disturbance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose a fictional fire removes most vegetation but leaves soil, underground plant organs and some seeds. The recovery would usually be described as secondary succession, with its course depending on surviving organisms, seed arrival and later weather. Herbivores may initially decline through loss of food or movement out of the area, while some early-stage species increase. Repeated burning could prevent slow-maturing plants from replenishing their seed bank, reducing their abundance over time. A sound response identifies the population or community affected, the relevant demographic or ecological mechanism and the timescale. It also explains uncertainty: a food-web model and a growth curve may omit direct fire effects, immigration and changing carrying capacity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Population and community changes", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Population and community changes</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence/qualification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Population change</td><td>B+I−D−E</td><td>Use a defined interval and consistent boundaries</td></tr><tr><td>Density</td><td>Individuals per area/volume</td><td>Equal counts in different areas imply different density</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Clumped, uniform or random pattern</td><td>Distinct from population size</td></tr><tr><td>Carrying capacity</td><td>Supported size under given conditions</td><td>Can change with resources and climate</td></tr><tr><td>Primary succession</td><td>Establishment on substrate lacking developed soil/community</td><td>Not a guaranteed identical sequence</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary succession</td><td>Recovery with soil/remnants retained</td><td>Fire responses depend on regime and life histories</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Population budget", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Population budget", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Density effects", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Density effects", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Succession after fire", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Succession after fire", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Carrying capacity", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Age structure", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Distribution", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Fire recommendation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Population budget", "prompt": "A population starts at 120. During one interval there are 30 births, 8 immigrants, 18 deaths and 10 emigrants. Calculate its final size and distinguish immigration from birth.", "steps": ["[2] Final N=120+30+8−18−10=130 individuals.", "[1] The net change is +10.", "[1] Immigration brings existing individuals from outside; births add newly produced individuals within the population."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-populations-q1"}, {"title": "Density effects", "prompt": "Explain why food competition is usually density dependent but a severe frost can be density independent. Give one qualification.", "steps": ["[2] More individuals competing for finite food can increase the per-capita effect of shortage.", "[2] Frost can harm organisms regardless of population density.", "[1] Density can modify exposure or vulnerability, so real effects may interact rather than fit perfectly separate categories."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-populations-q2"}, {"title": "Succession after fire", "prompt": "A burnt woodland retains soil, seeds and surviving roots. Identify the type of succession and explain how it differs from colonisation of newly exposed rock.", "steps": ["[2] It is secondary succession because soil and biological remnants remain.", "[2] Primary succession starts on substrate lacking an established soil/community, such as new rock.", "[1] Survivors and seed banks can speed recovery, but recovery depends on conditions and is not a guaranteed fixed sequence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-populations-q3"}, {"title": "Carrying capacity", "prompt": "A population follows an S-shaped curve and later declines during drought. Explain carrying capacity and why it is not a permanent species constant.", "steps": ["[2] Carrying capacity is the population size supported under specified environmental conditions and resources.", "[2] Resource limitation slows growth as density increases, producing a logistic pattern in a simplified model.", "[2] Drought changes resource availability and can lower carrying capacity; real populations can overshoot or fluctuate."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-populations-q4"}, {"title": "Age structure", "prompt": "Two populations each contain 200 animals. One has mostly breeding adults; the other mostly older non-reproductive animals. Evaluate a prediction based on total size alone.", "steps": ["[2] Equal current size does not imply equal reproductive capacity.", "[2] Breeding adults can contribute offspring, whereas non-reproductive individuals do not directly add births.", "[1] Survival, sex ratio and recruitment data are also needed; age structure is not a guaranteed forecast."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-populations-q5"}, {"title": "Distribution", "prompt": "Distinguish clumped, uniform and random distribution using plausible resource or interaction explanations.", "steps": ["[2] Clumped individuals occur in groups, for example around patchy water or through social aggregation.", "[2] Uniform spacing can arise from territoriality or competition.", "[2] Random distribution lacks a consistent spatial pattern in the defined frame; it requires evidence rather than being assumed from a scattered drawing."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-populations-q6"}, {"title": "Fire recommendation", "prompt": "A manager proposes annual intense burning at every site to increase biodiversity. Evaluate this proposal using succession, species life histories and investigation design. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Fire effects depend on frequency, intensity, season and extent, so one regime is not appropriate everywhere.", "[2] Frequent fire can prevent slow-maturing species from replenishing seed banks.", "[2] Survivors, roots and soil influence secondary succession; repeated intense fire can change these remnants.", "[2] Species differ in fire response, and mosaics or refuges may support different stages and fauna.", "[2] Consult Traditional Owners and local evidence, respecting place-specific cultural burning knowledge and management responsibilities.", "[2] Monitor replicated comparable sites over sufficient time, including abundance, regeneration and confounders, before recommending a justified regime."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-populations-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Which calculation gives population change?", "o": ["Births+immigration−deaths−emigration", "Deaths+births only", "Immigration−births", "All four added"], "a": 0, "why": "Population gains and losses must have appropriate signs over the same interval."}, {"q": "What is characteristic of the simple logistic model?", "o": ["Growth slows near carrying capacity", "Unlimited fixed-rate increase forever", "No resource limitation", "No births occur"], "a": 0, "why": "Density-related limits produce an S-shaped approach to carrying capacity under simplified conditions."}, {"q": "What can change carrying capacity?", "o": ["Changes in resources and environment", "Only a species name", "Only a diagram colour", "Nothing after establishment"], "a": 0, "why": "Carrying capacity is conditional on habitat and resources rather than a permanent species constant."}, {"q": "Which recovery is secondary succession?", "o": ["Recolonisation after fire with soil retained", "Colonisation of bare new rock without soil", "DNA replication", "Formation of a gamete"], "a": 0, "why": "Remaining soil and biological remnants distinguish secondary succession from primary establishment."}, {"q": "Why can equal population counts imply different futures?", "o": ["Age and breeding composition differ", "Every individual produces equal offspring", "Total number fixes fertility", "A census removes all mortality"], "a": 0, "why": "Composition, reproductive timing and survival matter; total size alone does not predict future change."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-conservation", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Ecosystems and biodiversity", "title": "Human impacts, conservation and evaluating management", "scope": "Fragmentation, salinity, invasive species, pollutants, climate change, genetic conservation and ecosystem management.", "screens": [{"title": "Habitat loss, degradation and fragmentation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Habitat destruction removes the conditions organisms need, while degradation reduces habitat quality without necessarily eliminating it completely. Fragmentation breaks a larger habitat into smaller, separated patches. Smaller populations can lose genetic diversity, encounter stronger edge effects and have reduced movement between patches. Roads and cleared land may block dispersal or expose moving animals to additional mortality. Not every species responds identically: some benefit from edges while specialists lose suitable interior habitat. Identify which resources or processes are affected. A total area figure does not fully describe habitat quality or connectivity. Conservation planning therefore considers patch size, configuration, surrounding land use and the biological requirements of the species rather than protecting isolated patches solely because their combined area appears large.", "diagram": "fragmentation"}, {"title": "Erosion and dryland salinity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Vegetation removal can expose soil to erosion and alter water movement. In some Australian landscapes, replacing deep-rooted native vegetation with shallow-rooted crops reduces water uptake, allowing more water to reach groundwater. Rising saline groundwater can bring salts toward the soil surface, affecting plant water uptake and survival. This process is dryland salinity, which depends on local hydrology, geology and land use. Salt-related osmotic effects can make water harder for roots to obtain even when the soil is wet. Erosion can remove nutrients and carry sediment into waterways, affecting aquatic habitats. Link the land-use change to the physical process and biological outcome. Management can include vegetation restoration and hydrological measures, but effectiveness requires local evidence rather than a universal prescription.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Introduced and invasive species", "kind": "learn", "text": "An introduced species occurs outside its original range through human-associated movement. An invasive species spreads and causes substantial ecological or other impacts in the receiving environment. These terms are related but not interchangeable because not every introduced species becomes invasive. Impacts can arise through predation, competition, habitat modification or disease. An invasive predator may reduce native prey that lack effective defences, while an invasive plant can change fuel loads or resource availability. Prevention, early detection and targeted control can be more effective than attempting eradication after widespread establishment. Control methods need evaluation for effects on non-target organisms and ecosystem processes. Explain why the species has an advantage in the particular setting instead of assuming it is inherently more evolved than native species.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Unsustainable resource use and population decline", "kind": "learn", "text": "Harvesting can become unsustainable when removals exceed population replacement over time or damage the habitats supporting future reproduction. The outcome depends on survival rates, age at maturity, reproductive output and the number or type of individuals removed. Taking breeding adults can have a greater long-term effect than an equal count from another life stage. Fisheries and other management systems can use monitoring, seasonal closures, catch limits or habitat protection, but rules should respond to evidence and uncertainty. A short-term high catch does not prove that the population is healthy because greater effort or new technology can maintain catches during decline. Compare effort-standardised data and demographic indicators, and distinguish economic benefits from the biological capacity to sustain the activity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bioaccumulation and biomagnification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bioaccumulation is the build-up of a substance within an organism when uptake exceeds removal. Biomagnification is an increase in concentration across trophic levels, often involving persistent substances retained in tissues. A predator can receive contaminants from many prey organisms, producing higher concentrations than those in individual prey. Not every pollutant biomagnifies; persistence, uptake and elimination properties matter. Use concentration units such as milligrams per kilogram of tissue when comparing trophic levels, rather than comparing total amounts in organisms of different sizes. High contamination can affect reproduction or survival and therefore population dynamics. A food web helps identify possible pathways but does not quantify exposure by itself. Management should address sources and long-term monitoring as well as visible effects on top predators.", "diagram": "biomagnification"}, {"title": "Eutrophication and oxygen depletion", "kind": "learn", "text": "Excess nutrient input can increase algal and plant production in a water body. When large amounts of this organic material die, decomposer respiration can consume dissolved oxygen. Reduced oxygen can stress or kill organisms requiring aerobic respiration, changing community composition. Some blooms also produce toxins or block light, but these effects depend on the organisms and conditions. Eutrophication should not be explained merely as algae using up all the oxygen while photosynthesising. The timing of photosynthesis, respiration and decomposition matters. Nutrients may come from fertiliser runoff, wastewater or other sources. A management proposal should identify the source, expected ecological mechanism and evidence needed to judge whether nutrient reduction improves water quality and biodiversity.", "diagram": "eutrophication"}, {"title": "Climate change and ecosystem responses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Enhanced greenhouse warming results from changes to atmospheric greenhouse gases and the Earth's energy balance. Biological consequences can include shifts in temperature and rainfall, altered seasonal timing, changed ranges, marine heat stress and modifications to fire conditions. Ocean uptake of carbon dioxide also changes seawater chemistry and can affect calcifying organisms. Species differ in their ability to disperse, acclimatise or evolve, and interacting changes can alter food webs. A single unusual season is not by itself evidence of a long-term climate trend. Distinguish weather variation from sustained change and use suitable records. Biological predictions should connect the environmental change to the species' requirements and interactions rather than claiming every organism moves uniformly toward the poles.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Gene banks, seed banks and captive breeding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Genetic conservation aims to preserve variation that supports future survival and reproductive options. Seed banks store appropriate plant material under controlled conditions, but not all seeds tolerate the same storage methods. Gene banks can preserve other biological material or genetic resources. Captive breeding can increase numbers of threatened organisms, but breeding plans need to minimise loss of diversity and avoid excessive relatedness. Reintroduction also requires suitable habitat and control of the original threats. Increasing headcount without maintaining a viable gene pool may provide limited long-term security. Distinguish ex situ conservation outside the natural habitat from in situ protection within it. Evaluate benefits, costs and limitations using the particular species, rather than treating stored material as a complete replacement for a functioning ecosystem.", "diagram": "conservation"}, {"title": "Reserves, restoration and Indigenous rights", "kind": "learn", "text": "Environmental conservation can include revegetation, control of introduced species, habitat corridors and restoration of ecological processes. Management strategies include protected areas and limits on commercial or recreational access. Designation as a reserve requires ecological evidence alongside consideration of competing uses and Indigenous Peoples' rights and responsibilities. The 2027 syllabus clarifies the inclusion of terrestrial and marine conservation areas. Collaboration with Traditional Owners can improve the legitimacy and ecological understanding of decisions, but knowledge should not be treated as freely extractable or universally transferable between places. Define measurable goals and monitor outcomes over time. A reserve boundary alone cannot prevent all threats, particularly mobile pollutants, climate effects and invasive species entering from surrounding areas.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "International cooperation, technology and evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "World Heritage protection, biodiversity hotspot priorities and migratory-species agreements illustrate the need for cooperation beyond one jurisdiction. Shark Bay, the Great Barrier Reef and south-west Western Australia provide relevant contexts, while migratory birds, whales and turtles connect distant habitats. Satellite observations and remote sensors can reveal habitat change and improve repeated monitoring, but ground observations are needed to identify organisms and validate interpretations. Models help compare management options while depending on assumptions and data quality. Debate about single-species conservation concerns whether protecting one target also maintains wider ecosystem interactions. A reasoned evaluation explains the proposed mechanism, cites evidence, identifies uncertainties and considers ecological, social, cultural and economic consequences without pretending that scientific measurements alone determine every value judgement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Threats and management mechanisms", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Threats and management mechanisms</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Issue</th><th scope=\"col\">Mechanism</th><th scope=\"col\">Response to evaluate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fragmentation</td><td>Isolation, edge effects, smaller breeding pools</td><td>Habitat protection and suitable connectivity</td></tr><tr><td>Dryland salinity</td><td>Reduced water uptake can raise saline groundwater</td><td>Restore appropriate hydrology/vegetation with local evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Eutrophication</td><td>Nutrients → growth → decomposition → oxygen demand</td><td>Reduce catchment inputs; monitor O₂ and nutrients</td></tr><tr><td>Bioaccumulation</td><td>Within-organism retention over time</td><td>Measure uptake/elimination and concentration</td></tr><tr><td>Biomagnification</td><td>Higher concentration across trophic levels</td><td>Investigate persistence and dietary transfer</td></tr><tr><td>Ex situ conservation</td><td>Store/breed material outside natural habitat</td><td>Complement habitat and genetic management, not replace them</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Fragmentation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Fragmentation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Pollutant concentrations", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Pollutant concentrations", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Dryland salinity", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Dryland salinity", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Ex situ limits", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Introduced species", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Climate pressures", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Design a conservation plan", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Fragmentation", "prompt": "A road splits a woodland into isolated patches. Explain two population risks and one benefit and limitation of a habitat corridor.", "steps": ["[2] Smaller isolated populations can have stronger drift and inbreeding, reducing genetic variation or fitness.", "[2] Edge effects and restricted movement can reduce usable habitat and access to resources or mates.", "[2] A suitable corridor can restore movement and gene flow, but may also facilitate predators, disease or invasive organisms and must match species needs."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-conservation-q1"}, {"title": "Pollutant concentrations", "prompt": "A fictional persistent pollutant rises from 0.1 mg kg⁻¹ in producers to 1 in herbivores and 8 in predators. Identify the process and distinguish accumulation within one organism.", "steps": ["[2] The increasing concentrations across trophic levels illustrate biomagnification.", "[2] Bioaccumulation is increasing concentration within an organism over time when uptake exceeds elimination.", "[1] Persistence and storage can permit both processes, but the cross-trophic dataset alone measures biomagnification."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-conservation-q2"}, {"title": "Dryland salinity", "prompt": "Explain how replacing deep-rooted perennial vegetation with shallow-rooted crops can increase surface soil salinity.", "steps": ["[2] Reduced year-round water uptake can allow more recharge and a rising water table.", "[2] Saline groundwater moves closer to the surface and evaporation can concentrate salts.", "[2] High salinity lowers soil water potential and can cause ion toxicity, impairing plant survival."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-conservation-q3"}, {"title": "Ex situ limits", "prompt": "Evaluate storing seed in a bank as the only action to protect a threatened plant.", "steps": ["[2] Seed banking can retain some genetic material and provide future propagation material.", "[2] It does not alone preserve habitat, ecological interactions or all genetic diversity.", "[2] Combine representative genetic sampling with habitat protection, threat reduction and monitored restoration; some species have unsuitable seed storage biology."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-conservation-q4"}, {"title": "Introduced species", "prompt": "Explain why an introduced species is not automatically invasive and propose evidence for evaluating its impact.", "steps": ["[2] Introduced means brought outside its natural range; invasive refers to establishment/spread with harmful impacts in the relevant context.", "[2] Measure spread, abundance and effects on native species or ecosystem processes using comparison data.", "[1] Evaluate control benefits against non-target harm and reinvasion risk."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-conservation-q5"}, {"title": "Climate pressures", "prompt": "Explain two different ways climate change could alter a marine ecosystem and one limit on inferring impacts from a single warm year.", "steps": ["[2] Changed temperatures can shift distributions, physiological tolerance or seasonal timing.", "[2] Added dissolved CO₂ alters carbonate chemistry and pH, affecting some calcifying organisms.", "[1] A single year is insufficient to establish the long-term trend; other environmental variables and species-specific responses matter."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-conservation-q6"}, {"title": "Design a conservation plan", "prompt": "A threatened wetland species occurs in three small isolated populations near nutrient runoff. Propose and justify an integrated conservation and monitoring plan. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reduce nutrient inputs and restore habitat quality to address a stated ecological threat.", "[2] Protect relevant terrestrial and aquatic/marine habitats as appropriate, including buffers and hydrological connections.", "[2] Assess population size, breeding and genetic diversity before deciding on corridors or translocation.", "[2] Consider ex situ genetic/seed or breeding support while addressing the habitat needed for reintroduction.", "[2] Work with Traditional Owners and stakeholders, respecting rights, responsibilities and place-specific knowledge.", "[2] Monitor abundance, water quality and genetic measures at comparable sites over time, and adapt the plan if evidence shows poor outcomes or non-target harm."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-conservation-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What distinguishes biomagnification?", "o": ["Rising pollutant concentration across trophic levels", "Rising body length only", "Only accumulation within one animal", "Increasing light intensity"], "a": 0, "why": "Biomagnification compares trophic levels; bioaccumulation concerns uptake and retention within organisms over time."}, {"q": "How can clearing contribute to dryland salinity?", "o": ["Reduced deep-rooted uptake permits groundwater rise", "All salt instantly disappears", "Trees produce every salt ion", "Only airborne pollen changes"], "a": 0, "why": "Hydrological changes can bring saline water toward the surface and concentrate salts through evaporation."}, {"q": "What is a limitation of a wildlife corridor?", "o": ["It can facilitate unwanted movement too", "It guarantees every species breeds", "It creates new alleles intentionally", "It prevents all disease"], "a": 0, "why": "Corridors can support gene flow but also spread predators, disease or invasive organisms, requiring species-specific evaluation."}, {"q": "Why is a seed bank insufficient alone?", "o": ["It does not preserve the whole habitat and interactions", "It stores no genetic material", "It makes all threats vanish", "It guarantees unlimited variation"], "a": 0, "why": "Ex situ storage complements habitat management and cannot by itself maintain all ecosystem processes or restore lost variation."}, {"q": "Which term necessarily describes an organism moved outside its native range?", "o": ["Introduced", "Always invasive", "Always keystone", "Always endemic"], "a": 0, "why": "Introduction concerns origin; invasion and impact require additional evidence and context."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-cells", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – From single cells to multicellular organisms", "title": "Cell structure, organelles and microscopy", "scope": "Cell requirements; prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisation; organelles; microscopy and size calculations.", "screens": [{"title": "Cells as living systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "Cells are the basic structural and functional units of life. They exchange matter with their surroundings, transform energy, carry out regulated chemical reactions and maintain conditions compatible with survival. Inputs include water, ions, nutrients and, for many cells, oxygen. Photosynthetic cells also need suitable light and inorganic carbon. Outputs include metabolic wastes and heat. Different cells have different requirements, so oxygen should not be described as necessary for every living cell. Structure supports function: membranes separate processes, proteins catalyse reactions and genetic information directs many cellular activities. A cell is an interacting system rather than a collection of independent parts. Explain how a structural feature affects a process and therefore contributes to the cell's survival.", "diagram": "cells"}, {"title": "Prokaryotic cells and shared ancestry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Prokaryotic cells have a plasma membrane, cytosol, ribosomes and genetic material, but lack a membrane-bound nucleus and the internal membrane-bound organelles typical of eukaryotes. Their chromosome is usually circular and located in a nucleoid region, with additional plasmids possible. Many have a cell wall, and some have structures such as flagella, but these features are not universal. Prokaryotes are generally smaller than eukaryotic cells, although size alone cannot identify every specimen. Shared features support a common evolutionary history. A bacterial cell is not simply an incomplete animal cell: it has organised, regulated processes suited to its way of life. Distinguish the chromosome from plasmids and the plasma membrane from an external cell wall when drawing or comparing cells.", "diagram": "prokaryote"}, {"title": "Eukaryotes, nuclei and chromosomes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus enclosing chromosomes and membrane-bound organelles that compartmentalise functions. Nuclear pores regulate exchange with the cytoplasm, while the nucleolus participates in ribosome production. Chromosomal DNA is associated with proteins, and genetic material also occurs in mitochondria and, in photosynthetic eukaryotes, chloroplasts. Animal, plant, fungal and protist cells share many features but differ in particular structures and functions. Most plant cells have a cell wall and a large central vacuole, but not every plant cell contains chloroplasts. Roots generally obtain organic compounds from other tissues rather than photosynthesising. Specialised cells may lose structures during development, so textbook cell diagrams represent common patterns rather than a requirement that every cell contains every labelled organelle.", "diagram": "eukaryote"}, {"title": "Ribosomes, rough ER and protein secretion", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ribosomes join amino acids into proteins using information carried by messenger RNA. Free ribosomes commonly make proteins used within the cytosol, while ribosomes associated with rough endoplasmic reticulum make many proteins destined for secretion, membranes or parts of the endomembrane system. The rough ER supports synthesis and early processing, with transport vesicles carrying material toward the Golgi apparatus. The Golgi modifies, sorts and packages proteins for destinations, including secretion by exocytosis. Smooth ER lacks attached ribosomes and participates in functions such as lipid synthesis and, in suitable cell types, detoxification. Explain the path of a secreted protein by linking structures to processes. The Golgi does not translate mRNA, and a vesicle carries a protein rather than replacing the ribosome's synthetic role.", "diagram": "secretion"}, {"title": "Lysosomes, vacuoles and cell walls", "kind": "learn", "text": "Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that break down material within membrane-bound compartments, helping cells recycle components and process engulfed material. Their membranes help separate these enzymes from other cellular contents. Vacuoles store materials and contribute to water balance, and a plant's large central vacuole helps maintain turgor. A plant cell wall containing cellulose provides support and resists excessive expansion, while the plasma membrane selectively controls many exchanges. A wall is not a substitute for the membrane. Cell walls differ among groups: fungal walls commonly contain chitin, and bacterial walls have different composition. Link each structure to a specific function rather than saying all organelles protect the cell. Compartments interact, and their presence or prominence depends on the cell's specialised activities.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Mitochondria and chloroplasts", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mitochondria provide sites for major stages of aerobic respiration. Their folded inner membranes create a large area for electron transport and ATP synthesis, while the matrix contains enzymes involved in other stages. Chloroplasts contain internal thylakoid membranes for light-dependent photosynthetic reactions and a stroma where carbon fixation reactions occur. Chloroplasts and mitochondria both have double membranes and their own genetic material, consistent with their evolutionary origins. Plants contain mitochondria as well as chloroplasts, so plants respire continuously as living organisms. Neither organelle creates energy from nothing: they transform energy and matter through chemical processes. A structure–function explanation identifies the relevant compartment, process and advantage of internal membrane organisation rather than simply associating an organelle with the word energy.", "diagram": "organelles"}, {"title": "Specialisation and cellular organisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Specialised cells have features suited to particular functions, such as a large exchange surface, abundant mitochondria or extensive rough ER. A cell that secretes large quantities of protein may have prominent rough ER and Golgi apparatus because it synthesises and packages those products. A photosynthetic leaf cell may contain many chloroplasts, while a transport cell may be structurally different. Cells of a multicellular organism generally share the same inherited genome but express different sets of genes. Specialisation creates interdependence because one cell type cannot independently perform all functions needed by the organism. State the feature, the process it supports and the effect on function. Larger numbers of an organelle suggest a functional demand but should be interpreted alongside the cell type and evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Light and electron microscopy", "kind": "learn", "text": "Light microscopy uses visible light and can examine living specimens under suitable conditions. Electron microscopy uses electrons and offers much higher resolving power, but routine preparation normally requires non-living material. Transmission electron microscopy shows internal ultrastructure in thin sections, while scanning electron microscopy primarily represents surface features. Magnification enlarges an image; resolution is the ability to distinguish close features as separate. Increasing magnification without improving resolution produces a larger blurred image rather than new detail. Preparation, staining and sectioning can alter specimens, so images are interpreted with awareness of artefacts. Compare the methods according to the question: watching living cell movement and examining fine internal membrane structure have different requirements.", "diagram": "microscopy"}, {"title": "Magnification and scale-bar calculations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Magnification equals image size divided by actual size, with both sizes in the same units. One millimetre equals 1,000 micrometres, and one micrometre equals 1,000 nanometres. If a 20 μm cell appears 40 mm long, convert 40 mm to 40,000 μm and calculate 40,000 ÷ 20 = 2,000 times magnification. A scale bar can remain useful when an image is resized because its printed length changes with the image. If a 10 μm bar measures 15 mm and a cell measures 45 mm, the cell is 30 μm long. Show conversions and units rather than treating μm and mm as interchangeable. Report a sensible precision given the image measurement and avoid implying that an uncertain boundary supports many decimal places.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Biological drawings and changing cell models", "kind": "learn", "text": "A biological drawing should communicate the observed structures clearly, with a title, appropriate scale and labelled features. Use clean lines and connect labels unambiguously to the intended structure. Distinguish what is actually visible in the preparation from an idealised textbook diagram. Labelled teaching models can show invisible processes, but their legends should identify simplification and scale limitations. Improved microscopy and preparation have revised cell models by revealing finer structures and correcting earlier interpretations. The 2027 syllabus emphasises improved technologies and associated preparation techniques in this development. A useful explanation links the technological improvement to the new evidence and the resulting model revision rather than claiming a microscope photograph independently proves every aspect of a cell's function.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Organelle reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Organelle reference</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Structure</th><th scope=\"col\">Role</th><th scope=\"col\">Important distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>Chromosomal DNA; transcription; regulation</td><td>Not present as an enclosed structure in prokaryotes</td></tr><tr><td>Ribosome</td><td>Translation and polypeptide synthesis</td><td>Present in both basic cell organisations</td></tr><tr><td>Rough ER</td><td>Synthesis/processing pathway for many exported or membrane proteins</td><td>Ribosomes perform peptide assembly</td></tr><tr><td>Golgi apparatus</td><td>Modification, sorting and packaging</td><td>Does not transcribe genes</td></tr><tr><td>Mitochondrion</td><td>Major aerobic stages and ATP production</td><td>Plants also possess and use mitochondria</td></tr><tr><td>Chloroplast</td><td>Photosynthesis: thylakoid reactions and stromal carbon fixation</td><td>Not every plant cell has one</td></tr><tr><td>Vacuole/wall</td><td>Storage/turgor and support in typical plant cells</td><td>Wall is not the selectively permeable membrane</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Magnification", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Magnification", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Secreted protein", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Secreted protein", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Compare cells", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Compare cells", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Scale bar", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Plant respiration", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Microscopy choice", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Specialisation and evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Magnification", "prompt": "A cell measures 40 mm on an image and is actually 20 μm long. Calculate magnification, showing unit conversion, and distinguish resolution.", "steps": ["[1] 40 mm = 40,000 μm.", "[2] Magnification=image size/actual size=40,000/20=2,000×.", "[2] Resolution is the ability to distinguish close points as separate; increasing image size alone does not reveal extra resolved detail."], "allocations": [1, 2, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-cells-q1"}, {"title": "Secreted protein", "prompt": "Trace production of a secreted protein through a eukaryotic cell and link each named structure to its function.", "steps": ["[2] DNA is transcribed into RNA in the nucleus; mRNA reaches ribosomes where amino acids are assembled.", "[2] Ribosomes associated with rough ER make proteins entering its processing/transport pathway.", "[2] Transport vesicles carry material to Golgi for modification/sorting; secretory vesicles fuse with the cell membrane in exocytosis."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-cells-q2"}, {"title": "Compare cells", "prompt": "A specimen has DNA, cytoplasm, ribosomes and a membrane but no nucleus or other membrane-bound organelles. Classify its basic cell type and explain why ribosomes alone do not make it eukaryotic.", "steps": ["[2] The described organisation is prokaryotic; DNA occurs in a nucleoid region rather than an enclosed nucleus.", "[2] Both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells use ribosomes to synthesise proteins.", "[1] A cell wall alone also does not decide the classification because several eukaryotic groups have walls."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-cells-q3"}, {"title": "Scale bar", "prompt": "A 10 μm scale bar measures 15 mm in an enlarged image. A cell spans 45 mm in the same image. Calculate actual length and state the effect of resizing the whole image proportionally.", "steps": ["[2] Cell/scale-bar ratio=45/15=3.", "[1] Actual length=3×10=30 μm.", "[2] Proportional resizing preserves this ratio, so the scale bar remains usable although nominal image magnification changes."], "allocations": [2, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-cells-q4"}, {"title": "Plant respiration", "prompt": "A student claims plant cells do not need mitochondria because chloroplasts make energy. Correct the statement and explain why a root cell may lack chloroplasts.", "steps": ["[2] Photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical compounds; mitochondria participate in aerobic respiration to provide ATP for cell processes.", "[2] Plants respire in light and darkness; photosynthetic cells can contain both organelles.", "[1] Many root cells receive organic compounds from sources and do not photosynthesise, so may lack chloroplasts."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-cells-q5"}, {"title": "Microscopy choice", "prompt": "Compare light microscopy, TEM and SEM for observing living cell movement, internal ultrastructure and surface topology.", "steps": ["[2] Light microscopy can observe suitable living cells and movement but has lower resolution of fine structures.", "[2] TEM provides high-resolution views of prepared thin sections revealing internal ultrastructure.", "[2] SEM provides detailed surface information from prepared specimens; conventional electron preparation does not show living motion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-cells-q6"}, {"title": "Specialisation and evidence", "prompt": "Explain how cell specialisation and compartmentalisation support a multicellular organism. Use a protein-secreting cell and a photosynthetic leaf cell, and evaluate the limits of a teaching cell diagram. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Different cells express different genes and develop structures supporting their roles while usually retaining the same genome.", "[2] A secretory cell uses many ribosomes/RER to synthesise proteins and Golgi/vesicles for processing and export.", "[2] A leaf mesophyll cell often has chloroplasts with membranes supporting light reactions and stroma supporting carbon fixation.", "[2] Mitochondria supply ATP through respiration and both cell types depend on membranes regulating exchange.", "[2] Compartments organise distinct conditions and reactions rather than implying every organelle works alone.", "[2] Teaching diagrams simplify scale, number and movement; not every cell contains every depicted organelle, so observations and appropriate microscopy are needed."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-cells-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What primarily distinguishes typical eukaryotic from prokaryotic organisation?", "o": ["An enclosed nucleus and membrane-bound organelles", "Presence of ribosomes alone", "Presence of DNA alone", "Presence of cytoplasm alone"], "a": 0, "why": "Both cell types share DNA, ribosomes, cytoplasm and membranes, but typical eukaryotes have compartmentalised organelles."}, {"q": "Where are polypeptides assembled?", "o": ["Ribosomes", "Only cell walls", "Only vacuoles", "Phospholipid heads"], "a": 0, "why": "Ribosomes translate mRNA using amino acids; Golgi and ER have subsequent processing/transport roles."}, {"q": "Which plant-cell claim is accurate?", "o": ["Many root cells lack chloroplasts but respire", "Every plant cell has chloroplasts", "Plants never have mitochondria", "Roots make all sugar using darkness"], "a": 0, "why": "Plant-cell structures vary with specialisation, and roots often receive organic compounds from sources."}, {"q": "A 30 mm image represents a 10 μm object. What is magnification?", "o": ["3000×", "3×", "300×", "0.003×"], "a": 0, "why": "Convert 30 mm to 30,000 μm then divide by 10 μm to obtain 3,000×."}, {"q": "What does higher resolution permit?", "o": ["Distinguishing closer points separately", "Only a larger blurry image", "Changing the organism’s genome", "Making any specimen live"], "a": 0, "why": "Resolution concerns detail separation, while magnification concerns image size."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-membranes", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – From single cells to multicellular organisms", "title": "Membranes, diffusion, osmosis and active transport", "scope": "Fluid mosaic structure; passive and active transport; tonicity; bulk transport; exchange factors and surface area.", "screens": [{"title": "The fluid mosaic model", "kind": "learn", "text": "A plasma membrane consists mainly of a phospholipid bilayer with associated proteins and other components. Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic region facing the watery environment and hydrophobic tails directed into the bilayer. Many components can move laterally, producing the term fluid, while the mixture of proteins and lipids contributes to the mosaic. Proteins can provide channels, carriers, receptors and enzymes. Carbohydrate groups on outer surfaces participate in recognition, and cholesterol in animal membranes influences membrane properties. Selective permeability depends on the properties of the bilayer and its proteins. A membrane is not a solid wall or an unrestricted sieve. The model helps explain movement and communication while simplifying the dynamic arrangement and diversity of real membranes.", "diagram": "membrane"}, {"title": "Simple diffusion and concentration gradients", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diffusion is net movement arising from random particle motion down a concentration gradient. Individual particles move in both directions, but more move from the region of higher concentration to lower concentration until the net difference decreases. At equilibrium, particles continue moving even though there is no net transfer in one direction. Small non-polar molecules such as oxygen can diffuse through the phospholipid bilayer. The rate depends on the concentration gradient, surface area, distance, temperature and properties of the substance and membrane. Diffusion does not require direct metabolic energy input. Explain the direction using the concentration of the particular substance, and avoid saying particles deliberately seek empty space or stop moving when equilibrium is reached.", "diagram": "diffusion"}, {"title": "Facilitated diffusion and transport proteins", "kind": "learn", "text": "Facilitated diffusion moves substances down their concentration gradient through membrane proteins. Channels provide selective pathways, while carriers bind substances and change shape during transport. Polar molecules and ions that cross the lipid bilayer poorly can therefore move without direct ATP use when an appropriate pathway and gradient exist. Protein availability can limit the rate, so a transport graph may plateau even as concentration increases. A membrane protein does not automatically mean active transport: direction relative to the gradient and the source of energy must also be considered. Distinguish the protein's selective role from the random molecular movement underlying diffusion. Damage to a particular channel can affect one substance without making the membrane equally permeable to everything.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Osmosis across a partially permeable membrane", "kind": "learn", "text": "Osmosis is net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from higher water potential to lower water potential. At introductory level, when pressure conditions are comparable, this is often described as movement from a more dilute solution toward a solution with more effective non-penetrating solute. Water moves in both directions, with unequal rates giving a net transfer. The membrane must allow water movement while restricting relevant solutes for a sustained osmotic effect. Solute identity, membrane permeability and pressure can affect the result, so comparing total solute numbers without considering the membrane can mislead. State that water moves, name the direction relative to the two environments and explain how this changes cell volume or mass under the specified conditions.", "diagram": "osmosis"}, {"title": "Animal cells and tonicity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tonicity describes the tendency of a solution to change a cell's volume through the effects of non-penetrating solutes. In a hypotonic solution, an animal cell may gain water, swell and lyse. In a hypertonic solution, it may lose water and shrink. An isotonic solution produces no sustained net osmotic change in volume under those conditions, though molecules continue moving. Animal cells lack a rigid external wall that could oppose swelling. The terms are relative to the cell and membrane, so a solution is not inherently hypertonic without a comparison. Explain the physical consequence rather than equating dilute with harmless. The same external concentration can affect different cells differently when their internal conditions or membrane permeabilities differ.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Plant cells, turgor and plasmolysis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water entry into a plant cell enlarges the vacuole and presses the contents against the cell wall, generating turgor pressure. The wall resists further expansion, so plant cells generally do not burst in the same way as animal cells under typical hypotonic conditions. Loss of water reduces turgor, leaving cells flaccid and potentially contributing to wilting. In a sufficiently hypertonic environment, the plasma membrane and protoplast can pull away from the wall, a condition called plasmolysis. The wall itself does not shrink in the same manner as the living contents. Link water direction, vacuole change and pressure when explaining the effect. A plant can wilt through inadequate uptake or high loss even without every cell undergoing visible plasmolysis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Active transport against a gradient", "kind": "learn", "text": "Active transport uses an energy source to move substances against a concentration or electrochemical gradient. Membrane pumps can use ATP directly, while other transporters can use energy stored in an ion gradient created by earlier pumping. For the core comparison, identify the movement against the gradient, the involvement of specific transport proteins and the requirement for energy. Root cells can accumulate mineral ions even when their concentration outside is lower than inside. A decline in respiration can limit ATP-dependent transport. Do not identify active transport solely because a substance moves into a cell: substances can enter by diffusion when their gradient favours entry. State which substance, which direction and what supplies the energy in the example.", "diagram": "active"}, {"title": "Endocytosis and exocytosis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Endocytosis brings material into a cell through membrane invagination and vesicle formation. Phagocytosis engulfs relatively large particles, while other forms take in fluid or selected materials. Exocytosis releases material when intracellular vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane. These processes require cellular energy and machinery, and can transport material that cannot pass directly through the bilayer or a simple channel. A secretory cell can export a protein made through the rough ER and Golgi pathway by exocytosis. Bulk transport is distinct from moving single small solutes through a carrier, although all involve membrane function. Explain the membrane change and destination of the material, rather than simply saying active processes move anything in or out.", "diagram": "bulktransport"}, {"title": "Surface area to volume and exchange distance", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a cube with side length a, surface area is 6a² and volume is a³, so the surface-area-to-volume ratio is 6/a. As a similar-shaped cell becomes larger, its volume grows faster than its surface area. Exchange capacity may therefore become insufficient relative to the metabolic needs of the contents, particularly when diffusion distances also increase. A 1-unit cube has a ratio of 6:1, while a 3-unit cube has 54:27 or 2:1. Thin, flattened or folded shapes can provide more surface relative to volume than a compact shape of similar volume. A high ratio does not guarantee unlimited exchange because membrane properties, gradients and transport proteins also matter. State the actual dimensions and units before comparing calculations.", "diagram": "sav"}, {"title": "An osmosis investigation and its limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "An investigation using equal-sized plant tissue pieces can test the relationship between external solute concentration and percentage mass change. Record initial and final masses, standardise immersion time, temperature, tissue source and blotting method, and use independent replicates. Percentage mass change equals final minus initial mass divided by initial mass, multiplied by 100%. A zero crossing can estimate the concentration at which no net mass change occurred under those conditions. It does not directly prove the exact concentration of every solute inside every cell. Cutting damage, inconsistent drying and differences among tissue pieces can affect results. The explanation should connect mass changes to net water movement and evaluate specific procedural limitations rather than claiming any change proves active uptake of sugar.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Membrane transport", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Membrane transport</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Process</th><th scope=\"col\">Direction/route</th><th scope=\"col\">Energy and scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Simple diffusion</td><td>Down a concentration gradient through suitable routes</td><td>Passive; bidirectional motion with net movement</td></tr><tr><td>Facilitated diffusion</td><td>Down gradient via channel/carrier</td><td>Passive; transport proteins do not imply active transport</td></tr><tr><td>Osmosis</td><td>Water moves down water-potential gradient across selective membrane</td><td>Effective solutes and pressure influence water potential</td></tr><tr><td>Active transport</td><td>Can move against electrochemical gradient</td><td>Energy supplied directly or through coupled gradients</td></tr><tr><td>Endocytosis</td><td>Vesicle uptake</td><td>Coordinated energy-dependent bulk process</td></tr><tr><td>Exocytosis</td><td>Vesicle fusion releases material</td><td>Distinct from cargo crossing a channel</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Explore the numbers: surface", "kind": "interactive", "model": "surface"}, {"title": "Worked question: Osmosis in potatoes", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Osmosis in potatoes", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Surface area ratio", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Surface area ratio", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Transport distinction", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Transport distinction", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Plasmolysis", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Fluid mosaic", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Bulk transport", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Investigate water potential", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Osmosis in potatoes", "prompt": "A potato sample changes from 5.00 g to 4.25 g in a solution. Calculate percentage mass change and explain the likely direction of net water movement.", "steps": ["[2] Percentage change=(4.25−5.00)/5.00×100=−15%.", "[2] Net water left the cells through selectively permeable membranes toward the lower water potential outside.", "[1] Water movement is passive; the result is consistent with osmosis but handling, surface solution and other mass changes must be controlled."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-membranes-q1"}, {"title": "Surface area ratio", "prompt": "For cubes of side 1 and 3 arbitrary units, calculate surface area, volume and SA:V. Explain the exchange implication.", "steps": ["[2] Side 1: SA=6, V=1, SA:V=6:1.", "[2] Side 3: SA=54, V=27, SA:V=2:1.", "[2] Larger cubes have less surface area per unit volume and longer internal distances, potentially limiting exchange relative to demand."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-membranes-q2"}, {"title": "Transport distinction", "prompt": "A molecule crosses through a channel down its gradient. An ion is pumped against its electrochemical gradient using ATP. Identify and compare the processes.", "steps": ["[2] Channel-mediated movement down the gradient is facilitated diffusion and does not directly require ATP.", "[2] The ATP-driven pump is active transport against the electrochemical gradient.", "[1] Both involve membrane proteins but protein involvement alone does not establish active transport."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-membranes-q3"}, {"title": "Plasmolysis", "prompt": "A plant cell in a concentrated salt solution shows its protoplast pulling away from the wall. Explain the process and predict what may happen after transfer to pure water.", "steps": ["[2] The external solution has lower water potential, producing net water loss by osmosis.", "[2] The vacuole/protoplast shrinks and the membrane pulls from the relatively rigid wall: plasmolysis.", "[2] If the cell remains viable, water entering in pure water can restore turgor; the wall restrains excessive expansion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-membranes-q4"}, {"title": "Fluid mosaic", "prompt": "Describe phospholipid arrangement and give two different protein functions that help explain selective permeability.", "steps": ["[2] Hydrophilic heads face aqueous fluids and hydrophobic tails face inward in the bilayer.", "[2] Channels/carriers allow selected substances to cross that cannot readily cross the hydrophobic core.", "[2] Pumps can couple energy to transport, while receptors recognise signals; neither means all solutes cross freely."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-membranes-q5"}, {"title": "Bulk transport", "prompt": "Explain how exocytosis releases a large secreted molecule and why this differs from diffusion through a channel.", "steps": ["[2] A membrane-bound vesicle carries the material and fuses with the cell membrane.", "[2] Vesicle contents are released outside; membrane cycling requires cellular energy and coordinated processes.", "[1] The large cargo does not simply move through a channel down a concentration gradient."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-membranes-q6"}, {"title": "Investigate water potential", "prompt": "Design and evaluate a potato investigation using several sucrose concentrations to estimate the concentration at zero mean mass change. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Use a defined concentration series including values likely to produce both mass gain and mass loss.", "[2] Standardise potato variety/region, initial dimensions, solution volume, temperature and immersion time.", "[2] Measure initial and final mass consistently, blotting surface liquid using the same rule.", "[2] Calculate percentage change for each independent replicate and show mean/spread against concentration.", "[2] Estimate the zero-change crossing by justified interpolation rather than assuming one noisy point is exact.", "[2] Discuss balance precision, tissue variation and solution effects; zero net mass change estimates an isotonic condition under the method, not a direct measurement of every cell’s solute composition."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-membranes-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Which phospholipid arrangement is appropriate?", "o": ["Hydrophilic heads outward, tails inward", "All heads inside the core", "All tails exposed to water", "A solid wall with no proteins"], "a": 0, "why": "Amphipathic phospholipids form a bilayer with hydrophobic tails sheltered from aqueous fluids."}, {"q": "What is facilitated diffusion?", "o": ["Protein-mediated movement down a gradient", "Always ATP-driven uphill pumping", "Vesicle fusion only", "Movement without any membrane"], "a": 0, "why": "Channels/carriers assist passive movement; protein involvement alone does not make transport active."}, {"q": "What causes plasmolysis in a suitable plant cell?", "o": ["Net osmotic water loss", "Wall becoming a nucleus", "ATP creation by salt", "Chromosomes leaving the cell"], "a": 0, "why": "Water loss shrinks the protoplast away from the relatively rigid wall under an appropriate external water potential."}, {"q": "For a cube of side 2, what is SA:V?", "o": ["3:1", "2:1", "6:1", "1:6"], "a": 0, "why": "SA=6×4=24 and V=8, giving ratio 3:1; SA:V declines with increasing side length."}, {"q": "Which process releases large secreted cargo through vesicle fusion?", "o": ["Exocytosis", "Simple diffusion", "DNA translation", "Denitrification"], "a": 0, "why": "Exocytosis merges a vesicle membrane with the cell membrane and releases its contents outside."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "23 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-enzymes", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – From single cells to multicellular organisms", "title": "Biological molecules, metabolism and enzyme action", "scope": "Carbohydrates, proteins and lipids; monomers; anabolism and catabolism; enzyme models; rate factors and investigations.", "screens": [{"title": "Matter, energy and metabolism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Metabolism is the set of processes through which cells transform matter and energy to maintain life. Anabolic pathways build larger or more complex molecules and generally require energy input. Catabolic pathways break down molecules and can release energy that is captured for other cellular work. Energy and matter are conserved overall, but cells continually exchange both with their surroundings. ATP provides an immediate means of transferring usable chemical energy between reactions, movement and transport processes. The cell's internal organisation helps coordinate these pathways by positioning enzymes and separating incompatible conditions. A metabolic process should be explained through its inputs, products and purpose, not described as a substance disappearing because the cell needed energy.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Carbohydrates and condensation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carbohydrates include monosaccharides such as glucose, disaccharides and larger polysaccharides. Monomers can join through condensation reactions that form bonds with water released, while hydrolysis breaks suitable bonds through addition of water. Starch and glycogen provide storage forms in many organisms, whereas cellulose has a structural role in plant cell walls. Similar glucose building blocks can produce molecules with different bonding arrangements and properties. A monomer is a building unit, not a synonym for every small molecule. Relate molecular structure to function: an insoluble storage polymer can store many glucose units without producing the same osmotic effect as an equivalent number of free glucose molecules. Digestive and cellular enzymes are needed to catalyse the relevant synthesis or breakdown reactions.", "diagram": "molecules"}, {"title": "Proteins, amino acids and shape", "kind": "learn", "text": "Proteins contain amino acids joined by peptide bonds. The amino acid sequence influences folding and interactions, producing a three-dimensional structure suited to a particular function. Proteins can act as enzymes, structural components, transporters, receptors and signals. Their function depends on more than simply having the correct number of amino acids: order and folding matter. Changes in sequence or conditions can alter interactions and therefore activity. Denaturation disrupts higher-order structure and can reduce function, while not necessarily breaking the peptide bonds of the primary sequence. Avoid assuming every denaturation is reversible. Connect the change in structure to the affected process, such as a transporter losing its binding shape or an enzyme no longer positioning a substrate effectively.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Lipids and membrane structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Lipids are a diverse group of largely hydrophobic biological substances rather than one uniform class of true repeating polymers. Triglycerides form from glycerol and three fatty acids and provide concentrated energy storage. Phospholipids have polar and non-polar regions that support bilayer formation in water. Saturated fatty acids lack carbon–carbon double bonds, while unsaturated forms contain them, influencing packing and physical properties. The syllabus's building-unit idea can be applied to lipid synthesis, but triglycerides should not be described as long repeating chains of one identical monomer. Compare function and structure: storage lipids and membrane lipids have different arrangements suited to their roles. Hydrolysis of fats and subsequent metabolic processing can provide energy, but the amount depends on the pathway and organism.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Enzymes as biological catalysts", "kind": "learn", "text": "Enzymes are biological catalysts, commonly proteins, that increase reaction rate by providing a pathway with lower activation energy. They do not supply the reaction's overall energy, change its equilibrium position or become permanently consumed in each reaction. A substrate binds at an active site whose properties support the reaction, and products are released so the enzyme can act again. Specificity depends on shape and chemical interactions. Some enzymes need additional non-protein components for activity. Explain how catalysis affects the number of successful reactions per time under the conditions, rather than saying the enzyme gives molecules intelligence or simply makes them move faster. The rate observed also depends on substrate, enzyme concentration and other limiting conditions.", "diagram": "enzyme"}, {"title": "Lock-and-key and induced-fit models", "kind": "learn", "text": "The lock-and-key model emphasises a complementary fit between substrate and active site. It is useful for introducing specificity but can imply that the site is rigid. The induced-fit model represents a change in enzyme conformation when the substrate binds, helping position groups for catalysis and stabilise relevant interactions. Both are simplified models of molecular processes. A comparison should identify the common substrate–site relationship and the difference in flexibility. Use the model to explain why a changed active site may bind less effectively or why a suitable substrate is preferentially acted upon. Do not claim every binding event guarantees a reaction or that a model diagram shows molecules at their real sizes, positions and timescales.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Temperature and enzyme activity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Increasing temperature can initially raise reaction rate because particles collide more frequently with sufficient energy. Above a suitable range, changes to enzyme structure can reduce the proportion of functional active sites and lower activity. The balance produces an apparent optimum under the stated assay conditions. Low temperature usually slows reactions without necessarily denaturing the enzyme, while prolonged or extreme heat can cause lasting loss of function. Different enzymes and organisms have different temperature responses, and assay duration can affect the observed optimum. Explain both sides of the curve with distinct mechanisms. A lower product amount at high temperature could also involve changed substrate or assay conditions, so a valid investigation controls relevant variables and measures initial rates where appropriate.", "diagram": "enzymerate"}, {"title": "pH and inhibitory effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "pH affects charges and interactions in an enzyme and its substrate, which can alter binding and catalytic activity. An enzyme's optimum pH depends on its properties and operating environment. Extreme pH can also disrupt structure. Competitive inhibitors can occupy the active site and reduce access by substrate; in a simple reversible model, additional substrate can reduce their relative effect. Other inhibitors act through different sites or mechanisms and may not be overcome in that way. Not all inhibition is reversible, and non-competitive terminology is a simplified model for particular kinetics. Explain the actual interaction stated in a question rather than assuming every inhibitor permanently denatures the enzyme. Cells can regulate metabolic pathways through changes in enzyme activity as well as changes in enzyme amount.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Substrate, enzyme and product concentrations", "kind": "learn", "text": "When enzyme concentration is fixed, raising substrate concentration initially increases the chance of productive binding. At sufficiently high substrate concentration, most available active sites are occupied and the rate approaches a maximum under those conditions. Adding more enzyme can increase that maximum if substrate and other requirements are available. As a reaction proceeds, substrate depletion or product accumulation may reduce the observed rate, so a final product total is not necessarily equivalent to the initial rate. Some products can inhibit pathways or affect the reverse reaction. State what remains fixed when interpreting a graph. A plateau can indicate saturation, but evidence and controls are needed to distinguish this from limitations caused by another reagent or the measurement method.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Designing and evaluating an enzyme investigation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fictional experiment measures product formed per minute at several pH values while keeping enzyme and substrate concentrations, temperature, reaction volume and measurement timing constant. Use appropriate buffer solutions, blanks and independent replicate reactions. Specify how the dependent variable will be measured and express a rate in suitable units. Repeating a reading from the same reaction is not equivalent to several independent reactions. A colour change can indicate product formation only if the assay has been validated and other factors do not alter the colour. Draw conclusions supported by the tested range, such as highest measured activity at pH 7, rather than claiming an exact universal optimum between untested values. Suggest improvements that address identified sources of variation or bias.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Molecules and catalysis", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Molecules and catalysis</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Item</th><th scope=\"col\">Structure/process</th><th scope=\"col\">Exam distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Starch/glycogen/cellulose</td><td>Glucose-based polysaccharides</td><td>Different links/organisation give different functions</td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>Amino acids joined by peptide bonds</td><td>Sequence and folding affect function</td></tr><tr><td>Triglyceride</td><td>Glycerol plus three fatty acids</td><td>Lipids are not all repeating-monomer polymers</td></tr><tr><td>Phospholipid</td><td>Amphipathic head/tail molecule</td><td>Supports bilayer organisation</td></tr><tr><td>Enzyme</td><td>Lowers activation barrier</td><td>Does not alter overall energy change/equilibrium</td></tr><tr><td>Denaturation</td><td>Disrupts functional conformation</td><td>Does not necessarily break every peptide backbone bond</td></tr><tr><td>Competitive inhibitor</td><td>Competes for active site</td><td>Not the model for every inhibitor</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Rate curve", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Rate curve", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Cold versus hot", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Cold versus hot", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Molecules and hydrolysis", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Molecules and hydrolysis", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Induced fit", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Inhibition", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Energy barrier", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: pH experiment", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Rate curve", "prompt": "An enzyme’s initial rate rises with substrate concentration then reaches a plateau at constant enzyme concentration. Explain the curve and predict the effect of adding more enzyme if substrate is plentiful.", "steps": ["[2] At low substrate concentration, more substrate increases successful enzyme–substrate encounters.", "[2] At high concentration, available active sites are occupied much of the time, so enzyme availability limits the rate.", "[2] More active enzyme increases available sites and can raise the maximum rate if other conditions and substrate remain sufficient."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q1"}, {"title": "Cold versus hot", "prompt": "An enzyme reaction is slow at 5°C and also at 80°C. Explain why returning both samples to 30°C may restore only the cold sample’s activity.", "steps": ["[2] Cold slows molecular movement and encounters but usually does not denature the enzyme.", "[2] High temperature can disrupt interactions maintaining the enzyme’s conformation and active site.", "[2] Warming the cold sample can restore rate; cooling a denatured sample does not necessarily restore functional folding."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q2"}, {"title": "Molecules and hydrolysis", "prompt": "Compare starch and protein in terms of monomers, links and digestion by hydrolysis.", "steps": ["[2] Starch consists of glucose units joined by glycosidic bonds.", "[2] Proteins consist of amino acids joined by peptide bonds.", "[2] Hydrolysis uses water to break the relevant covalent links; different enzymes recognise the different substrates."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q3"}, {"title": "Induced fit", "prompt": "Explain how the induced-fit model differs from a rigid lock-and-key model and how it helps explain specificity.", "steps": ["[2] Both connect a suitable substrate with an enzyme active site.", "[2] Induced fit includes conformational changes during binding, rather than a completely rigid pre-existing fit.", "[2] Complementary interactions help position the substrate and support catalysis; a different substrate may not establish suitable interactions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q4"}, {"title": "Inhibition", "prompt": "A molecule occupies an enzyme’s active site without producing the normal product. Explain competitive inhibition and why increasing substrate can reduce its effect in the simple model.", "steps": ["[2] Substrate and inhibitor compete for the active site, reducing productive complexes.", "[2] Higher substrate concentration increases the chance that substrate occupies the site instead of inhibitor.", "[1] This applies to a simplified reversible competitive model, not all forms of inhibition."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q5"}, {"title": "Energy barrier", "prompt": "Explain why an enzyme speeds a reaction without changing its overall energy change or being used up as a reactant.", "steps": ["[2] An enzyme provides a reaction pathway with a lower activation barrier.", "[2] It does not change the energy difference between initial reactants and final products or the equilibrium position.", "[1] It is regenerated through catalytic cycles rather than permanently consumed in the reaction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q6"}, {"title": "pH experiment", "prompt": "Design an investigation of pH and amylase initial rate. Include variables, measurement, reliability and validity. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Use a justified buffered pH series as the independent variable and define initial rate of starch breakdown as the dependent measure.", "[2] Keep enzyme and substrate concentration/volume, temperature and timing procedures consistent.", "[2] Use an appropriate quantitative assay or consistently sampled endpoint with blanks/controls for assay effects.", "[2] Conduct independent replicates at each pH and compare means and variation.", "[2] Calibrate instruments and check buffer/substrate conditions; enzyme-free controls reveal non-enzymatic change.", "[2] Explain that pH can alter active-site charges and folding, while reporting uncertainty and distinguishing measurement repeatability from whether the method isolates the pH effect."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-enzymes-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What does an enzyme lower?", "o": ["Activation energy barrier", "Every product’s mass", "The equilibrium energy difference", "The number of conserved atoms"], "a": 0, "why": "Catalysis changes the reaction pathway/barrier rather than the overall energy difference or stoichiometry."}, {"q": "What best describes induced fit?", "o": ["Binding can change enzyme conformation", "The enzyme is always rigid", "Any molecule is a substrate", "The enzyme is consumed entirely"], "a": 0, "why": "Induced fit represents flexible enzyme–substrate interaction and positioning supporting catalysis."}, {"q": "Why does substrate-dependent rate plateau?", "o": ["Available enzyme becomes limiting", "All products turn into enzymes", "No encounters occur", "Temperature must be zero"], "a": 0, "why": "At high substrate, active sites are occupied much of the time, limiting further rate increase at fixed enzyme concentration."}, {"q": "What links amino acids in a protein backbone?", "o": ["Peptide bonds", "Only hydrogen bonds between DNA bases", "Glycosidic bonds in starch", "Cellulose walls"], "a": 0, "why": "Peptide bonds join amino acids, while folding involves additional interactions that may be altered by denaturation."}, {"q": "How does cooling usually differ from severe heating?", "o": ["It slows encounters without necessarily denaturing", "It always destroys every peptide bond", "It increases rate without limit", "It converts protein into DNA"], "a": 0, "why": "Low temperature commonly reduces kinetic activity reversibly; sufficiently high heat can disrupt functional conformation."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-energetics", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – From single cells to multicellular organisms", "title": "Photosynthesis, cellular respiration and ATP", "scope": "Chloroplast reactions, limiting factors, aerobic stages, fermentation, balanced equations and practical evidence.", "screens": [{"title": "ATP and coupled cellular work", "kind": "learn", "text": "ATP transfers usable chemical energy to cellular processes. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and inorganic phosphate can be coupled to reactions or movements requiring energy. Energy released through metabolism is used to regenerate ATP, allowing continual turnover rather than long-term stockpiling. The overall free-energy change of hydrolysis matters; merely breaking a chemical bond does not itself release energy, because bond breaking requires input and the complete reaction includes formation and stabilisation of products. At this course level, explain ATP as an immediate energy-transfer molecule connecting energy-releasing and energy-requiring processes. It supports active transport, biosynthesis and movement. A cell needs ongoing metabolic activity because ATP is continually used and its available pool is limited.", "diagram": "atp"}, {"title": "Overall photosynthesis and conservation of matter", "kind": "learn", "text": "A simplified balanced equation for oxygenic photosynthesis is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, with light energy captured by photosynthetic pigments. It summarises the net process, not one reaction occurring in a single step. Carbon atoms in carbohydrate come from carbon dioxide, while oxygen released in oxygenic photosynthesis originates from water splitting. The plant also needs mineral nutrients and suitable conditions for its enzymes. Photosynthesis transforms light energy into chemical energy and builds organic matter. Light is an energy input, not a material atom incorporated into glucose. Explain the roles of the two reaction sets and the chloroplast compartments when a question asks for process detail rather than relying only on the balanced equation.", "diagram": "photosynthesis"}, {"title": "Light-dependent reactions in thylakoids", "kind": "learn", "text": "Photosynthetic pigments associated with thylakoid membranes absorb light. Excited electrons enter an electron transport pathway, and splitting water supplies replacement electrons while releasing oxygen and protons. Electron transport supports a proton gradient across the membrane, which drives ATP formation through ATP synthase. Electrons are also used to reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH. ATP and NADPH provide energy and reducing power for carbon fixation reactions. This account describes the main functions without requiring every intermediate carrier to be memorised. Keep location and products clear: oxygen production occurs through water splitting, and the thylakoid membrane helps organise processes and maintain a gradient. The reactions do not directly create a finished glucose molecule at the instant light strikes chlorophyll.", "diagram": "chloroplast"}, {"title": "Light-independent reactions in the stroma", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carbon fixation reactions, commonly represented by the Calvin cycle, occur in the chloroplast stroma. They use carbon dioxide together with ATP and NADPH produced by the light-dependent reactions to generate small organic molecules that can contribute to carbohydrate synthesis. The carbon acceptor is regenerated through the cycle. The term light-independent means that the reactions do not require a photon directly at each reaction step, not that they operate best only in darkness or are independent of the light reactions' products. Their activity depends on enzymes, substrate availability and cellular conditions. Describe the connection between reaction sets: if light-dependent production of ATP and NADPH falls, sustained carbon fixation is restricted even when carbon dioxide remains available.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Limiting factors and photosynthesis graphs", "kind": "learn", "text": "At low light intensity, extra light can increase photosynthetic rate. At higher light intensity, another factor such as carbon dioxide availability or enzyme capacity can limit the response, producing a plateau. Raising carbon dioxide may increase the plateau under suitable conditions. Temperature affects enzyme activity, but excessively high temperature can impair processes and influence stomatal closure and water loss. The factor with the lowest effective availability relative to demand can limit rate. Changing one factor may reveal a different limitation, so no single curve applies universally. Explain the whole pattern with mechanism and conditions. If a graph measures net oxygen output, remember that respiratory oxygen consumption contributes to the measured result rather than equating it automatically with gross photosynthesis.", "diagram": "limiting"}, {"title": "Aerobic respiration and its equation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Aerobic cellular respiration transfers energy from organic compounds into ATP, with oxygen serving as the final electron acceptor in the main respiratory electron transport chain. A simplified balanced equation for glucose is C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O, with energy transferred to ATP and released as heat. The process consists of linked enzyme-controlled stages rather than a single combustion reaction. Respiration occurs in plants and animals, and photosynthetic cells still need ATP for cellular work. Breathing supplies and removes gases at organism level, while cellular respiration is a set of chemical reactions in cells. Keep these meanings separate when discussing an organism's ventilation or oxygen uptake.", "diagram": "respiration"}, {"title": "Glycolysis, mitochondrial stages and ATP synthesis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol and splits glucose into pyruvate, generating a small net ATP yield and reduced electron carriers. In aerobic eukaryotic respiration, pyruvate is processed in mitochondria and further oxidised through a cycle of reactions in the matrix, releasing carbon dioxide and transferring electrons to carriers. Electron transport on the inner mitochondrial membrane uses these electrons and oxygen, helping create a proton gradient for ATP synthase. Most ATP from aerobic glucose breakdown is associated with this final system. Exact ATP totals vary with conditions and assumptions, so do not treat one historical textbook total as universal unless an exam specifies it. Link each location to its function rather than assigning every respiratory step to mitochondria.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Anaerobic pathways and fermentation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Without sufficient oxygen, glycolysis can continue temporarily if a pathway regenerates the electron carrier needed for it. In animal muscle, lactate fermentation converts pyruvate to lactate and supports continued glycolysis, with a net two ATP per glucose from glycolysis itself. Yeast and many plant cells under appropriate conditions can produce ethanol and carbon dioxide through fermentation. Bacteria have varied anaerobic metabolisms, including fermentation and true anaerobic respiration using other electron acceptors. School materials sometimes use anaerobic respiration broadly for fermentation; distinguish the pathway and products in the supplied context. Fermentation yields much less ATP per glucose than aerobic respiration. Avoid claiming that every anaerobic organism produces lactate or that carbon dioxide is a product of lactate fermentation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Factors controlling respiration rate", "kind": "learn", "text": "Respiration rate depends on substrate availability, suitable temperature, enzyme activity and, for aerobic pathways, oxygen supply. Increasing temperature can initially raise metabolic rate, but excessive temperatures can disrupt proteins and membranes. Oxygen limitation can restrict the electron transport chain and alter the balance of pathways used. Different tissues and organisms have different energy demands, so rates should be standardised to mass or another appropriate measure for fair comparison. A higher rate of carbon dioxide release may indicate increased respiration, but the interpretation depends on other processes and the organism. Fermentation in yeast can produce carbon dioxide without aerobic respiration. Explain why the chosen measurement represents the process under the experimental conditions rather than treating any gas production as uniquely diagnostic.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Investigating energy transformations safely", "kind": "learn", "text": "Photosynthesis can be investigated through oxygen changes, carbon dioxide uptake or other suitable indicators, with controls for temperature, light, biomass and timing. Bubble counts are a rough proxy because bubble size can vary and some gas dissolves. Respiration can be investigated using appropriate sensors or respirometers, with suitable controls and consideration of gas absorption, leaks and temperature. Use safe non-pathogenic materials and school-approved methods. The 2027 wording focuses on food, beverages and biofuels as contexts for control of photosynthesis and respiration; older rubbish-breakdown wording should be recognised as a historical syllabus context. Evaluate technologies by explaining what they measure, their limitations and how they improve understanding, rather than claiming a sensor directly measures every cellular reaction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cellular energy pathways", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Cellular energy pathways</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Stage</th><th scope=\"col\">Location in the usual model</th><th scope=\"col\">Main connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Light-dependent reactions</td><td>Chloroplast thylakoid membranes</td><td>Light → ATP/NADPH; water supplies released O₂</td></tr><tr><td>Calvin cycle</td><td>Chloroplast stroma</td><td>CO₂ fixed using ATP and NADPH</td></tr><tr><td>Glycolysis</td><td>Cytosol</td><td>Glucose → pyruvate, small net ATP and reduced carrier yield</td></tr><tr><td>Pyruvate processing/citric acid cycle</td><td>Mitochondrial matrix</td><td>Carbon oxidation, CO₂ and reduced carriers</td></tr><tr><td>Electron transport/ATP synthesis</td><td>Mitochondrial inner membrane</td><td>Electron/proton gradients; O₂ final acceptor</td></tr><tr><td>Fermentation</td><td>Cytosol in standard examples</td><td>Carrier regeneration permits glycolysis; lactate or ethanol/CO₂ pathway</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Balanced reactions", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Balanced reactions", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Respiration locations", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Respiration locations", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Limiting light", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Limiting light", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Light-independent reactions", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Fermentation", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: ATP statement", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Photosynthesis investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Balanced reactions", "prompt": "Write simplified balanced equations for photosynthesis and aerobic respiration and explain why plants perform both.", "steps": ["[2] Photosynthesis: 6CO₂+6H₂O+light → C₆H₁₂O₆+6O₂.", "[2] Aerobic respiration: C₆H₁₂O₆+6O₂ → 6CO₂+6H₂O, with energy transferred to ATP and dissipated as heat.", "[2] Photosynthesis produces organic compounds; respiration supplies ATP for plant cell activities in light and darkness."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-energetics-q1"}, {"title": "Respiration locations", "prompt": "Locate glycolysis, the matrix stages and the electron transport chain in a typical aerobic eukaryotic cell. State oxygen’s direct role.", "steps": ["[2] Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol, producing pyruvate with a small net ATP yield.", "[2] Pyruvate processing/citric acid cycle occur in the mitochondrial matrix; electron transport occurs at the inner membrane.", "[2] Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in aerobic electron transport, forming water and permitting continued electron flow."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-energetics-q2"}, {"title": "Limiting light", "prompt": "At fixed temperature and CO₂, net photosynthesis rises with light then plateaus. Explain the two regions and why zero net gas exchange does not mean both processes stopped.", "steps": ["[2] At low light, light supply limits photosynthetic reactions, so more light can raise rate.", "[2] At a plateau, another factor such as CO₂ supply limits further increase under those conditions.", "[2] At zero net exchange, photosynthetic uptake and respiratory release can balance; both processes may still occur."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-energetics-q3"}, {"title": "Light-independent reactions", "prompt": "Correct the claim that the Calvin cycle occurs only at night. Include location, carbon source and energy inputs.", "steps": ["[2] The Calvin cycle occurs in the chloroplast stroma and fixes carbon from CO₂.", "[2] It uses ATP and reduced electron carrier NADPH supplied by light-dependent reactions.", "[1] Light-independent means no direct photon requirement at those reaction steps, not that the process is restricted to night."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energetics-q4"}, {"title": "Fermentation", "prompt": "Compare animal lactate fermentation and yeast alcoholic fermentation, including ATP source and the role of regenerated carriers.", "steps": ["[2] Both allow glycolysis to continue by regenerating NAD⁺ without the aerobic electron transport pathway.", "[2] Lactate fermentation produces lactate; yeast alcoholic fermentation produces ethanol and CO₂.", "[2] Their net ATP in the standard scheme comes from glycolysis; neither provides the full aerobic yield."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energetics-q5"}, {"title": "ATP statement", "prompt": "Evaluate the statement “breaking ATP’s terminal bond releases energy”. Give a chemically accurate explanation of coupling.", "steps": ["[2] Breaking a bond itself requires energy; overall ATP hydrolysis is favourable because product formation and interactions outweigh those costs.", "[2] The overall hydrolysis reaction can be coupled to an otherwise unfavourable cellular process.", "[1] ATP is a short-term energy-transfer molecule continuously regenerated, not the organism’s largest long-term energy store."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energetics-q6"}, {"title": "Photosynthesis investigation", "prompt": "Design an investigation of light intensity and aquatic plant net oxygen production, and evaluate bubble counting. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Manipulate measured light intensity across a justified range and define oxygen production per time as the response.", "[2] Keep plant species/size, temperature, CO₂ availability, water volume and acclimation/time interval consistent.", "[2] Use independent replicates and a calibrated oxygen method with appropriate blank/background measurement.", "[2] Plot mean rate and variation against measured intensity; identify increasing and plateau regions without forcing linearity.", "[2] Bubble number is an imperfect proxy because bubbles differ in size and gas can dissolve; sensor readings also require calibration.", "[2] The result is net oxygen exchange including respiration; explain limiting factors and avoid inferring gross photosynthesis without additional measurements."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-energetics-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What is oxygen’s direct role in aerobic electron transport?", "o": ["Final electron acceptor", "Initial source of every carbon atom", "Enzyme substrate in transcription", "Sole product of glycolysis"], "a": 0, "why": "Oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the aerobic chain and contributes to water formation."}, {"q": "Where does glycolysis occur in a typical eukaryotic cell?", "o": ["Cytosol", "Chloroplast thylakoid only", "Nuclear pore only", "Cell wall only"], "a": 0, "why": "Glycolysis is cytosolic, while later aerobic stages involve mitochondrial matrix and inner membrane."}, {"q": "Where does carbon fixation occur in a chloroplast?", "o": ["Stroma", "Outer cell wall", "Animal lysosome", "Golgi lumen"], "a": 0, "why": "The Calvin cycle uses CO₂ and products of light reactions in the chloroplast stroma."}, {"q": "What does fermentation permit?", "o": ["NAD⁺ regeneration supporting glycolysis", "Unlimited aerobic ATP without oxygen", "All glucose becoming DNA", "Oxygen generation in mammals"], "a": 0, "why": "Regenerating carriers permits continued glycolysis; fermentation does not deliver the full aerobic ATP yield."}, {"q": "What does zero net CO₂ exchange in light necessarily establish?", "o": ["Uptake and release are balanced at that measurement", "All respiration stopped", "All photosynthesis stopped", "No cells are alive"], "a": 0, "why": "Net exchange can be zero when opposing processes balance; it does not independently establish that both rates are zero."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-animals", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – From single cells to multicellular organisms", "title": "Animal exchange, digestion, transport and excretion", "scope": "Multicellular organisation; gills, skin, spiracles and alveoli; digestive specialisation; circulation; nitrogenous waste.", "screens": [{"title": "Cells, tissues, organs and systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "In multicellular organisms, specialised cells form tissues that carry out related functions. Several tissues combine in an organ, and organs interact in systems. A digestive organ can contain epithelial, muscle, connective and nervous tissues, showing why an organ is not simply one large tissue. Specialisation improves performance but creates dependence on exchange and transport processes elsewhere in the organism. Most internal cells are too far from the external environment for direct diffusion alone to meet their needs. Organ systems provide routes for nutrients, gases and waste while maintaining suitable internal conditions. A structure–function explanation should identify the relevant level and trace how its activity supports the cells, rather than listing systems without connecting their roles.", "diagram": "organisation"}, {"title": "Effective gas-exchange surfaces", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gas exchange requires suitable gradients and a surface through which oxygen and carbon dioxide can diffuse. Effective surfaces generally provide large area, short diffusion distance and a moist interface, with ventilation or internal transport maintaining gradients. The arrangement must suit the organism's environment and metabolic demand. A large dry impermeable surface is not automatically effective because area alone cannot overcome other limitations. Some small or thin organisms rely strongly on direct surface exchange, while larger or more active organisms need specialised structures. Explain each feature's effect: thin barriers shorten diffusion paths, large area permits more simultaneous transfer, and movement of air, water or blood helps prevent local equilibration. Distinguish gas exchange from cellular respiration.", "diagram": "exchange"}, {"title": "Skin and insect spiracles", "kind": "learn", "text": "Some animals exchange gases across a moist body surface, which requires suitable permeability and can impose constraints on body size, thickness and water balance. Insects use spiracles opening into a branching tracheal system that delivers air near tissues. Gas movement occurs through diffusion and can be assisted by body movements in larger or active insects. In many insects, oxygen transport does not mainly depend on circulatory haemolymph, so the mammalian blood pathway should not be imposed on them. Spiracle opening can affect water loss, creating a balance between gas exchange and water conservation. Describe the pathway from the external air to tissues and identify where the exchange occurs, rather than stating simply that all animals breathe through lungs.", "diagram": "tracheae"}, {"title": "Fish gills and countercurrent exchange", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gills provide a large area through filaments and lamellae, with a thin barrier between water and blood. Water movement over the surface and blood flow maintain exchange. In a countercurrent arrangement, water and blood move in opposite directions, so an oxygen gradient can persist along much of the exchange surface. This permits more complete extraction than an equivalent co-current arrangement under the simplified comparison. The blood does not necessarily encounter water with identical oxygen concentration at every point. Label flow directions and the relative concentrations when explaining the mechanism. Gills are suited to aquatic conditions, and their fine structures can collapse or lose effective moist support outside water. A diagram is a model of exchange, not an exact scale drawing of every vessel.", "diagram": "gills"}, {"title": "Alveoli and pulmonary gas exchange", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mammalian alveoli create a large internal gas-exchange area. Thin alveolar and capillary barriers shorten diffusion distance, while ventilation replenishes air and blood flow transports gases. Oxygen diffuses from alveolar air into blood under the relevant partial-pressure gradient, while carbon dioxide moves in the reverse direction. A moist surface supports gas dissolution before diffusion. Haemoglobin helps carry oxygen, contributing to effective transport between lungs and tissues. The direction of diffusion depends on local gradients, not on oxygen always moving into every cell regardless of conditions. Damage that thickens the barrier or reduces area can impair exchange. Explain the feature, gradient and consequence rather than describing alveoli only as small balloons or saying the lungs actively pump individual oxygen molecules into blood.", "diagram": "alveoli"}, {"title": "Digestion, absorption and dietary requirements", "kind": "learn", "text": "Animals obtain nutrients through ingestion and processing of organic material. Mechanical breakdown increases accessible surface area, while chemical digestion uses enzymes to hydrolyse larger molecules into absorbable products. Absorption transfers those products across an exchange surface into internal transport, and assimilation uses them in cells. A gastrovascular cavity has a single main opening and combines digestive and distribution functions, whereas an alimentary canal has separate entry and exit with specialised regions. Egestion removes undigested material and differs from excretion of metabolic waste. Follow the sequence and keep the terms distinct. A meal's energy content is not all converted to useful cellular work because digestion, absorption and metabolism each have limitations and require energy themselves.", "diagram": "digestion"}, {"title": "Herbivore and carnivore alimentary specialisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Digestive systems reflect diet and evolutionary history. Many herbivores have structures supporting prolonged processing and microbial fermentation of cellulose, such as enlarged foregut or hindgut regions. Animals generally rely on microbial enzymes for extensive cellulose breakdown rather than producing all the required enzymes themselves. Carnivorous diets often contain more readily digestible proteins and fats, but digestive anatomy varies among groups. Compare named structures and functions rather than assuming every herbivore has multiple stomachs or that gut length alone defines diet. Symbiotic microorganisms can provide useful products, while the host supplies a habitat and resources. Explain how retaining food and supporting microorganisms improves extraction from plant material, and acknowledge that the effective arrangement depends on the particular organism.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Open and closed circulatory systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "In an open circulatory system, circulatory fluid leaves vessels and bathes tissues in body spaces before returning. In a closed system, blood remains within vessels while substances exchange across vessel walls and surrounding fluid. Closed circulation can support controlled distribution and higher pressures suited to many active organisms. An open system can adequately serve organisms with different metabolic requirements, especially when gas delivery uses another system such as insect tracheae. Neither arrangement should be described as universally superior in every environment. Distinguish arteries, veins and capillaries by flow and function rather than by oxygen content alone. The transport system maintains exchange gradients and links specialised organs to internal cells, with its design reflecting size, activity and habitat.", "diagram": "circulation"}, {"title": "A mammalian transport pathway and exchange", "kind": "learn", "text": "A simplified mammalian pathway runs from the right side of the heart to pulmonary vessels and lungs, then to the left side and systemic circulation before returning. This separates pulmonary and systemic circuits, enabling efficient movement of blood with different oxygen contents. Capillary networks provide extensive thin-walled exchange surfaces, and their arrangement permits slower local flow than large vessels. Oxygen and nutrient delivery, carbon dioxide removal and signalling all depend on transport and exchange. Pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood away from the heart, showing why artery cannot mean oxygenated vessel. Use arrows for flow direction and distinguish the movement of blood from diffusion of substances across the capillary boundary. The whole system ultimately supports cellular metabolism.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Excretion and nitrogenous waste", "kind": "learn", "text": "Excretion removes metabolic wastes such as carbon dioxide, excess salts and nitrogen-containing products of amino acid breakdown. Ammonia is highly toxic and generally requires substantial water for rapid removal or dilution. Urea is less toxic and can be stored and excreted with less water, while uric acid can conserve more water but requires greater energy to produce and handle. Many aquatic animals release nitrogenous waste across surfaces or specialised structures, while terrestrial organisms often need mechanisms that balance waste removal with water conservation. The main waste form varies among groups and conditions, so avoid assigning one form to every animal in a habitat. Egestion of undigested food and excretion of nitrogenous metabolic products are distinct processes.", "diagram": "nitrogenwaste"}, {"title": "Exchange, circulation and waste", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Exchange, circulation and waste</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">System</th><th scope=\"col\">Structure–function link</th><th scope=\"col\">Qualification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gills</td><td>Large thin lamellae; water/blood gradient</td><td>Countercurrent improves sustained exchange in the model</td></tr><tr><td>Alveoli</td><td>Large moist thin surface, ventilation and perfusion</td><td>Blood then transports gases</td></tr><tr><td>Tracheae</td><td>Air tubes from spiracles toward tissues</td><td>Standard insect oxygen route differs from blood transport</td></tr><tr><td>Artery/vein</td><td>Away from/toward heart</td><td>Not defined by oxygen content</td></tr><tr><td>Ammonia</td><td>Toxic, water-demanding, relatively cheap synthesis</td><td>Often suited to abundant water</td></tr><tr><td>Urea/uric acid</td><td>Lower water requirement with synthesis cost</td><td>Uric acid typically needs least water but costs more energy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Countercurrent gills", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Countercurrent gills", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Double circulation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Double circulation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Waste choices", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Waste choices", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Digestion vocabulary", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Insect gas exchange", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Herbivore comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Exchange-system essay", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Countercurrent gills", "prompt": "Explain how a fish gill’s structure and countercurrent flow support oxygen uptake.", "steps": ["[2] Filaments and lamellae provide large surface area with a thin water–blood barrier.", "[2] Water and blood flow in opposite directions, maintaining an oxygen gradient along much of the exchange surface.", "[2] Oxygen diffuses into blood and is transported away; ventilation renews oxygenated water."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-animals-q1"}, {"title": "Double circulation", "prompt": "Trace blood from the right atrium back to it through pulmonary and systemic circulation. Explain why an artery is not defined by oxygen content.", "steps": ["[2] Right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary vein → left atrium.", "[2] Left ventricle → systemic arteries → body capillaries → systemic veins → right atrium.", "[2] Arteries carry blood away from the heart; pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, so oxygen content is not their defining feature."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-animals-q2"}, {"title": "Waste choices", "prompt": "Compare ammonia, urea and uric acid in toxicity, water requirement and energy cost, relating them to habitats.", "steps": ["[2] Ammonia is highly toxic but relatively low-cost to produce; elimination typically requires plentiful water.", "[2] Urea is less toxic and needs less water than ammonia but costs energy to synthesise.", "[2] Uric acid can be eliminated with little water and is relatively expensive energetically; habitat and physiology influence the trade-off."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-animals-q3"}, {"title": "Digestion vocabulary", "prompt": "Distinguish digestion, absorption, assimilation, egestion and excretion using one food example.", "steps": ["[1] Digestion breaks food into absorbable components.", "[1] Absorption transfers digested components across the digestive surface into the organism.", "[1] Assimilation incorporates or uses absorbed material in cells.", "[1] Egestion removes undigested gut contents.", "[1] Excretion removes metabolic wastes, such as nitrogenous waste, rather than simply all material leaving the body."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-animals-q4"}, {"title": "Insect gas exchange", "prompt": "Explain how spiracles and tracheae supply insect tissues with oxygen and why closed circulation is not necessary for that route.", "steps": ["[2] Spiracles connect the exterior to branching air-filled tracheae/tracheoles.", "[2] Air movement and diffusion maintain oxygen transfer toward tissues across short terminal distances.", "[1] In the standard insect model oxygen is delivered largely through this airway system rather than transported primarily by blood."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-animals-q5"}, {"title": "Herbivore comparison", "prompt": "Explain how gut specialisations and microorganisms support digestion of plant material without assuming every herbivore has four stomach compartments.", "steps": ["[2] Cellulose can be difficult for the animal’s own digestive enzymes to hydrolyse.", "[2] Symbiotic microorganisms ferment plant material in specialised gut regions, supplying usable products.", "[2] Foregut and hindgut fermenters differ in region and structure; not every herbivore has the ruminant arrangement."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-animals-q6"}, {"title": "Exchange-system essay", "prompt": "Compare fish gills, mammalian alveoli and insect tracheal systems. Link structures to gradients and transport, and give one environmental limitation for each. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Gills use thin large-area lamellae and ventilated water; countercurrent blood supports a sustained oxygen gradient.", "[2] Fine gill structures are adapted to water and may collapse or dry in air.", "[2] Alveoli have thin moist barriers and large area; ventilation and blood flow maintain gradients and circulation distributes gases.", "[2] Alveoli require internal protection/ventilation and are vulnerable to impaired surfaces or transport, rather than functioning as exposed aquatic gills.", "[2] Tracheae distribute air from spiracles toward tissues and reduce dependence on circulatory oxygen transport.", "[2] Spiracle opening trades gas exchange against water loss; system size and diffusion distances constrain performance in the specific organism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-animals-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What defines an artery?", "o": ["Blood flows away from the heart", "Blood is always oxygenated", "Blood returns to the heart", "It has no lumen"], "a": 0, "why": "Direction relative to the heart defines artery/vein; pulmonary arteries demonstrate the oxygen-content exception."}, {"q": "How does countercurrent exchange help?", "o": ["It maintains a gradient along the surface", "It makes oxygen active transport", "It stops blood moving", "It removes all barriers instantly"], "a": 0, "why": "Opposing flows preserve a favourable concentration gradient across much of the exchange region."}, {"q": "Which removal is excretion?", "o": ["Eliminating nitrogenous metabolic waste", "Only undigested food leaving the gut", "Absorbing glucose", "Chewing starch"], "a": 0, "why": "Excretion removes metabolic wastes; egestion removes undigested gut contents."}, {"q": "Which structure is central to insect gas delivery?", "o": ["Tracheae connected to spiracles", "Only mammalian alveoli", "Only a four-chambered heart", "Only intestinal villi"], "a": 0, "why": "Tracheal tubes convey air toward tissues in the standard insect model, rather than relying primarily on blood for oxygen delivery."}, {"q": "Which waste generally requires least water for elimination?", "o": ["Uric acid", "Ammonia", "Dissolved nitrate in a leaf", "All wastes require identical water"], "a": 0, "why": "Uric-acid elimination conserves water but costs relatively more energy to synthesise, with species-specific details."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio11-plants", "year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – From single cells to multicellular organisms", "title": "Plant gas exchange, xylem, phloem and transport investigations", "scope": "Stomata; root uptake; cohesion, adhesion and transpiration; root pressure; phloem translocation and source–sink relationships.", "screens": [{"title": "Plant organisation and specialised tissues", "kind": "learn", "text": "Vascular plants contain tissues specialised for support, exchange, photosynthesis and transport. Root tissues obtain water and mineral ions, leaf tissues provide photosynthetic surfaces and stomatal exchange, and xylem and phloem connect regions. Structure reflects function: root hairs increase contact with soil, while many leaf mesophyll cells contain abundant chloroplasts. Plant cells depend on coordinated processes even though plants do not have the same organs and circulation as animals. Roots need cellular respiration and receive organic products from source tissues. A plant diagram should distinguish vascular transport from gas diffusion in leaf air spaces. Label the material moving through each route and explain how the specialised tissue contributes to the organism's needs.", "diagram": "planttissues"}, {"title": "Stomata and diffusion of gases", "kind": "learn", "text": "Stomata are pores associated with guard cells that regulate aperture. Carbon dioxide can diffuse into leaf air spaces for photosynthesis, while oxygen and water vapour can move outward under their gradients. Plants also respire, so net gas movement depends on the balance of processes and conditions. Gas exchange occurs through diffusion at stomata and plant surfaces rather than through xylem or phloem carrying air as the main pathway. Guard-cell turgor affects aperture, enabling regulation but creating a trade-off: opening pores can improve carbon dioxide supply while increasing water loss. Different plant species and environmental conditions produce different patterns. Avoid claiming all stomata always open by day or that a plant stops respiring when photosynthesis is occurring.", "diagram": "stomata"}, {"title": "Root hair uptake and water potential", "kind": "learn", "text": "Root hairs extend from specialised epidermal cells and increase the surface contacting soil water. Water can enter when water potential is lower in the root than in the surrounding soil, with solutes and pressure contributing to the relationship. Mineral ions may enter through channels or active transport depending on their gradients and the membrane proteins involved. Active ion uptake can contribute to osmotic conditions supporting water entry. Water is not normally pumped across the membrane using ATP in the same sense as an ion pump. Saline soil can reduce the favourable water-potential gradient even when moisture is present. Explain the direction of water movement using conditions on both sides of the membrane rather than assuming roots always absorb water regardless of soil chemistry.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Xylem structure and transported materials", "kind": "learn", "text": "Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral nutrients from roots toward other plant regions. Mature vessel elements and tracheids are adapted with reinforced walls and conductive pathways, with lignin contributing strength and resistance to collapse under tension. Their structural arrangement supports relatively low-resistance movement through a continuous column. Xylem also contributes to mechanical support. The main movement is generally upward from roots toward transpiring tissues, although flow patterns can vary locally with the conditions. Xylem should not be described as the main route for distributing sugars from photosynthesis. Link wall reinforcement, continuity and the water column to transport function, and distinguish the properties of the conductive elements from those of surrounding living cells.", "diagram": "xylem"}, {"title": "Transpiration and cohesion–tension", "kind": "learn", "text": "Transpiration is water loss as vapour from plant surfaces, largely through stomata in many vascular plants. Evaporation from moist internal surfaces and diffusion of vapour through pores lower water potential in the leaf. This contributes to tension that pulls water through xylem from roots. Cohesion between water molecules helps maintain a continuous column, while adhesion to walls contributes to its interaction with the pathway. Energy for much of the process comes from environmental heating and evaporation, rather than ATP-powered pumping along each vessel. The system depends on an adequate water supply and intact conductive pathways. Explain how events in the leaf connect to upward flow instead of treating transpiration as a separate event unrelated to root uptake.", "diagram": "transpiration"}, {"title": "Root pressure and capillary action", "kind": "learn", "text": "Root pressure can arise when active ion accumulation into root vascular regions promotes osmotic water entry and generates positive pressure. It can contribute to movement under some conditions and may be associated with guttation, which releases liquid water at specialised leaf structures. Capillary effects depend on cohesion, adhesion and narrow spaces, but do not alone explain water transport to the tops of very tall trees. Transpiration-driven tension usually provides the major mechanism under transpiring conditions. Compare these contributions instead of replacing the whole explanation with one label. Root pressure uses metabolically generated solute gradients, whereas transpiration pull is linked to evaporative water loss. Guttation droplets and dew have different origins even though both can appear on leaves.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Environmental factors and transpiration rate", "kind": "learn", "text": "Transpiration depends on the water-vapour gradient, stomatal aperture, available surface and boundary layer. Higher temperature can increase evaporation, dry air can increase the vapour gradient and wind can remove humid air near leaves. Light often promotes stomatal opening in many species, but water stress can trigger closure and reduce loss. Effects are not unlimited: severe stress or temperature can alter stomatal behaviour and damage tissues. Compare one factor while holding other conditions sufficiently constant. A statement that wind always increases transpiration overlooks possible responses under extreme conditions. Explain the physical gradient and any biological regulation. The same external conditions can affect plant species differently because their leaf structures, root systems and stomatal controls differ.", "diagram": "transpirationfactors"}, {"title": "Phloem and source–sink transport", "kind": "learn", "text": "Phloem transports organic products such as sucrose and some mineral nutrients between sources and sinks. Sieve tube elements are associated with companion cells that support transport processes. A source exports material, such as a photosynthesising leaf or a storage organ mobilising reserves, while a sink imports it for growth or storage. Source and sink status can change with season and developmental stage. Loading sucrose at a source can lower water potential, bringing water from xylem and increasing pressure. Unloading at a sink contributes to a pressure difference that drives bulk movement. The simplified pressure-flow model links osmosis and transport without implying every step is passive. Different sieve tubes can carry material in different directions, so phloem transport is not always downward.", "diagram": "phloem"}, {"title": "Xylem versus phloem and changing demands", "kind": "learn", "text": "Xylem and phloem differ in main transported materials, conductive structures and driving mechanisms. Xylem chiefly carries water and mineral ions through reinforced conductive elements, largely under transpiration-related tension. Phloem carries organic assimilates through living associated systems between sources and sinks, with loading and unloading supporting pressure gradients. Their functions interact because water can move between them. A developing fruit can be a strong sink, while a mature illuminated leaf acts as a source. Removing a strip of bark that disrupts phloem can affect sugar transport even when xylem initially remains functional. Explain the consequence for particular tissues and timescales rather than treating every transport failure as immediately stopping all movement of all substances.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Potometers and interpreting water uptake", "kind": "learn", "text": "A potometer estimates water uptake by a cut shoot under controlled conditions, often using bubble movement in a known capillary. It is a proxy for transpiration, because some water may remain in tissues or support metabolism rather than being lost immediately as vapour. A watertight setup, cutting under water, adequate acclimatisation and controlled environmental conditions improve the evidence. Compare uptake rates per suitable leaf area when shoots differ in size. Include independent shoots for replication rather than only repeatedly reading one apparatus. Bubble movement can be affected by leaks or temperature changes, so identify and test alternative causes. An ethical investigation should use appropriate plant material and avoid unnecessary damage. State the limitations of the measurement rather than claiming it directly counts water molecules leaving stomata.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Vascular transport", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Vascular transport</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Feature</th><th scope=\"col\">Xylem</th><th scope=\"col\">Phloem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conducting cells</td><td>Dead lignified vessels/tracheids</td><td>Living sieve elements with companion-cell support</td></tr><tr><td>Main cargo</td><td>Water and mineral ions</td><td>Organic solutes, especially sucrose</td></tr><tr><td>Driving mechanism</td><td>Transpiration-related tension, cohesion and water-potential gradients</td><td>Source loading, water entry and pressure gradient toward sink</td></tr><tr><td>Direction</td><td>Commonly root-to-leaf along transpiration pathway</td><td>Source-to-sink; different tubes can run different directions</td></tr><tr><td>Energy link</td><td>No ATP water pump in dead vessels</td><td>Loading/unloading often involves cellular energy</td></tr><tr><td>Investigation</td><td>Potometer estimates uptake, an indirect transpiration proxy</td><td>Tracer/girdling evidence requires careful interpretation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Cohesion–tension", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Cohesion–tension", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Source and sink", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Source and sink", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Potometer rate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Potometer rate", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Xylem and phloem", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Humidity effect", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Root uptake", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Transport investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Cohesion–tension", "prompt": "Explain the chain from leaf evaporation to water uptake at roots and state whether xylem has an ATP-powered water pump.", "steps": ["[2] Evaporation from leaf surfaces lowers water potential and generates tension in leaf xylem.", "[2] Cohesion transmits tension through continuous water columns, with adhesion supporting contact with walls.", "[2] Water enters roots and moves along the water-potential gradient; xylem flow is not powered by ATP water pumps in the dead conducting cells."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-plants-q1"}, {"title": "Source and sink", "prompt": "A mature leaf exports sucrose to a growing root. Explain a simplified pressure-flow model and why phloem is not always downward.", "steps": ["[2] Loading sugar at the source lowers water potential so water enters from xylem and raises pressure.", "[2] A pressure difference drives mass flow toward the sink, where unloading/use/storage helps maintain the gradient.", "[2] Source and sink positions vary; different tubes can carry material in different directions, so phloem is not universally downward."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-plants-q2"}, {"title": "Potometer rate", "prompt": "An air bubble moves 30 mm in 5 min through a capillary of area 0.20 mm². Estimate uptake rate and state why it is not a direct transpiration measurement.", "steps": ["[2] Volume=distance×area=30×0.20=6.0 mm³.", "[2] Rate=6.0/5=1.2 mm³ min⁻¹.", "[2] Uptake is a proxy because water also contributes to storage/growth and apparatus leaks may affect readings; not all uptake is immediately evaporated."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio11-plants-q3"}, {"title": "Xylem and phloem", "prompt": "Compare xylem and phloem conducting structures and transported materials.", "steps": ["[2] Xylem conducting vessels/tracheids are dead at maturity with lignified walls and carry water/mineral ions.", "[2] Phloem sieve elements are living and supported by companion cells; they translocate organic solutes including sucrose.", "[2] Xylem flow commonly follows the root-to-leaf transpiration pathway, while phloem follows changing source–sink pressure gradients."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-plants-q4"}, {"title": "Humidity effect", "prompt": "Predict and explain the short-term effect of higher humidity on transpiration when temperature, air movement and stomatal opening are unchanged.", "steps": ["[2] Higher humidity reduces the water-vapour concentration gradient from moist leaf air spaces to outside air.", "[2] Diffusive water loss generally decreases under the stated controls.", "[1] Actual stomatal responses and other environmental changes can modify the outcome."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-plants-q5"}, {"title": "Root uptake", "prompt": "Explain how roots can accumulate mineral ions against a gradient while water enters passively.", "steps": ["[2] Transport proteins can use energy directly or through ion gradients to accumulate selected mineral ions.", "[2] Solute accumulation can lower root water potential.", "[2] Water then enters by osmosis along its water-potential gradient; water is not actively pumped by the ion carrier."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-plants-q6"}, {"title": "Transport investigation", "prompt": "Design a potometer comparison of wind speeds and evaluate its validity as evidence about transpiration. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Use measured wind speed as the independent variable and capillary water uptake per time as the response.", "[2] Keep light, temperature, humidity, species and leaf area comparable; seal the apparatus and use a consistent acclimation period.", "[2] Calculate volume rate from capillary area and movement/time, correcting or normalising for leaf area where justified.", "[2] Use independent plant replicates and compare means and variation, avoiding repeated readings from one shoot as sole replication.", "[2] Faster wind can remove humid boundary air and increase the gradient, but stomatal closure can modify responses.", "[2] Uptake is an indirect proxy, with storage, growth and leaks as limitations; a separate mass-loss approach or other measurement can strengthen the interpretation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio11-plants-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What supplies the principal pull in transpiration-driven xylem flow?", "o": ["Evaporation generating tension", "ATP pumps in dead vessels", "Phloem walls producing sunlight", "Cell walls actively drinking"], "a": 0, "why": "Leaf evaporation creates tension transmitted through cohesive water columns; xylem is not an ATP water pump."}, {"q": "What do source and sink describe?", "o": ["Net export and import/use/storage regions", "Always top and bottom", "Only root and flower respectively", "Only oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor tissue"], "a": 0, "why": "Sources and sinks depend on developmental state and activity, so phloem direction is not universally downward."}, {"q": "What is a potometer’s direct measurement?", "o": ["Water uptake", "Every molecule evaporated", "Gross photosynthesis", "Blood pressure"], "a": 0, "why": "Water uptake approximates transpiration but differs because storage, growth and leaks can affect measured flow."}, {"q": "With other conditions fixed, higher humidity usually does what?", "o": ["Reduces vapour gradient and water loss", "Guarantees doubled loss", "Stops every root process", "Raises light intensity"], "a": 0, "why": "Higher external humidity lowers the diffusion gradient, although stomatal/environmental interactions can modify real responses."}, {"q": "Which conducting tissue is usually dead at maturity?", "o": ["Xylem vessels", "Phloem companion cells", "Leaf mesophyll", "Root meristem"], "a": 0, "why": "Xylem conducting elements are lignified and dead at maturity, while phloem relies on living sieve elements and companion cells."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-dna-proteins", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Continuity of species", "title": "DNA, replication, genetic code and protein synthesis", "scope": "DNA and RNA; chromosomes; semiconservative replication; transcription; translation; sequence interpretation and phenotype.", "screens": [{"title": "Genes, alleles and genetic information", "kind": "learn", "text": "DNA carries inherited information through its nucleotide sequence. A gene is a DNA sequence contributing to a functional product, often a protein or a functional RNA. An allele is a variant of a gene at a particular locus. Chromosomes contain many genes and other DNA sequences, so gene and chromosome are not interchangeable. The genome is the full genetic material of an organism in the relevant context. Eukaryotic nuclear chromosomes associate DNA with proteins, while mitochondria and chloroplasts also contain DNA. Prokaryotes typically have a circular chromosome in the cytosol's nucleoid region and can carry plasmids. Accurate terminology matters when explaining heredity: an offspring receives particular alleles, not a different basic gene for every version of a trait.", "diagram": "dna"}, {"title": "Nucleotides, pairing and antiparallel strands", "kind": "learn", "text": "A DNA nucleotide contains a phosphate group, deoxyribose sugar and one nitrogenous base: adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine. Covalent bonds form each sugar–phosphate backbone, while hydrogen bonds connect complementary bases between strands. Adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine with guanine. The strands run in opposite orientations, described as antiparallel, and form a double helix. Complementary pairing allows one strand to provide information for rebuilding the other. Do not confuse the weaker hydrogen bonds between bases with the covalent backbone bonds. A simplified ladder diagram is useful for showing pairing but omits the three-dimensional helix and many molecular interactions. When writing sequences, include orientation when the question supplies or requires it.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Semiconservative DNA replication", "kind": "learn", "text": "Before a cell divides, DNA must be copied so inherited information can be transferred. The two strands separate, and each acts as a template for synthesis of a complementary strand. DNA polymerase catalyses addition of appropriate nucleotides and extends a new strand in its permitted direction. Each completed DNA double helix contains one original strand and one newly synthesised strand, so replication is semiconservative. Complementary pairing supports accurate copying, while proofreading and repair reduce errors without eliminating them completely. The process occurs before chromosome separation in mitosis or meiosis, not as a new full replication between meiotic divisions. Explain how pairing and strand separation make copying possible rather than simply saying DNA doubles because the cell needs more.", "diagram": "replication"}, {"title": "Replication direction and model limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "New DNA is synthesised in the 5′ to 3′ direction. Because template strands are antiparallel, synthesis at a replication fork proceeds differently on the two templates: one new strand can be extended more continuously, while the other is assembled from segments that are later joined. This extension explains the direction constraint without requiring every molecular component to be memorised for the syllabus. A drawing should distinguish old and new strands and show the consequence of complementarity. Replication is a regulated enzyme-mediated process, not random bases permanently sticking wherever they touch. A simplified model can represent the central mechanism while omitting multiple replication origins, detailed proofreading and chromatin organisation. Identify what is included and what remains beyond the diagram's purpose.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "DNA, RNA and the triplet code", "kind": "learn", "text": "RNA usually contains a single strand with ribose sugar and uracil in place of DNA's thymine. Messenger RNA carries a sequence that can be translated at ribosomes. The genetic code is read in base triplets, with an mRNA triplet called a codon. Most codons specify an amino acid, while some signal termination. Several codons can specify the same amino acid, making the code redundant or degenerate. This does not mean one codon specifies any amino acid arbitrarily. Use an mRNA codon table unless the question states another convention, and convert a DNA sequence appropriately first. A reading-frame shift changes how subsequent triplets are grouped and can therefore alter more than one amino acid.", "diagram": "geneticcode"}, {"title": "Transcription in eukaryotic cells", "kind": "learn", "text": "Transcription synthesises RNA using a DNA template. In eukaryotic cells, transcription of nuclear genes occurs in the nucleus. RNA polymerase uses complementary base pairing, with RNA uracil pairing to template adenine. The DNA coding strand has the same base sequence as the corresponding RNA segment except for thymine in place of uracil, when both are written in the same direction. Processing can modify the initial RNA transcript before export, including removal of relevant intervening sequences in many eukaryotic genes. Coding and non-coding DNA should not be confused with coding versus template strands: these describe different distinctions. Explain which strand provides the template, what molecule is produced and where the process occurs in the stated organism.", "diagram": "transcription"}, {"title": "Translation at ribosomes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Translation uses the mRNA codon sequence to assemble an amino acid chain at a ribosome. Transfer RNA molecules carry amino acids and contain anticodons that pair with suitable codons. The ribosome coordinates recognition and peptide-bond formation, advancing through the message until a stop signal is reached. The resulting polypeptide may need folding and modification before it functions as a protein. The DNA does not normally leave the nucleus to be directly read by a cytoplasmic ribosome in a eukaryotic cell. Distinguish transcription from translation by template, product and location. A diagram that includes mRNA, tRNA, amino acids and ribosome should label each role rather than showing protein appearing merely because RNA is present.", "diagram": "translation"}, {"title": "A worked sequence convention", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose the DNA template is written 3′–TAC GGA CTT–5′. The complementary mRNA is 5′–AUG CCU GAA–3′, using U rather than T. With a standard mRNA codon table, AUG codes for methionine, CCU for proline and GAA for glutamate. The corresponding DNA coding strand is 5′–ATG CCT GAA–3′. This example is a short teaching sequence, not a complete gene or functional protein. If the question gives a strand in the opposite direction, orientation must be considered before using the codon table. Show the conversion clearly to reduce avoidable errors. A tRNA anticodon is complementary to the codon and should not simply be copied as the same triplet.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Proteins connect genotype and cellular function", "kind": "learn", "text": "Proteins contribute to structure, transport, signalling and catalysis. A DNA sequence can influence a protein's amino acid sequence, which can influence folding and activity. Changes in a transporter or enzyme can then affect cellular processes and contribute to an observable phenotype. The connection is not always one gene producing one visible trait: many traits depend on multiple genes and environmental influences, and some DNA sequences produce functional RNA rather than protein. Non-coding sequences can have regulatory or structural roles, so they should not all be called useless. A strong explanation traces the relevant steps from genetic information through a functional product to the particular trait, while acknowledging what evidence would be needed to establish the effect of a variant.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Gene expression, environment and phenotype", "kind": "learn", "text": "Phenotype results from interactions between genetic information and the environment. Cells can express different genes or different amounts of their products, contributing to specialisation and responses to conditions. Nutrition, temperature or light can influence traits without necessarily changing the inherited DNA sequence. The currently prescribed syllabus specifies that epigenetics is not required, so detailed epigenetic mechanisms are extension material rather than a core demand. Compare genotypic change with an environmental effect on expression or function. An environmental effect can alter the performance of an enzyme or development of a structure, and the same genotype can produce different outcomes under different conditions. Avoid interpreting every visible difference as a new allele or assuming genes determine traits independently of context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sequence orientation and gene expression", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Sequence orientation and gene expression</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Item</th><th scope=\"col\">Reference</th><th scope=\"col\">Check your answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DNA pairing</td><td>A–T and C–G; antiparallel strands</td><td>Covalent backbone differs from hydrogen bonds between paired bases</td></tr><tr><td>Template example</td><td>3′ TAC GGA CTT 5′</td><td>Read template 3′→5′</td></tr><tr><td>mRNA example</td><td>5′ AUG CCU GAA 3′</td><td>RNA uses U, not T</td></tr><tr><td>Coding DNA example</td><td>5′ ATG CCT GAA 3′</td><td>Same sequence as mRNA except T/U</td></tr><tr><td>Translation fragment</td><td>Met–Pro–Glu</td><td>Use an mRNA codon table and correct frame</td></tr><tr><td>Allele/gene</td><td>Variant at locus / functional genetic sequence</td><td>Not synonyms for chromosome or trait</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Sequence conversion", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Sequence conversion", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Semiconservative replication", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Semiconservative replication", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Protein consequence", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Protein consequence", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transcription and translation", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Gene terminology", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Frameshift", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: From DNA to phenotype", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Sequence conversion", "prompt": "For DNA template 3′-TAC GGA CTT-5′, write mRNA 5′→3′ and the corresponding peptide using AUG=Met, CCU=Pro and GAA=Glu. Distinguish the coding strand.", "steps": ["[2] Complementary antiparallel mRNA is 5′-AUG CCU GAA-3′.", "[1] The peptide in this fragment is Met–Pro–Glu.", "[2] Coding DNA is 5′-ATG CCT GAA-3′, matching mRNA except T replaces U.", "[1] The template is read 3′→5′ while RNA is synthesised 5′→3′."], "allocations": [2, 1, 2, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q1"}, {"title": "Semiconservative replication", "prompt": "Explain semiconservative DNA replication, including pairing, polymerase direction and its relationship to cell division.", "steps": ["[2] Strands separate and each original strand acts as a template for a complementary new strand.", "[2] DNA polymerase extends new DNA 5′→3′; A pairs with T and C with G.", "[2] Each product double helix contains one original and one new strand; replication precedes division and is not repeated between meiosis I and II."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q2"}, {"title": "Protein consequence", "prompt": "A coding-DNA substitution changes GAA to GAG. Both mRNA codons encode glutamate. Explain why this may leave the peptide unchanged and why not every DNA mutation changes phenotype.", "steps": ["[2] The genetic code is redundant: different codons can specify the same amino acid.", "[2] This substitution is synonymous for the stipulated codon, preserving that amino acid in the peptide.", "[2] Effects depend on location, regulation, protein role and environment; sequence change alone does not establish a visible phenotype."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q3"}, {"title": "Transcription and translation", "prompt": "Compare transcription and translation in a typical eukaryotic cell using template, product, location and major machinery.", "steps": ["[2] Transcription uses DNA as a template to make RNA in the nucleus, involving RNA polymerase.", "[2] Translation uses mRNA codons at ribosomes in the cytoplasm or on RER to assemble amino acids into a polypeptide.", "[2] tRNA matches anticodons to codons and delivers amino acids; it does not replicate the DNA."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q4"}, {"title": "Gene terminology", "prompt": "Distinguish gene, allele, chromosome and genome and explain why genotype does not always specify one inevitable phenotype.", "steps": ["[1] A gene is a DNA sequence contributing to a functional product, including RNA or protein.", "[1] An allele is a variant at a genetic locus.", "[1] A chromosome is an organised DNA/protein structure containing many loci.", "[1] A genome is the organism’s total genetic material.", "[2] Expression, interactions between genes and environmental conditions can affect phenotype."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q5"}, {"title": "Frameshift", "prompt": "Compare a one-base deletion in a coding region with a three-base deletion, excluding regulatory effects. Explain why a frameshift often has a greater downstream effect.", "steps": ["[2] A one-base deletion shifts the triplet reading frame downstream and can change many codons or create a premature stop.", "[2] A three-base deletion preserves the downstream frame but removes an amino acid, potentially affecting function.", "[1] Effects depend on position and protein requirements; neither guarantees lethality or a particular phenotype."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q6"}, {"title": "From DNA to phenotype", "prompt": "Explain how inherited DNA information can influence a phenotype. Include replication, gene expression, protein function and environmental influence, using an original enzyme example. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Replication copies hereditary DNA through complementary semiconservative synthesis before appropriate cell divisions.", "[2] Transcription produces RNA from a DNA template; regulated expression affects where/when products are made.", "[2] Ribosomes translate mRNA codons using tRNA-delivered amino acids, producing a polypeptide.", "[2] Sequence affects folding and an enzyme’s active site; an allele can change activity or amount without every mutation changing peptide sequence.", "[2] A changed enzyme can alter a reaction pathway and therefore a measurable trait, such as pigment production.", "[2] Temperature, nutrients and other genes can modify expression or activity, so genotype, mechanism and environment must be linked rather than treating phenotype as inevitable."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-dna-proteins-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "For template 3′-TAC-5′, what is mRNA?", "o": ["5′-AUG-3′", "5′-TAC-3′", "3′-ATG-5′", "5′-UAC-3′"], "a": 0, "why": "Complementary antiparallel RNA pairs A with U and C with G; template TAC gives AUG."}, {"q": "What is semiconservative replication?", "o": ["Each product has one old and one new strand", "Both products contain only old strands", "One product is entirely old and the other entirely new", "Every base is converted to RNA"], "a": 0, "why": "Original strands each template new complementary strands, conserving one in each daughter double helix."}, {"q": "What does code redundancy mean?", "o": ["Several codons can encode one amino acid", "Each codon must encode four amino acids", "Proteins contain no information", "All DNA changes are harmful"], "a": 0, "why": "Synonymous codons can leave the amino-acid sequence unchanged, though other effects can still depend on context."}, {"q": "Which molecule brings amino acids to translation machinery?", "o": ["tRNA", "A chromosome wall", "Nitrate", "Phospholipid"], "a": 0, "why": "Transfer RNA matches anticodon/codon information and delivers the corresponding amino acid to a ribosome."}, {"q": "Why may genotype not determine a unique measured phenotype?", "o": ["Gene interactions and environment affect expression/function", "DNA never affects proteins", "All traits are acquired only", "Every allele is dominant"], "a": 0, "why": "Phenotype emerges through gene expression, product interactions and environmental influences rather than a universal one-genotype one-outcome rule."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-division", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Continuity of species", "title": "Cell division, meiosis, fertilisation and variation", "scope": "Binary fission; cell cycle; mitosis versus meiosis; chromosome accounting; crossing over; assortment; mutation.", "screens": [{"title": "Replication before division and chromosome accounting", "kind": "learn", "text": "A chromosome is counted by its centromere under the conventional school counting system. After DNA replication, one duplicated chromosome consists of two sister chromatids joined together, so DNA amount doubles without necessarily doubling chromosome number. Homologous chromosomes are corresponding maternal and paternal chromosomes with the same types of genes at corresponding loci, but they can carry different alleles. Sister chromatids are copies produced from one chromosome and are initially very similar. Distinguish these relationships when drawing meiosis. Ploidy describes the number of chromosome sets, not the number of DNA strands or chromatids. A diploid cell has two sets and a haploid cell one, while chromosomes in either cell may be duplicated or unduplicated depending on the stage.", "diagram": "chromosomes"}, {"title": "Binary fission in prokaryotes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Binary fission produces two cells from one prokaryotic cell after replication and separation of its genetic material. The cell grows, copies its chromosome, distributes copies and separates through membrane and wall changes. It does not involve the same nuclear division stages as eukaryotic mitosis because there is no membrane-bound nucleus. Offspring are genetically similar to the parent under normal copying, but mutations can introduce differences. Plasmid distribution and genetic exchange can also influence prokaryotic variation. Binary fission is a reproductive process for a unicellular organism, not merely growth of one tissue. Explain the continuity of genetic information and increase in cell number while recognising that a simplified two-cell diagram omits regulatory and structural differences among prokaryotic groups.", "diagram": "fission"}, {"title": "The cell cycle and mitotic purposes", "kind": "learn", "text": "The cell cycle includes periods of growth and preparation, DNA replication and division. Interphase is metabolically active and includes the DNA synthesis stage, so it should not be described simply as a resting phase. Mitosis distributes replicated chromosomes into daughter nuclei, and cytokinesis divides the cell's cytoplasm. The processes support growth, repair and replacement in many multicellular organisms and asexual reproduction in some eukaryotes. Regulation limits inappropriate division, and daughter cells can differentiate after division. Mitosis generally preserves chromosome set number, but it does not create genetic uniformity across every cell in an organism indefinitely because mutations and later expression differences can occur. State the role and outcome required by the particular biological context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Mitosis: chromosome movement", "kind": "learn", "text": "During mitosis, chromosomes condense, a spindle forms and nuclear envelope arrangements change. Duplicated chromosomes align with spindle attachments so that sister chromatids can separate toward opposite poles. New nuclear arrangements form around the separated sets before or alongside cytokinesis. The familiar stages are prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase, with more detailed classifications sometimes distinguishing intermediate events. The key mechanism is accurate separation of sister chromatids to preserve genetic information in daughter cells. A labelled model should show what separates and where it moves. Do not draw homologous pairing and crossing over as ordinary mitotic events. Cytokinesis differs structurally between many animal and plant cells, with a cleavage furrow or formation of a cell plate respectively.", "diagram": "mitosis"}, {"title": "Meiosis I: homologues and reduction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Meiosis begins after one round of DNA replication and includes two divisions. During meiosis I, homologous chromosomes pair, and non-sister chromatids can exchange corresponding segments through crossing over. Homologous pairs align at the equator, and the homologues then separate to opposite cells. Sister chromatids ordinarily remain together during this first separation. Reduction in chromosome set number occurs because each daughter receives one member of each homologous pair. The orientation of different pairs can vary independently, contributing to genetic variation. Clearly distinguish homologous chromosomes from sister chromatids when naming what separates. A cell entering meiosis with two sets can produce cells with one set after the first division even though each chromosome may still contain two chromatids.", "diagram": "meiosis"}, {"title": "Meiosis II and haploid products", "kind": "learn", "text": "Meiosis II separates sister chromatids after the first division, without a new full round of DNA replication between the divisions. A typical model produces four haploid products from one original diploid cell. The products can differ genetically because of crossing over and chromosome assortment. In actual reproductive development, products can have different sizes or fates, as in animal egg formation, so four identical-looking circles should not imply four equivalent functioning eggs. The syllabus mechanism concerns transfer of genetic information and variation. In a species with diploid number 2n = 8, the final products ordinarily have n = 4 chromosomes, rather than two because there are two divisions. Count chromosome sets and identify which structures separate at each stage.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Crossing over creates new combinations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Crossing over exchanges corresponding DNA segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I. This can create new combinations of existing alleles on a chromatid. It ordinarily does not create a new allele in the way mutation can, although unusual exchanges can cause changes. Show matching loci and exchanged segments in a drawing, keeping the homologues distinguishable. Crossing over can separate alleles at linked loci, with the frequency depending in part on their relative positions. The basic implication is increased genetic variety among meiotic products. An explanation should state the event, stage, structures involved and effect on allele combinations instead of using the word swapping without identifying what is exchanged.", "diagram": "crossingover"}, {"title": "Independent assortment and fertilisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Independent assortment arises from the alternative orientations of homologous pairs during meiosis I, producing different combinations of chromosomes in gametes when the loci or chromosomes under discussion assort independently. For n homologous pairs, the simple model allows 2ⁿ combinations from assortment alone, excluding crossing-over effects. Random fertilisation adds variation by combining gametes from two parents, restoring the diploid set number in a zygote. The product is not a complete copy of either parent. These processes recombine existing variation; mutation remains the ultimate source of new alleles. Use probabilities carefully, recognising assumptions about independent events and compatible gametes. A genetic prediction gives an expected distribution over many reproductive events, not a promise of a fixed outcome for a small family.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Gene mutations and their possible effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mutations are changes in genetic material that can arise during replication or through physical and chemical damage. A base substitution can be silent, change an amino acid or create a premature stop, depending on the sequence and genetic code. Insertions or deletions not divisible by three can shift the reading frame in a coding region, potentially altering many later codons. The effect also depends on location and biological context. Mutations are not directed toward what the organism needs and are not necessarily harmful or beneficial in every environment. Some affect non-coding or regulatory regions, while others have little detectable phenotype. Distinguish a new allele from recombination of existing alleles, and explain the mechanism rather than assuming every mutation changes chromosome number.", "diagram": "mutation"}, {"title": "Chromosome changes, inheritance and errors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chromosomal mutations can involve deletion, duplication, inversion or translocation of segments. Errors in chromosome separation, including non-disjunction, can produce cells with an abnormal number of particular chromosomes. Such outcomes differ from a single base substitution. A mutation in a cell contributing to gametes can be transmitted to offspring, while a mutation in a typical somatic cell is not inherited through sexual reproduction in the same way. Some plants and asexually reproducing systems create additional contexts for transmission, so define the organism and route. Reduced fertility or altered development can follow certain changes, but effects vary. The comparison between mitosis and meiosis should include number of divisions, what separates, ploidy outcome, genetic variation and biological purpose.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cell division and chromosome counts", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Cell division and chromosome counts</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Stage/example</th><th scope=\"col\">Chromosomes per cell</th><th scope=\"col\">Key event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diploid 2n=8 before replication</td><td>8 unduplicated</td><td>Homologous pairs present</td></tr><tr><td>After replication</td><td>8 duplicated; 16 chromatids</td><td>DNA amount doubles, centromere count remains 8</td></tr><tr><td>After meiosis I</td><td>4 duplicated</td><td>Homologous chromosomes separated</td></tr><tr><td>After meiosis II</td><td>4 unduplicated</td><td>Sisters separated; no intermediate replication</td></tr><tr><td>After fertilisation</td><td>8 in the new diploid cell</td><td>Haploid sets combine</td></tr><tr><td>Mitosis</td><td>Ordinarily same ploidy as starting cell</td><td>Sister separation for growth/repair and other roles</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Chromosome counts", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Chromosome counts", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Sources of variation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Sources of variation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Mitosis comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Mitosis comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Non-disjunction", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Independent combinations", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Mutation origin", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Division and variation essay", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Chromosome counts", "prompt": "A species has 2n=8. State chromosome numbers in a cell after DNA replication, after meiosis I and after meiosis II, using centromeres for counting.", "steps": ["[2] After replication there are still 8 chromosomes, each with two sister chromatids.", "[2] After meiosis I each daughter cell has 4 duplicated chromosomes: homologues separated but sisters remain.", "[2] After meiosis II each gamete has 4 unduplicated chromosomes; no intervening DNA replication occurs."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-division-q1"}, {"title": "Sources of variation", "prompt": "Explain crossing over and independent assortment and distinguish their effects from mutation.", "steps": ["[2] Crossing over exchanges corresponding segments between non-sister chromatids of paired homologues in meiosis I.", "[2] Independent orientation of homologous pairs distributes maternal/paternal combinations into gametes.", "[2] These reshuffle existing alleles; mutation is an ultimate source of new alleles rather than every crossover automatically creating one."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-division-q2"}, {"title": "Mitosis comparison", "prompt": "Compare mitosis and meiosis in the number of divisions, separation events, ploidy and usual roles.", "steps": ["[2] Mitosis has one division separating sister chromatids, generally retaining chromosome number for growth, repair or asexual reproduction.", "[2] Meiosis has two divisions: homologues separate in I and sisters in II.", "[2] Meiosis reduces ploidy from diploid to haploid for gamete production and creates varied combinations; fertilisation restores diploidy."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-division-q3"}, {"title": "Non-disjunction", "prompt": "A homologous pair fails to separate in meiosis I. Explain why all four resulting gametes may have an abnormal number for that chromosome in the simple model.", "steps": ["[2] Both homologues enter one daughter cell and neither enters the other.", "[2] Meiosis II separates sisters in each cell, producing two gametes with an extra copy and two lacking the chromosome.", "[1] Fertilisation can produce aneuploid offspring, with effects dependent on the chromosome and organism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-division-q4"}, {"title": "Independent combinations", "prompt": "A diploid species has five chromosome pairs. Calculate possible whole-chromosome combinations from independent assortment alone and state an assumption.", "steps": ["[2] 2⁵=32 combinations per gamete.", "[1] Each homologous pair has two orientations relative to the others.", "[2] This assumes independent assortment and ignores crossing over and other sources of variation, so it is not the total number of genetically possible gametes."], "allocations": [2, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-division-q5"}, {"title": "Mutation origin", "prompt": "Compare germline and somatic mutations and correct the claim that a bacterium makes a useful resistance mutation because it needs to survive an antibiotic.", "steps": ["[2] Germline mutations can enter gametes and be inherited; somatic mutations affect descendant cells within the organism and usually not offspring through gametes.", "[2] Mutations arise without anticipating the selective benefit required by an antibiotic.", "[2] Antibiotic exposure favours existing or newly arising resistant variants through differential survival/reproduction; need does not direct the useful mutation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-division-q6"}, {"title": "Division and variation essay", "prompt": "Explain how meiosis and fertilisation generate genetic variation while maintaining a species’ chromosome number across sexual generations. Compare one process with mitosis. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] DNA replicates once before meiosis; homologues pair and can exchange segments between non-sister chromatids.", "[2] Independent orientation of homologous pairs at meiosis I produces different parental chromosome combinations.", "[2] Homologues separate in I and sisters in II, yielding haploid products without another DNA replication.", "[2] Random fertilisation combines gametes and restores the diploid number.", "[2] These events recombine existing alleles while mutations can add new alleles, with neither purposeful adaptation nor identical outcomes assumed.", "[2] Mitosis separates sisters in one division and ordinarily retains number and genetic information for growth/repair, rather than reducing ploidy."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-division-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "A 2n=8 cell has replicated DNA. How many chromosomes before separation?", "o": ["8", "16", "4", "2"], "a": 0, "why": "Chromosomes are counted by centromeres; replication produces sister chromatids without doubling chromosome number at this stage."}, {"q": "What separates at meiosis I?", "o": ["Homologous chromosomes", "Normally all sister chromatids", "Every nucleotide", "Only ribosomes"], "a": 0, "why": "Meiosis I segregates homologues, whereas meiosis II separates sister chromatids."}, {"q": "Between which chromatids does ordinary meiotic crossing over occur?", "o": ["Non-sister chromatids of homologues", "Only sister chromatids of one chromosome", "Only unrelated RNA strands", "Only cell-wall fibres"], "a": 0, "why": "Corresponding segments exchange between non-sister chromatids in paired homologous chromosomes."}, {"q": "How many independent-assortment combinations for n=4, ignoring crossover?", "o": ["16", "8", "4", "32"], "a": 0, "why": "Two orientations for each of four pairs give 2⁴=16 whole-chromosome combinations in the simplified model."}, {"q": "Which event restores diploidy after haploid gamete production?", "o": ["Fertilisation", "Another meiosis II without fusion", "Denaturation", "Gel electrophoresis"], "a": 0, "why": "Fusion of haploid gametes combines chromosome sets, restoring the diploid number in the standard sexual cycle."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-inheritance", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Continuity of species", "title": "Inheritance patterns, Punnett squares and pedigrees", "scope": "Dominance, codominance, incomplete dominance, multiple alleles, sex linkage, polygenes and probability.", "screens": [{"title": "Genotype, phenotype and a notation system", "kind": "learn", "text": "A genotype describes the allele combination at the relevant locus or loci, while a phenotype describes the observed or measured trait. A homozygote has two matching alleles at a diploid locus and a heterozygote has different alleles. In a simple dominance model, A can represent a dominant allele and a a recessive allele, but explain the symbols before using them. Dominance concerns the phenotype expressed in a heterozygote, not which allele is common, stronger or more evolutionarily advanced. Environmental effects and gene interactions can complicate the relationship. Identify parental genotypes from evidence, derive possible gametes and then combine them. A Punnett square displays possible combinations and expected probabilities under its assumptions rather than recording actual offspring automatically.", "diagram": "punnett"}, {"title": "Monohybrid crosses and expected ratios", "kind": "learn", "text": "For Aa × Aa, each parent produces A or a gametes with probability one half under normal segregation. The possible offspring combinations are AA, Aa, Aa and aa, giving expected genotype proportions of one quarter, one half and one quarter. With complete dominance, the expected dominant-to-recessive phenotype ratio is 3:1. The probabilities concern independent reproductive events, and a small actual sample may depart from the ratio through chance. A previously born recessive offspring does not make the next child more or less likely to be recessive in this simple model. Show the gametes, square and genotype–phenotype interpretation. Do not use the 3:1 phenotype ratio for a cross with different parental genotypes or a different pattern of dominance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Test crosses and inferring an unknown genotype", "kind": "learn", "text": "An individual showing a dominant phenotype could be homozygous dominant or heterozygous under a simple model. A test cross pairs it with a homozygous recessive individual. If the unknown is Aa, expected offspring are half Aa and half aa; if it is AA, all offspring are expected to be Aa. Observing a recessive offspring supports the presence of the recessive allele in the unknown parent, under the stated assumptions. Observing only dominant offspring in a small sample cannot prove homozygosity because chance may conceal a heterozygous cross. Distinguish evidence from certainty and explain what additional offspring or genetic testing would improve confidence. The method assumes a known inheritance pattern and that relevant environmental or scoring effects do not obscure the phenotypes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Incomplete dominance and codominance", "kind": "learn", "text": "In incomplete dominance, the heterozygous phenotype is distinguishable and often intermediate between the two homozygous phenotypes, such as an intermediate pigment level in a simplified flower example. In codominance, both allelic effects are detectably expressed in the heterozygote rather than combining into one intermediate effect. These patterns change genotype–phenotype relationships but do not invalidate the segregation of alleles during meiosis. A heterozygote cross can produce a 1:2:1 phenotype ratio as well as a 1:2:1 genotype ratio when all three phenotypes are distinct. Use an appropriate notation and define each genotype's phenotype. Saying the dominant allele is weaker in incomplete dominance is less useful than explaining the actual heterozygote outcome and the distinction from codominance.", "diagram": "dominance"}, {"title": "Multiple alleles and the ABO model", "kind": "learn", "text": "A population can contain more than two alleles at a locus even though a diploid individual carries only two there. The ABO blood-group model uses Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i. Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant, while each is dominant relative to i. Genotypes IᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi give phenotype A, IᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi give B, IᴬIᴮ gives AB and ii gives O in the simplified model. If Iᴬi and Iᴮi parents are crossed, the expected phenotypes A, B, AB and O each occur with probability one quarter. This is a genetic teaching model, not a complete guide to clinical transfusion. State the parental genotypes because a phenotype A or B alone may not uniquely determine the alleles.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Autosomal and X-linked inheritance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Autosomal loci are on non-sex chromosomes. An X-linked locus is on the X chromosome and follows transmission patterns influenced by the sex-chromosome arrangement. In a standard XX/XY model for an X-linked recessive allele, an XY individual has only one allele at that X locus, so a recessive allele can be expressed without a matching second copy. Fathers pass their X chromosome to daughters and their Y to sons in that model, so an X-linked allele is not directly passed from father to son. Use symbols such as Xᴬ and Xᵃ and write the Y appropriately. Avoid treating sex-linked as a synonym for a trait that merely differs between sexes because of hormonal or environmental effects.", "diagram": "sexlinked"}, {"title": "Conditional probabilities in sex-linked crosses", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an XᴬXᵃ mother and XᴬY father, the standard model predicts XᴬXᴬ, XᴬXᵃ, XᴬY and XᵃY offspring with equal probability. The probability of an affected son among all offspring is one quarter, while the probability of being affected conditional on the offspring being a son is one half. The wording of the question determines the denominator. A carrier daughter in an X-linked recessive model has a different phenotype from an affected daughter under the simplified assumptions. Show the gametes and explain which cases satisfy the requested outcome. Do not label half of all offspring affected simply because half of the sons are affected. The square represents a model with stated chromosomal and inheritance assumptions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Two loci, independence and linked genes", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an AaBb individual with independent assortment at the loci, the possible gametes AB, Ab, aB and ab have equal probability. A cross between two such individuals can give a 9:3:3:1 phenotype ratio under complete dominance at both loci and no relevant interactions. This is an extension application of probability, not a ratio to impose on every two-trait question. Linked genes on the same chromosome may produce different gamete frequencies, especially when crossing over is limited. Use information supplied about linkage and recombination rather than assuming all loci assort independently. Multiplication of probabilities is appropriate only when the events are independent under the model. Explicit assumptions improve both calculations and explanations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Polygenic traits and environmental interaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Polygenic traits depend on contributions from several genes and often show continuous variation, with environmental influences adding to the distribution. Height, for example, is affected by many genetic variants and developmental conditions. A smooth trait distribution does not mean one allele has unlimited values, and a continuous trait cannot usually be analysed with one simple dominant–recessive Punnett square. Multiple alleles concerns several variants at one locus, while polygenic inheritance concerns contributions from several loci. Keep those concepts distinct. Family resemblance supports genetic influence but does not remove shared environmental explanations. A biological account should connect gene effects, environment and phenotype without claiming that a broad continuous trait is fully determined by one gene or always inherited in a fixed ratio.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reading and evaluating pedigrees", "kind": "learn", "text": "A pedigree shows relationships and trait occurrence across generations using a stated symbol convention. Squares and circles commonly represent sexes in the classical convention, shading indicates the recorded phenotype, and lines show parentage and partnerships. Infer genotypes using an explicit inheritance hypothesis and test it against every relevant relationship. An autosomal recessive trait can appear in offspring of unaffected carrier parents, while an autosomal dominant trait often appears in successive generations, but small families can produce ambiguous patterns. X-linked inheritance adds sex-specific transmission clues. Absence of a phenotype does not always prove absence of an allele. State uncertainty when several models fit, and distinguish a calculated probability under a model from a medically confirmed diagnosis or a guarantee for a real family.", "diagram": "pedigree"}, {"title": "Inheritance models", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Inheritance models</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Model</th><th scope=\"col\">Example</th><th scope=\"col\">Expected relationship</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complete dominance</td><td>Aa×Aa</td><td>1AA:2Aa:1aa; 3 dominant:1 recessive phenotype</td></tr><tr><td>Incomplete dominance</td><td>RR red, Rr pink, rr white</td><td>Heterozygote intermediate; phenotype 1:2:1 in Rr×Rr</td></tr><tr><td>Codominance</td><td>IᴬIᴮ expresses AB</td><td>Both allele products expressed, not blending</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple alleles</td><td>Iᴬ, Iᴮ, i in a population</td><td>Each diploid individual has two at the locus</td></tr><tr><td>X-linked recessive</td><td>XᴬXᵃ×XᴬY</td><td>1/4 all offspring affected; 1/2 conditional on a son in stated model</td></tr><tr><td>Polygenic trait</td><td>Several loci contribute to a trait</td><td>Distinct from multiple alleles at one locus</td></tr><tr><td>Hemizygous</td><td>One copy of a locus in the stipulated XY X-linked model</td><td>A recessive X allele can be expressed without a second X-locus allele</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Explore the numbers: punnett", "kind": "interactive", "model": "punnett"}, {"title": "Worked question: Monohybrid cross", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Monohybrid cross", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: X-linked risk", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: X-linked risk", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: ABO probabilities", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: ABO probabilities", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Test cross", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Incomplete dominance", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Pedigree reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Inheritance comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Monohybrid cross", "prompt": "For complete dominance, cross Aa × Aa. Give gametes, genotype proportions and phenotype proportions and explain why four actual offspring need not show the ratio.", "steps": ["[2] Each parent produces A and a gametes; outcomes are AA, Aa, Aa, aa.", "[2] Genotypes are 1/4 AA, 1/2 Aa, 1/4 aa; phenotypes are 3/4 dominant and 1/4 recessive.", "[2] These are probabilities across many independent outcomes; chance can make a small family depart from 3:1."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q1"}, {"title": "X-linked risk", "prompt": "In the stipulated XX/XY model, cross carrier XᴬXᵃ with unaffected XᴬY. Give the probability an offspring is affected and the probability a son is affected.", "steps": ["[2] Outcomes are XᴬXᴬ, XᴬXᵃ, XᴬY and XᵃY, each with probability 1/4.", "[2] An affected offspring is XᵃY: probability 1/4 of all offspring.", "[2] Conditional on being a son, probability is 1/2; father supplies Y to sons, so this is not father-to-son transmission of his X allele."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q2"}, {"title": "ABO probabilities", "prompt": "Cross Iᴬi × Iᴮi in the simplified ABO model. Give all offspring genotypes and phenotypes and identify dominance relationships.", "steps": ["[2] Outcomes IᴬIᴮ, Iᴬi, Iᴮi and ii each have probability 1/4.", "[2] Phenotypes are AB, A, B and O respectively.", "[2] Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant with each other and each is dominant to i; there are multiple alleles in the population but two at this locus in each diploid individual."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q3"}, {"title": "Test cross", "prompt": "A dominant-phenotype organism could be AA or Aa. Explain a test cross with aa and interpret the production of one recessive offspring.", "steps": ["[2] Cross the unknown with homozygous recessive aa.", "[2] A recessive offspring requires a from both parents, establishing the unknown carries a and is Aa under this model.", "[2] Aa predicts 1:1 dominant:recessive; all observed dominant offspring in a small sample does not conclusively prove AA."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q4"}, {"title": "Incomplete dominance", "prompt": "For a locus with RR red, Rr pink and rr white, cross two pink individuals. Contrast the genotype and phenotype ratios with complete dominance.", "steps": ["[2] Rr × Rr gives 1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr.", "[2] Phenotypes are 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white because heterozygotes have the stipulated intermediate phenotype.", "[1] With complete dominance of R, the same genotype ratio gives a 3:1 phenotype ratio instead."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q5"}, {"title": "Pedigree reasoning", "prompt": "Two unaffected parents have an affected child. Under a simple fully penetrant autosomal recessive model with no new mutation, infer parental genotypes and explain one model limitation.", "steps": ["[2] The affected child is aa and must receive a from each parent.", "[2] Each unaffected parent must therefore be Aa, with A preventing the recessive phenotype under the stated model.", "[1] Real pedigrees may involve penetrance, new mutations or a different inheritance model, so the inference depends on assumptions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q6"}, {"title": "Inheritance comparison", "prompt": "Compare autosomal complete dominance, codominance and X-linked recessive inheritance. Use appropriate crosses and distinguish expected probability from observed small-family ratios. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] For Aa×Aa complete dominance, genotypes are 1:2:1 and phenotypes 3:1, with dominance not implying frequency or strength.", "[2] Codominant heterozygotes show both allele products; IᴬIᴮ expresses AB rather than a blended intermediate.", "[2] Iᴬi×Iᴮi yields A, B, AB and O with expected probability 1/4 each.", "[2] Carrier XᴬXᵃ×XᴬY yields an affected-son outcome with 1/4 overall probability or 1/2 conditional on a son.", "[2] The father supplies Y to sons in the model, so his X-linked allele is not passed father-to-son.", "[2] Ratios describe expected probabilities over many outcomes; small families and model assumptions limit conclusions about inheritance."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inheritance-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "For Aa×Aa under complete dominance, what is recessive phenotype probability?", "o": ["1/4", "1/2", "3/4", "1"], "a": 0, "why": "The aa outcome occupies one of four equally probable combinations; observed small samples can differ from expectation."}, {"q": "What does an affected aa child imply about two unaffected parents under the stated simple recessive model?", "o": ["Both are Aa", "Both must be AA", "Both must be aa", "Neither supplied a"], "a": 0, "why": "Each parent supplied a and each remains unaffected, making both heterozygous under complete penetrance and no new mutation."}, {"q": "Which describes codominance?", "o": ["Both allele products are expressed in a heterozygote", "Every heterozygote is intermediate", "One allele vanishes from DNA", "All alleles are equally common"], "a": 0, "why": "Codominance differs from incomplete dominance; AB is a standard simplified example of co-expression."}, {"q": "A carrier mother and unaffected father have an X-linked-recessive model. What is risk conditional on a son?", "o": ["1/2", "1/4", "3/4", "0"], "a": 0, "why": "Half the sons receive the mother’s affected allele; among all offspring the affected-son outcome is one quarter under the stipulated sex probabilities."}, {"q": "Does a dominant allele have to be common?", "o": ["No, dominance concerns heterozygote phenotype", "Yes, always over 50%", "Yes, always over 90%", "Only if it is on an autosome"], "a": 0, "why": "Dominance describes expression relationships and does not by itself determine population frequency or fitness."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "23 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-technology", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Continuity of species", "title": "Gene technology, DNA identification and biotechnology", "scope": "PCR, electrophoresis, sequencing, profiling, recombinant DNA, transgenic organisms, applications, risks and bioinformatics.", "screens": [{"title": "Different technologies answer different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "DNA amplification, separation, sequencing, profiling and recombinant DNA construction serve related but distinct purposes. PCR increases the amount of a targeted sequence. Gel electrophoresis separates suitable DNA fragments mainly by size under controlled conditions. Sequencing determines base order, while profiling compares selected variable regions to distinguish samples or estimate relatedness. Recombinant DNA combines genetic material from different sources, and a transgenic organism contains introduced genetic material from another source in the relevant context. An exam explanation should state what the method produces and why that output helps answer the question. A gel image does not directly read the whole genome, and making many DNA copies does not by itself identify their base sequence.", "diagram": "genetech"}, {"title": "PCR amplifies a selected target", "kind": "learn", "text": "Polymerase chain reaction uses repeated cycles to amplify a DNA region bounded by primers. Heating separates strands, cooling allows appropriate primers to bind, and a heat-tolerant polymerase extends new strands using nucleotides. Repeated cycles can produce many copies from a small starting amount, under suitable conditions. The chosen primers determine the target, so PCR does not necessarily copy every gene in the sample. Contamination can also be amplified, requiring controls and careful handling. The ideal doubling model gives approximately 2ⁿ copies from one target after n cycles, but actual efficiency can differ and later cycles can plateau. Focus on the application and interpretation rather than memorising every reagent concentration or claiming perfect amplification in every reaction.", "diagram": "pcr"}, {"title": "Gel electrophoresis: charge and size", "kind": "learn", "text": "DNA's phosphate backbone gives it an overall negative charge, so it moves toward the positive electrode in an electric field. A gel matrix impedes larger fragments more strongly, allowing smaller fragments to move farther under the same conditions. A size standard helps estimate fragment lengths, and visible bands represent many DNA molecules rather than one molecule drawn as a thick stripe. Equal positions indicate similar fragment lengths under the assay but do not prove complete sequence identity. Label wells, migration direction, electrodes and the reference ladder when interpreting a gel. Loading amount, staining and resolution can influence the appearance. A missing band can reflect failed preparation or insufficient detection rather than proving the corresponding DNA never existed in the organism.", "diagram": "gel"}, {"title": "DNA sequencing and genome mapping", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sequencing identifies nucleotide order in a DNA region, providing information that can be compared with reference sequences or assembled into a genome. Genome mapping concerns the location and relationships of genetic regions, and sequencing provides increasingly detailed information for that purpose. Technologies vary in how they generate and assemble reads, so the core application should be distinguished from intricate instrument mechanics. Similar sequences can support evolutionary relationships or reveal variants, but interpretation requires alignment, quality assessment and relevant reference data. A difference between sequences may be neutral or functional depending on its location and effect. Sequence data alone do not establish every phenotype because expression, interactions and environment also matter. Explain what evidence is obtained and how it addresses the particular question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "DNA profiling and interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "DNA profiling compares selected variable regions, often repeat-number patterns, to distinguish biological samples or assess relationships. Several loci provide more informative comparisons than one shared band. In a parentage model, a child's alleles at the tested autosomal loci should be explainable by contributions from the biological parents, subject to mutation and technical limitations. A match supports an inference with a probability that depends on the loci and population information; it is not automatically absolute proof of identity. Contamination, mixed samples and handling errors can complicate interpretation. Identical twins ordinarily share very similar inherited profiles at common loci. Describe the evidence and limitations without treating DNA as immune to sampling or analytical error.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recombinant DNA and vectors", "kind": "learn", "text": "A simplified recombinant DNA process identifies a sequence of interest, prepares a vector and inserts the sequence so it can be maintained or expressed in a host. Restriction enzymes can cut DNA at recognised sequences, and DNA ligase can join suitable DNA ends. A plasmid can serve as a vector, with additional features needed for replication, selection and expression in the chosen host. Host cells containing the desired construct must be identified and assessed rather than assuming every exposed cell takes up the sequence successfully. Complementary sticky ends can aid joining, but not every recombinant method uses identical cutting strategies. Explain the role of each component and the application while avoiding an unrequested laboratory recipe or the claim that inserted DNA always produces the intended trait.", "diagram": "recombinant"}, {"title": "Transgenic crops: benefits and non-target effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "Engineered traits can include resistance to particular pests or herbicides, altered growth, improved product quality and tolerance to certain environmental stresses. Potential benefits must be evaluated against evidence for the particular trait and setting, rather than assuming every modified organism increases yield or reduces impacts. Risks can include effects on non-target organisms, changes to agricultural practice and spread of a trait through compatible gene flow. Herbicide tolerance in a crop does not automatically make all neighbouring weeds resistant, but gene flow to related plants or selection among weeds can create management difficulties. Distinguish a crop's engineered mechanism from evolutionary responses in other populations. Discuss likely effects, uncertainties and monitoring requirements, with ecological and social considerations separated from unsupported claims.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Resistance evolution and gene-flow management", "kind": "learn", "text": "A pest population may contain heritable variation affecting survival after a control measure. If resistant individuals leave more offspring, the relevant alleles can increase over generations. An engineered pest-resistant crop can therefore create selection pressures, just as other control practices can. Refuge strategies and varied management can sometimes reduce the rate of resistance evolution, but effectiveness depends on biology and implementation. Gene flow requires a transmission route, such as compatible pollen transfer; physical proximity alone does not establish it. Evaluate risks by identifying the receiving population, reproductive compatibility, spread and ecological consequence. Avoid saying individual pests learn to be genetically resistant because they are exposed, or that mutation occurs specifically to solve the problem imposed by the crop.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Biotechnology in conservation and biosecurity", "kind": "learn", "text": "DNA methods can identify species, monitor threatened populations, estimate relatedness and assess variation in breeding programmes. Environmental DNA can provide evidence that genetic material from a species is present, but persistence, contamination and transport can complicate inference about current abundance or precise location. Genetic screening can help manage captive breeding to retain variation and reduce close-relative pairings. Biosecurity applications include detecting organisms or pathogens in movement pathways, with validated assays and suitable sampling. A negative result from one small sample does not prove a shipment or ecosystem is free of the target. Combine genetic information with biogeography, reproductive behaviour and population dynamics when planning conservation rather than treating sequence data as a complete management plan.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bioinformatics, evidence and ethical judgement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bioinformatics uses organised databases and computational analysis to investigate biological data such as nucleotide and amino acid sequences. The published 2027 syllabus makes this application explicit. Reliable comparisons require suitable data quality, alignment and awareness of sampling and model assumptions. A database can reveal candidate relationships or variants, but conclusions need biological interpretation and corroboration. Biotechnology decisions also involve values, including environmental effects, access, cultural interests, ownership and use of genetic information. Present the intended benefit, relevant evidence, uncertainties and possible adverse consequences. Scientific evidence can constrain claims and inform choices, while not alone deciding every ethical priority. A balanced evaluation is more useful than declaring all gene technology automatically beneficial or harmful.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Selecting a genetic tool", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Selecting a genetic tool</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Tool</th><th scope=\"col\">What it does</th><th scope=\"col\">What it does not prove alone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PCR</td><td>Amplifies a primer-defined target</td><td>A band does not automatically prove a viable organism</td></tr><tr><td>Gel electrophoresis</td><td>Separates appropriate fragments by migration/size</td><td>Equal length is not guaranteed equal sequence</td></tr><tr><td>Sequencing</td><td>Determines base order with quality constraints</td><td>A sequence is not automatically a full phenotype</td></tr><tr><td>DNA profiling</td><td>Compares selected informative markers</td><td>Not absolute identity without appropriate assumptions</td></tr><tr><td>Recombinant DNA</td><td>Joins selected genetic material</td><td>DNA uptake does not alone prove expression</td></tr><tr><td>Bioinformatics</td><td>Analyses database/aligned sequences and relationships</td><td>Computing does not remove poor input or model assumptions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: PCR amplification", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: PCR amplification", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Gel interpretation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Gel interpretation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Recombinant plasmid", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Recombinant plasmid", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Controls in PCR", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Environmental DNA", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Bioinformatics", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Transgenic evaluation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "PCR amplification", "prompt": "Starting with one double-stranded target molecule, calculate ideal copies after five PCR cycles and explain the three cycle stages.", "steps": ["[1] Ideal target copies=2⁵=32.", "[2] Heating separates strands; cooling allows complementary primers to anneal at target boundaries.", "[2] Thermostable polymerase extends from primers using nucleotides.", "[1] Ideal doubling assumes efficient reactions; PCR amplifies the chosen target, not automatically every DNA sequence."], "allocations": [1, 2, 2, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-technology-q1"}, {"title": "Gel interpretation", "prompt": "DNA fragments of 200,500 and 1,000 base pairs are loaded near the negative end of a gel. Order their expected migration distances and explain the role of a ladder.", "steps": ["[2] Negatively charged DNA moves toward the positive electrode.", "[2] Smaller fragments migrate farther in the stated size-separating gel: 200 farthest, then 500, then 1,000.", "[2] A known-size ladder estimates fragment sizes; equal-size bands alone do not prove identical sequences or identity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-technology-q2"}, {"title": "Recombinant plasmid", "prompt": "Explain a simplified route for inserting a gene into a plasmid and obtaining cells that produce its protein.", "steps": ["[2] Obtain the gene and cut/prepare compatible vector and insert sequences using appropriate enzymes or methods.", "[2] DNA ligase joins compatible DNA to form a recombinant plasmid, which is introduced into suitable cells.", "[2] Select and verify successful constructs and ensure expression controls/host machinery support protein production; uptake alone does not prove expression."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-technology-q3"}, {"title": "Controls in PCR", "prompt": "A target band occurs in both a sample and a no-template control. Evaluate the result and recommend a next step.", "steps": ["[2] A no-template control should not contain the target, so the band suggests contamination or another assay artefact.", "[2] The sample cannot be confidently interpreted as a true positive from this run.", "[2] Repeat with clean preparation, appropriate negative/positive controls and verified target specificity rather than ignoring the control."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-technology-q4"}, {"title": "Environmental DNA", "prompt": "A protected wetland water sample contains DNA matching a threatened fish. Explain what this supports and what it cannot prove.", "steps": ["[2] The result supports presence of matching genetic material in the sampled water under a validated assay.", "[2] DNA may persist or move, so it does not by itself prove a live fish at that exact location or estimate abundance.", "[2] Replicate samples, assay controls and independent ecological observations strengthen interpretation; non-detection also need not prove absence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-technology-q5"}, {"title": "Bioinformatics", "prompt": "Explain how aligned DNA or amino-acid sequences from a database can support a phylogenetic inference and give two limitations.", "steps": ["[2] Homologous sequences are aligned and comparable positions assessed to identify shared and differing characters.", "[2] Patterns can support common ancestry and relative relationship, using appropriate models and reference data.", "[2] Gene choice, alignment quality, sampling and variable rates matter; one sequence or raw similarity percentage is not an infallible species history."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-technology-q6"}, {"title": "Transgenic evaluation", "prompt": "Evaluate a proposed insect-resistant crop using gene technology, ecological effects and evidence quality. Explain the production concept and propose monitoring. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Introduce and verify a genetic construct in a suitable crop, with expression of the intended product tested rather than assumed.", "[2] Intended pest effects can reduce damage or some pesticide use, but benefits require field comparison.", "[2] Assess non-target organisms and possible gene flow to compatible populations rather than assuming all weeds can receive the gene.", "[2] Pest populations can evolve resistance through selection of heritable variants; management strategies need evidence and monitoring.", "[2] Use replicated comparison plots measuring yield, pest abundance, non-target effects and environmental variables over time.", "[2] Evaluate uncertainties, local agricultural context, socioeconomic interests and transparent reporting before recommending adoption."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-technology-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What determines a PCR target’s boundaries?", "o": ["Appropriate primer sequences", "Only the gel colour", "Any random microscope lens", "Only ambient humidity"], "a": 0, "why": "Complementary primers flank the intended target and allow extension through that selected region."}, {"q": "Where does negatively charged DNA move in the stated gel?", "o": ["Toward the positive electrode", "Toward the negative electrode", "Only upward against electricity", "No direction under an electric field"], "a": 0, "why": "Electrical charge drives migration; smaller fragments usually travel farther under comparable gel conditions."}, {"q": "What does matching band size alone establish?", "o": ["Similar migration/estimated fragment size", "Identical full sequence for certain", "Identical organisms for certain", "Identical phenotype for certain"], "a": 0, "why": "Different sequences can share length, and profiles require appropriate loci, controls and probabilistic interpretation."}, {"q": "What does ligase do in a simplified recombinant method?", "o": ["Joins compatible DNA fragments", "Always sequences all bases", "Creates every primer from heat", "Denatures all protein permanently"], "a": 0, "why": "Ligase seals DNA backbone joins; restriction/preparation, delivery and verified expression are separate steps."}, {"q": "Why use a no-template PCR control?", "o": ["To detect contamination or assay artefacts", "To prove every sample is positive", "To replace all primers", "To measure an organism’s abundance directly"], "a": 0, "why": "Unexpected target amplification in a negative control undermines confident sample interpretation and requires investigation."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-evidence", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Continuity of species", "title": "Evidence for evolution and reading phylogenies", "scope": "Fossils; anatomy and embryology; genomic and protein comparisons; evolutionary timescales; clades and interpretation.", "screens": [{"title": "Evolution and continuity of life", "kind": "learn", "text": "Life has existed on Earth for approximately 3.5 billion years or longer according to different evidence and estimates, and living lineages have changed and diversified over that time. Evolution describes change in inherited population characteristics across generations, with common ancestry explaining patterns of similarity and diversity. A scientific theory is a well-supported explanatory framework rather than a casual guess. Multiple lines of evidence include fossils, anatomy, development, proteins and genomes. Each has limitations, so converging evidence is especially important. Evolution does not require every species to become more complex or to progress toward humans. Explain the relevant change and evidence at population or lineage level rather than saying an individual organism evolved during its own lifetime.", "diagram": "evolutionevidence"}, {"title": "The fossil record and incomplete preservation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fossils preserve traces or remains of past organisms, providing evidence for changing distributions, extinct groups and sequences of structural features. Preservation is uneven because organisms differ in body structures, environments and likelihood of burial. Soft-bodied organisms and certain habitats can be underrepresented. The absence of a fossil from one sampled layer is therefore not automatically proof of absence of the species everywhere. Stratigraphic relationships and suitable dating methods help establish sequence and age, with uncertainty reported. Transitional features connect groups without requiring every intermediate individual to be preserved. Interpret the record as evidence with biases, rather than rejecting all fossil evidence because it is incomplete or treating a single specimen as a complete history of life.", "diagram": "fossils"}, {"title": "Relative and numerical age evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Relative dating places events or layers in order, using relationships such as superposition where geological disturbance has not overturned them. Numerical dating can estimate ages through suitable physical methods, including radioactive decay in appropriate materials. Not every fossil contains material suitable for every radiometric technique; surrounding volcanic layers can provide bounds. Carbon-14 is useful for relatively recent organic material and cannot date every ancient fossil billions of years old. These details are background for interpreting evolutionary evidence, not a demand to memorise all isotopes. Distinguish an estimated age from a position in a sequence and account for uncertainty. Independent dating information strengthens comparisons across locations when fossils and geological records are evaluated together.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Homologous and analogous structures", "kind": "learn", "text": "Homologous structures reflect shared ancestry, even when their current functions differ. A common skeletal arrangement in vertebrate forelimbs can support descent from an ancestral structure modified in different lineages. Analogous features have similar functions but can arise through independent evolution under similar selection pressures, such as wings in distantly related groups when considered at the appropriate structural level. A feature can have different degrees of homology depending on what is compared: the vertebrate forelimb underlying a wing and the whole flight apparatus are not identical comparison units. State the structure and evidence rather than using function alone to decide ancestry. Convergent evolution explains why ecological similarity can produce resemblance without making the species close relatives.", "diagram": "homology"}, {"title": "Vestigial features and comparative development", "kind": "learn", "text": "Vestigial structures are inherited features reduced or altered relative to their ancestral form or role. They may retain some function, so vestigial does not necessarily mean completely useless. Comparative embryology examines developmental similarities and differences that can reflect inherited developmental systems. Organisms do not literally repeat the adult stages of their evolutionary ancestors during development. Historical textbook diagrams can oversimplify or exaggerate similarities, so use accurate evidence and explain its scope. Developmental and anatomical findings are stronger when consistent with molecular and fossil information. A comparison should name the relevant feature and infer a relationship cautiously rather than claiming every early vertebrate embryo is identical or every shared shape independently proves the exact branching order.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "DNA sequence comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Closely related organisms often have more similar corresponding DNA sequences because they share a more recent common ancestor, although rates vary across genes and lineages. Compare homologous regions with a defensible alignment rather than unrelated fragments of the same length. Insertions, deletions, multiple changes at a site and different mutation rates can affect apparent similarity. A lower number of differences can support closer relationship under the stated model, but one sequence is not automatically a complete species history. Broader genomic evidence and other data improve confidence. In a supplied exercise, calculate differences consistently and identify which pair has the fewest, then explain the inference and its assumptions without inventing a universal percentage that defines every species boundary.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Protein sequences and comparative biochemistry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Corresponding proteins can be compared through their amino acid sequences. Similarity can reflect shared ancestry, while conserved functional requirements can also limit changes in particular regions. Several DNA codons can encode the same amino acid, so DNA sequence changes do not always produce protein changes. Different proteins evolve at different rates and may be informative over different timescales. A table of sequence differences supports a relationship comparison when the same homologous protein is used across organisms. It does not by itself reveal every divergence date. Explain why a chosen marker is suitable, and combine information where possible. Improved sequencing and computational methods have expanded the amount of comparative evidence available rather than removing the need for careful interpretation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Phylogenetic branching, clades and sister groups", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a phylogenetic tree, nodes represent inferred common ancestors and branches represent lineages. Sister groups share an immediate common ancestor in the displayed topology. A clade includes an ancestor and all descendants. Tip order can be rotated around a node without changing relationships, so proximity on the printed page is not a valid relationship measure. Branch lengths show elapsed time or amount of change only when the diagram specifies that meaning. The root indicates an ancestral direction in a rooted tree. Living tips generally represent extant lineages, not a ladder in which one modern species directly turns into the next. Read the node relationships first, then interpret the scale and supporting evidence.", "diagram": "phylogeny"}, {"title": "Building and testing a relationship model", "kind": "learn", "text": "A simple relationship exercise can compare shared derived characters or homologous sequence differences to propose a tree. An outgroup can help infer ancestral versus derived states when chosen appropriately. Conflicting characters or uncertain measurements can leave more than one possible model. Convergent traits, reversals and different gene histories make real inference more complex than one visual resemblance. A model should explain the observations and remain open to revision when additional evidence appears. State whether the evidence is anatomical, protein-based or genomic, and avoid placing unmeasured certainty on branch lengths. The 2027 bioinformatics clarification supports understanding how databases and computational methods organise comparisons while highlighting that output still depends on input data and analytical choices.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluating an evolutionary claim", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose a fictional report says two species must be closest relatives because they both live in deserts and have similar external shapes. Their resemblance may reflect common ancestry or convergence, so examine inherited structural details, corresponding DNA or protein sequences and other evidence. A robust evaluation identifies which observations support the claim, which alternatives remain and what further data would discriminate between them. Fossil and developmental information can add context, but each must be appropriately dated and interpreted. Use the distinction between homology and analogy precisely. An explanation of why an organism is suited to its habitat is different from evidence establishing its exact ancestry. The best response links methods, observations and the limits of the conclusion.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evolutionary evidence", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Evolutionary evidence</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Inference</th><th scope=\"col\">Limit to explain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fossils</td><td>Historical organisms and changes in dated sequences</td><td>Selective preservation and incomplete sampling</td></tr><tr><td>Homology</td><td>Shared inherited structural pattern</td><td>Distinguish analogous function</td></tr><tr><td>Biogeography</td><td>Distribution consistent with ancestry and geological history</td><td>Dispersal and environment also affect patterns</td></tr><tr><td>Molecular sequences</td><td>Comparable inherited differences/shared characters</td><td>Alignment, rate and gene-choice effects</td></tr><tr><td>Embryological patterns</td><td>Shared developmental information</td><td>Not literal replay of all adult ancestors</td></tr><tr><td>Phylogeny</td><td>Common ancestors and clades</td><td>Tip position and unscaled branch length are not time measures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Homology or analogy", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Homology or analogy", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Fossil sequence", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Fossil sequence", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Sequence comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Sequence comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Dating methods", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Tree reading", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Vestigial structures", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Multiple lines of evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Homology or analogy", "prompt": "Compare mammalian forelimb bones with the flight surfaces of a bird and an insect. Explain why the level of comparison matters.", "steps": ["[2] Shared underlying mammalian forelimb bone arrangement is homologous evidence of common ancestry.", "[2] Bird and insect flight surfaces as wings are analogous, having evolved flight in distinct structural lineages.", "[2] Bird forelimb bones can still be homologous to other vertebrate forelimbs; function alone does not decide the evolutionary comparison."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-evidence-q1"}, {"title": "Fossil sequence", "prompt": "A dated sequence contains older fish-like forms and younger forms combining aquatic and terrestrial features. Explain how it can support evolutionary change and give two limitations.", "steps": ["[2] Ordered structural changes and transitional combinations are consistent with descent with modification.", "[2] Preservation is selective and the record is incomplete, especially for soft tissues and unsampled habitats.", "[2] Dating and relationships require independent evidence; a fossil is not automatically the direct ancestor of a particular living species."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-evidence-q2"}, {"title": "Sequence comparison", "prompt": "Three homologous protein sequences differ by 2 residues between A and B and by 18 between A and C over the same aligned region. Interpret relatedness cautiously.", "steps": ["[2] Greater similarity between A and B is consistent with closer common ancestry for this sequence.", "[2] Variable rates, selection and sequence choice can affect differences, so the counts alone do not establish exact divergence times.", "[1] Combine additional genes, structural evidence and a justified phylogenetic model."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-evidence-q3"}, {"title": "Dating methods", "prompt": "Distinguish relative dating from radiometric dating and explain why carbon-14 is not the method for every ancient fossil.", "steps": ["[2] Relative dating places material earlier/later using strata and relationships without necessarily assigning a numerical age.", "[2] Radiometric dating uses appropriate isotope decay and material to estimate ages under stated assumptions.", "[2] Carbon-14 has a limited useful timescale and suits some relatively recent organic material; other systems/materials are needed for much older rocks/fossils."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-evidence-q4"}, {"title": "Tree reading", "prompt": "For topology (outgroup,(A,(B,C))), identify the sister pair and describe what a node and a clade represent.", "steps": ["[1] B and C form the sister pair.", "[2] Their joining node represents their inferred common ancestor.", "[2] A clade contains an ancestor and all its descendants; rotating the B/C branches preserves the relationship."], "allocations": [1, 2, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-evidence-q5"}, {"title": "Vestigial structures", "prompt": "Correct the claims that a vestigial structure must be useless and that embryos repeat every adult ancestral stage.", "steps": ["[2] A vestigial feature is reduced or altered relative to an ancestral form and can retain functions.", "[2] Shared developmental patterns can inform ancestry, but embryos do not literally replay all adult ancestors.", "[1] Evaluate specific comparative evidence instead of treating either claim as a universal law."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-evidence-q6"}, {"title": "Multiple lines of evidence", "prompt": "Explain how fossils, comparative anatomy, molecular sequences and phylogenetic analysis jointly support evolution. Include limitations and distinguish a scientific theory from a guess. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Fossils show organisms and structural changes through dated sequences, with preservation/sampling limits.", "[2] Homologous features support shared ancestry while analogous adaptations warn against using function alone.", "[2] Aligned DNA/protein sequences provide many comparable characters, though rates, gene choice and alignment matter.", "[2] Phylogenetic analyses organise inferred common ancestors and clades, with topology and support more informative than label positions.", "[2] Concordant independent evidence strengthens inference and conflicting results can motivate model revision or additional sampling.", "[2] Evolutionary theory is an evidence-supported explanatory framework for change and ancestry, not simply an unsupported guess or a predetermined ladder of improvement."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-evidence-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What most directly indicates homology?", "o": ["Shared inherited underlying structure", "Identical function alone", "Same environment alone", "Same printed colour"], "a": 0, "why": "Homologous structure supports ancestry; similar function can arise independently through convergence."}, {"q": "What does a tree node represent?", "o": ["An inferred common ancestor", "Always a living tip species", "A guaranteed exact date without scale", "Only a sample size"], "a": 0, "why": "Branching nodes propose common ancestry, and lengths need an explicit scale to represent time or change."}, {"q": "Why is the fossil record incomplete?", "o": ["Preservation and sampling are selective", "Every organism fossilises identically", "All fossils are modern", "No geological evidence exists"], "a": 0, "why": "Soft tissues, environments and sampling differ, so absence from the record does not automatically prove absence of organisms."}, {"q": "Which statement about vestigial features is accurate?", "o": ["They may retain functions", "They must have zero function", "They cannot reflect ancestry", "They prove every ancestor was identical"], "a": 0, "why": "Reduction relative to an ancestral condition does not require total uselessness, and context matters."}, {"q": "Why combine several molecular sequences?", "o": ["One gene may not fully represent species history", "It makes all rates identical", "It removes every modelling assumption", "It proves every topology without analysis"], "a": 0, "why": "Multiple appropriate evidence sources can strengthen inference while acknowledging different gene histories, rates and alignment limitations."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-gene-pools", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Continuity of species", "title": "Natural selection, gene pools, speciation and extinction risk", "scope": "Allele frequency; mutation, drift, founder effects and gene flow; selection; artificial selection; isolation and conservation.", "screens": [{"title": "Gene pools and allele-frequency calculation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A gene pool comprises the alleles present in a reproducing population. Allele frequency is the proportion of allele copies of a particular type at the locus, not simply the proportion of individuals showing a phenotype. In a diploid sample with 20 AA, 30 Aa and 50 aa individuals, there are 200 allele copies: A contributes 40 from AA and 30 from Aa, so its frequency is 70/200 = 0.35. The a frequency is 0.65. Dominance can conceal alleles in phenotypes, so counts of dominant-looking individuals alone may not reveal allele frequency. Define the sampled population and locus, and consider whether the sample represents the whole gene pool. A calculated frequency is an estimate when only part of the population is sampled.", "diagram": "genepool"}, {"title": "Mutation as the origin of new alleles", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mutation introduces new genetic variants and is the ultimate source of new alleles. Recombination, chromosome assortment and fertilisation create new combinations of alleles already present. Mutation can arise without regard to an organism's need, although mutation rates and mechanisms vary with conditions. The effect on fitness depends on environment and genetic context. A variant that is disadvantageous in one setting may be useful in another, while many have little measurable effect. A mutation must enter the reproducing population to contribute to inherited population change. Distinguish creating variation from sorting it: natural selection does not deliberately manufacture the allele that would solve a challenge. Correct explanations state where variation comes from and how its frequency can subsequently change.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Natural selection as differential reproduction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Natural selection requires heritable variation and differences in reproductive success associated with that variation under the environmental conditions. A selection pressure can make a phenotype more effective at surviving to reproduce or producing viable offspring. Individuals carrying relevant alleles may leave more descendants, causing those alleles to become more frequent over generations. Survival matters because of its connection to reproduction, not because the strongest-looking organism must always win. Populations evolve through this frequency change; individuals do not change inherited alleles because they try harder to adapt. State the initial variation, pressure, phenotype advantage, reproductive consequence and generational change. The environment influences which traits are advantageous, so fitness is context-dependent rather than a universal ranking of organisms.", "diagram": "selection"}, {"title": "Directional, stabilising and disruptive patterns", "kind": "learn", "text": "Selection can favour one end of a trait distribution, favour intermediate values or favour different extremes under different conditions. These are commonly termed directional, stabilising and disruptive selection as extension representations of selection outcomes. The patterns describe changes in distributions, not different conscious strategies adopted by organisms. A phenotype distribution can also shift through migration or environmental effects without proving selection, so inherited differences and reproductive evidence matter. A graph should identify the measured trait, distribution and before–after conditions. Explain the selective advantage using the specific scenario rather than saying evolution always improves a trait in one direction. Disruptive selection alone does not guarantee immediate speciation because continuing gene flow can maintain one population.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Genetic drift, bottlenecks and founder effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "Genetic drift changes allele frequencies through chance sampling of reproductive contributions rather than consistent fitness differences. Its effects are generally stronger in small populations. A bottleneck sharply reduces population size and can leave an unrepresentative subset of alleles. A founder effect occurs when a small number of individuals establish a new population with allele frequencies differing from the source through chance. Drift can reduce variation or fix an allele without making it advantageous. A founder population can also face new selection pressures, so mechanisms can operate together. Explain what makes the change random with respect to the trait's fitness. Do not identify every decline in population size as evidence that a particular beneficial allele was selected.", "diagram": "drift"}, {"title": "Gene flow between populations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gene flow occurs when movement of individuals or gametes contributes alleles to another reproducing population. Movement alone is insufficient if immigrants do not contribute to reproduction. Gene flow can introduce variation and reduce genetic differences between connected populations. Its effects depend on the alleles moved, reproductive success and existing population structure. Habitat corridors can help connect populations, but they may also facilitate disease or invasive species, so their value needs context-specific evaluation. Distinguish gene flow from drift and mutation: it transfers alleles rather than necessarily creating new ones or changing frequencies purely through chance. Reduced migration can permit populations to diverge under different selection pressures, but isolation must persist sufficiently to affect reproductive connections.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Allopatric speciation and reproductive isolation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Allopatric speciation can begin when a geographic barrier restricts gene flow between populations. Mutation, drift and differing selection pressures can lead to divergence over generations. If sufficient reproductive isolation develops, the populations may remain distinct even after contact is restored. The barrier alone does not instantly create a new species. Reproductive barriers can act before fertilisation or after it, affecting mating, fertilisation, hybrid survival or fertility. The time and outcome depend on the organisms, variation and environment. Explain the sequence from reduced gene flow to genetic divergence and reproductive isolation. Do not claim a river automatically causes speciation or that every isolated population must eventually become a different species.", "diagram": "speciation"}, {"title": "Microevolution, macroevolution and artificial selection", "kind": "learn", "text": "Microevolution concerns changes in inherited population characteristics, often represented by allele-frequency change within populations. Macroevolution concerns broader patterns such as lineage diversification and extinction over longer scales. These larger patterns reflect accumulated changes and events rather than a separate mechanism that suddenly abandons heredity. Artificial selection intentionally favours reproduction of individuals with desired traits, changing allele frequencies over generations. Selective breeding can improve a chosen characteristic while reducing diversity or increasing relatedness if poorly managed. It is distinct from inserting a gene through recombinant technology. Explain the chosen trait, parental selection and inherited consequences rather than assuming every breeding programme produces an entirely new species or that selection can generate any trait without relevant variation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reduced diversity and extinction risk", "kind": "learn", "text": "Small, genetically restricted populations can be less able to respond to changed conditions and may experience increased mating between relatives. Inbreeding can raise the chance that harmful recessive alleles occur in homozygous combinations, while drift can remove variation. Demographic chance, habitat loss and environmental events create additional risks. Genetic diversity is therefore important but not the only determinant of survival. Conservation planning considers biogeography, reproductive behaviour and population dynamics alongside genetic information. Restoring numbers without restoring habitat or addressing the original threat can fail. A scientifically justified management plan identifies the risk mechanism and uses suitable evidence to select breeding, connectivity or habitat strategies, while monitoring unintended effects and uncertainty.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Testing a resistance explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a fictional bacterial population, a rare inherited variant allows some cells to survive a treatment that kills susceptible cells. The survivors reproduce, and the resistant variant becomes more common. This illustrates selection, not cells developing a useful mutation because the treatment tells them what to do. Mutation and gene transfer can provide variants, while treatment changes their relative reproductive success. The observed population pattern may also depend on migration, dose and other environmental conditions. A complete explanation distinguishes initial variation, selection pressure and change across generations. Resistance belongs to the pathogen population rather than the treated person's body becoming resistant to the medicine. Use this mechanism to connect evolution with disease management without treating an educational model as individual clinical advice.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Mechanisms of allele-frequency change", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Mechanisms of allele-frequency change</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Mechanism</th><th scope=\"col\">Cause</th><th scope=\"col\">Distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mutation</td><td>New inherited sequence variation</td><td>Not directed toward future usefulness</td></tr><tr><td>Natural selection</td><td>Differential reproductive success of heritable variants</td><td>Fitness depends on context</td></tr><tr><td>Genetic drift</td><td>Chance sampling changes</td><td>Especially important in small populations</td></tr><tr><td>Founder/bottleneck effect</td><td>Restricted starting/surviving genetic sample</td><td>Numerical recovery need not restore lost alleles</td></tr><tr><td>Gene flow</td><td>Inherited transfer between populations</td><td>Movement alone is insufficient without contribution</td></tr><tr><td>Recombination</td><td>New combinations of existing alleles</td><td>Not the same as generating every new allele</td></tr><tr><td>Speciation</td><td>Divergence and relevant species isolation</td><td>Geographic separation is a potential start, not instant proof</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Explore the numbers: alleles", "kind": "interactive", "model": "alleles"}, {"title": "Worked question: Allele frequencies", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Allele frequencies", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Selection mechanism", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Selection mechanism", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Founder effect", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Founder effect", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Gene flow", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Allopatric speciation", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Diversity risk", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Evolution and management", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Allele frequencies", "prompt": "A fictional diploid population has 20 AA,30 Aa and 50 aa individuals. Calculate A and a frequencies, showing allele counting.", "steps": ["[2] There are 100 individuals and 200 allele copies at this locus.", "[2] A copies=2×20+30=70, so f(A)=70/200=0.35.", "[2] a copies=2×50+30=130, so f(a)=130/200=0.65; frequencies sum to 1."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q1"}, {"title": "Selection mechanism", "prompt": "After antibiotic exposure, a bacterial population contains a higher fraction of resistant variants. Explain the change without suggesting the drug deliberately causes useful mutations.", "steps": ["[2] Heritable variation in resistance exists or arises without anticipating the drug’s need.", "[2] Susceptible bacteria are less likely to survive/reproduce during exposure than resistant bacteria.", "[2] Resistant descendants contribute disproportionately, increasing resistance frequency; the bacterial population evolves, not the patient becoming resistant."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q2"}, {"title": "Founder effect", "prompt": "Ten birds establish an island population and carry a different allele frequency from the mainland. Explain a founder effect and distinguish it from natural selection.", "steps": ["[2] The founders are a small sample whose allele frequencies can differ by chance.", "[2] Their descendants begin from this restricted genetic sample: a form of genetic drift.", "[2] Selection requires differential reproductive success associated with heritable traits; frequency change alone does not establish that mechanism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q3"}, {"title": "Gene flow", "prompt": "Birds move between two reserves but do not breed after migration. Explain why movement alone does not demonstrate gene flow.", "steps": ["[2] Gene flow involves transfer of alleles between populations through successful reproduction or equivalent genetic exchange.", "[2] Migrants must contribute alleles to the recipient gene pool; mere temporary presence need not do so.", "[1] Breeding/genetic evidence is needed to establish the transfer."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q4"}, {"title": "Allopatric speciation", "prompt": "Explain why a new river separating a population is not immediate proof that two species have formed.", "steps": ["[2] Separation can reduce gene flow and permit genetic divergence through mutation, drift and selection.", "[2] Divergence must lead to relevant reproductive isolation under the species concept; a geographic barrier alone does not establish it.", "[2] Reproductive, genetic and ecological evidence over time is needed; the populations could remain compatible."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q5"}, {"title": "Diversity risk", "prompt": "Explain how a bottleneck can reduce variation and why increasing census size alone may not immediately restore lost alleles.", "steps": ["[2] Few survivors retain only a subset of prior alleles, with losses by chance.", "[2] Their offspring reproduce the restricted pool, so numerical recovery need not restore missing variants.", "[2] Mutation and appropriate gene flow can add variation over time, but effects and risks require evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q6"}, {"title": "Evolution and management", "prompt": "An isolated population declines to 15 individuals and later recovers to 150. Explain possible genetic consequences and evaluate a proposal to introduce animals from another population. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The bottleneck can randomly lose alleles and shift frequencies through drift.", "[2] A small breeding pool can increase related mating and expression of deleterious recessive variants.", "[2] Recovery in numbers does not automatically restore the previous gene pool.", "[2] Successful immigrant breeding can introduce alleles and reduce some inbreeding risks.", "[2] Assess compatibility, local adaptation, disease and ecological impacts rather than treating any immigrant as beneficial.", "[2] Monitor breeding, survival and genetic diversity, with habitat threats addressed and uncertainties reported in an evidence-based conservation plan."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-gene-pools-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "For 10 AA,20 Aa and20 aa individuals, what is f(A)?", "o": ["0.40", "0.20", "0.60", "0.80"], "a": 0, "why": "There are 100 allele copies; A contributes 20+20=40, giving frequency 0.40."}, {"q": "What distinguishes genetic drift?", "o": ["Chance changes in allele frequencies", "Always improved adaptation", "Directed useful mutation", "Individuals changing because they need to"], "a": 0, "why": "Sampling effects can change allele frequencies independently of trait advantage, especially in small populations."}, {"q": "What is required for migrant movement to constitute gene flow?", "o": ["Contribution of alleles through reproduction/exchange", "A photograph at a new site only", "Any temporary visit", "A name change"], "a": 0, "why": "Gene flow changes a recipient gene pool through inherited transfer, rather than mere geographic movement."}, {"q": "Does geographic separation immediately establish speciation?", "o": ["No, relevant reproductive isolation must develop", "Yes, every river makes a new species", "Yes, all isolated organisms lose DNA", "Only if individuals are large"], "a": 0, "why": "Isolation can permit divergence, but reproductive/species-boundary evidence is needed under the chosen concept."}, {"q": "Why can antibiotic resistance frequency rise?", "o": ["Resistant variants leave more descendants during exposure", "The patient’s cells become resistant bacteria", "The drug chooses a useful mutation intentionally", "All susceptible bacteria learn to resist"], "a": 0, "why": "Heritable variation and differential reproductive success explain population change without teleological mutation."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "23 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-homeostasis", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Surviving in a changing environment", "title": "Homeostasis, feedback and thermoregulation", "scope": "Stimulus–response coordination; negative feedback; heat exchange; endotherms and ectotherms; structural, behavioural and physiological responses.", "screens": [{"title": "A relatively stable internal environment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Homeostasis maintains internal conditions within ranges compatible with cellular function despite external or internal changes. It involves dynamic regulation, not an unchanging value for every variable. Temperature, water balance and solute concentrations affect enzyme function, membranes and metabolic processes. A variable can fluctuate around a suitable range while coordinated responses oppose harmful departures. The organism exchanges matter and energy with the environment, so maintaining stability requires ongoing processes. A set point or operating range can vary with circumstances rather than being identical at every time. Explain why the regulated condition matters to cells, and distinguish a temporary corrective response from an evolutionary adaptation inherited across generations.", "diagram": "homeostasis"}, {"title": "The stimulus–response model", "kind": "learn", "text": "A stimulus is a detectable change, a receptor detects it, a coordination or control component processes information, and effectors produce a response. Negative feedback occurs when the response opposes the initial change in the regulated variable, reducing the stimulus to further response. For a rising temperature, suitable detection can activate heat-loss mechanisms that reduce the temperature rise. The pathway can involve nervous signals, hormones or local cellular mechanisms depending on the organism and variable. Identify the actual receptor, effector and response where the question supplies them. A list of words is insufficient unless the links are explained. Feedback need not remove the environmental cause; it regulates the body's response to that continuing influence within physiological limits.", "diagram": "feedback"}, {"title": "Negative versus positive feedback", "kind": "learn", "text": "Negative feedback stabilises a variable by opposing change. Positive feedback amplifies a change within a process, often until a separate event terminates it. Positive feedback can have useful biological roles but should not be treated as the ordinary explanation for maintaining a stable internal temperature. Negative does not mean harmful and positive does not mean beneficial; the terms describe the direction of feedback. A change may also be anticipated before a variable departs greatly from its range, but the core syllabus model emphasises detection and response. Explain the direction of the response relative to the stimulus instead of using the label as the whole answer. Regulatory capacity is limited, so extreme conditions can overwhelm otherwise effective homeostatic mechanisms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Nervous and hormonal coordination", "kind": "learn", "text": "Nervous signalling can convey information rapidly through electrical and chemical activity in pathways, while endocrine signalling releases hormones that affect target cells with suitable receptors. Hormones can influence metabolic or transport processes, and their effects depend on concentration, receptors and cellular responses. The systems interact rather than operating as mutually exclusive alternatives. In a temperature response, neural coordination can influence behaviour, blood flow and muscular activity. Water-balance regulation can include hormonal effects on kidney permeability. A target-cell response does not occur simply because a hormone passes nearby; recognition and signal processing are needed. These examples help interpret the stimulus–response model, but the syllabus does not require every human endocrine pathway to be memorised as a complete Human Biology course.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Heat exchange with the environment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat can transfer by conduction through contact, convection through moving fluid, radiation and evaporation. Conduction and convection can add or remove heat depending on the temperature difference. Radiation exchanges energy with surrounding surfaces, while evaporation removes heat when liquid water becomes vapour at the surface. Sweating is effective only when sufficient evaporation occurs, and high humidity can reduce that effectiveness. A warm environment can therefore challenge regulation even when an animal produces sweat. Identify the physical process and direction under the stated conditions. Insulation reduces heat transfer but does not itself generate heat. A behavioural change such as entering shade reduces radiation exposure, whereas changing skin blood flow alters the pathway for heat transfer from internal tissues.", "diagram": "heatexchange"}, {"title": "Endothermy and ectothermy", "kind": "learn", "text": "Endotherms derive a substantial part of the heat regulating body temperature from internal metabolic processes, while ectotherms depend more strongly on environmental heat sources. These terms describe the main heat source, not a guarantee of constant or highly variable temperature. Ectotherms can regulate temperature behaviourally through basking or shade seeking, and endotherms can allow variation under some conditions. Endothermy commonly requires higher food energy to support heat production, while ectothermy can reduce that metabolic demand. Neither strategy is universally superior. Compare the energy source, environmental dependence and resulting trade-offs in the particular habitat. Avoid the imprecise idea that reptiles necessarily have cold blood or that an endotherm cannot become dangerously overheated or cold.", "diagram": "thermoregulation"}, {"title": "Structural adaptations and surface area", "kind": "learn", "text": "Insulating fur, feathers and fat can reduce unwanted heat transfer, while body shape and appendage size influence surface area relative to volume. Larger similarly shaped bodies generally have lower surface-area-to-volume ratios, slowing exchange relative to their heat-containing mass. Thin or extensive appendages can provide heat-exchange surfaces when blood flow permits it. These are tendencies with context-specific exceptions, not absolute rules for every animal. A structural adaptation is a physical feature, whereas moving into a burrow is a behaviour and vasodilation is a physiological response. State the category requested by the question. Explain how the feature changes a transfer pathway rather than assuming any visible feature is automatically an adaptation for temperature.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Physiological responses to heat and cold", "kind": "learn", "text": "In appropriate endotherms, vasodilation increases skin blood flow and can increase heat transfer to a cooler environment. Vasoconstriction reduces that pathway during cold exposure. Sweating or panting can increase evaporative loss in species with those mechanisms, while shivering increases metabolic heat production through muscular activity. These responses have costs, including water loss or energy demand. The actual effect depends on environmental gradients, humidity and the organism's physiology. If the surrounding air is hotter than the skin, increased exposed circulation does not automatically produce net convective cooling. Explain the mechanism and conditions. A physiological response changes body activity and should be distinguished from inherited body structure or a deliberate change in location.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Behavioural responses and trade-offs", "kind": "learn", "text": "Animals can alter posture, activity time, habitat use or social grouping to influence heat exchange. Basking increases access to environmental radiation, seeking shade reduces solar gain and using burrows can provide a more stable microclimate. Nocturnal activity can avoid daytime heat, while huddling reduces exposed surface and heat loss in some groups. These behaviours can conflict with feeding, predator avoidance or reproductive opportunities. A prediction should therefore consider the animal's other requirements, not assume it can always choose an ideal temperature without cost. Explain why the behaviour changes exposure or exchange. The behaviour is a short-term action enabled by the organism's biology, whereas the evolution of that capacity involves inherited variation and selection across generations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explaining and evaluating a response curve", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fictional endotherm maintains a fairly stable core temperature across a moderate range of ambient temperatures. At colder values its metabolic rate rises, while at hotter values evaporative water loss increases. The flat temperature region reflects effective regulation, not absence of heat exchange. The changing metabolic and water-loss measures show the costs of maintaining that temperature. Beyond tolerance limits, regulation can fail and core temperature changes more strongly. Interpret each axis and use the numerical evidence supplied. A graph does not alone identify all receptors or mechanisms, so connect it to relevant biology while stating any assumptions. Do not infer that a stable core temperature means the organism uses the same amount of energy at every external temperature.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Thermal responses", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Thermal responses</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Category</th><th scope=\"col\">Example</th><th scope=\"col\">Mechanism/trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structural</td><td>Fur, fat, body dimensions</td><td>Insulation and area/volume affect heat exchange</td></tr><tr><td>Physiological</td><td>Vasoconstriction/vasodilation</td><td>Blood flow changes heat transfer to the surface</td></tr><tr><td>Physiological</td><td>Shivering/metabolic heat</td><td>Uses substrates to generate heat</td></tr><tr><td>Physiological</td><td>Sweating/panting</td><td>Evaporation cools where gradient permits; costs water</td></tr><tr><td>Behavioural</td><td>Shade, basking, burrowing, activity timing</td><td>Changes exposure with feeding/predation trade-offs</td></tr><tr><td>Feedback</td><td>Receptor → coordination → effector → response</td><td>Negative loop opposes deviation; range is dynamic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Negative feedback", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Negative feedback", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Humidity trade-off", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Humidity trade-off", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Endotherm or ectotherm", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Endotherm or ectotherm", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Surface-area adaptation", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Structure versus physiology", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Positive feedback", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Compare thermal survival", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Negative feedback", "prompt": "An endotherm becomes warmer than its regulated range. Trace a negative-feedback response using stimulus, receptor, coordination, effector and response.", "steps": ["[1] Increased body temperature is the stimulus detected by temperature-sensitive receptors.", "[2] Coordinating centres receive information and activate relevant effectors.", "[2] Vasodilation and appropriate evaporation increase heat loss, opposing the temperature rise.", "[1] As the deviation decreases, the corrective drive reduces; negative means opposition to the change, not harmful effect."], "allocations": [1, 2, 2, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q1"}, {"title": "Humidity trade-off", "prompt": "Explain why sweating may cool less effectively at high humidity even if sweat production continues.", "steps": ["[2] Evaporation transfers heat from the body to water vapour.", "[2] High humidity reduces the vapour gradient and limits evaporation.", "[1] Sweat that drips away without evaporating contributes less cooling, and water loss remains a cost."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q2"}, {"title": "Endotherm or ectotherm", "prompt": "Distinguish endothermy from ectothermy and explain why a basking reptile may maintain a fairly stable temperature for part of a day.", "steps": ["[2] Endotherms rely substantially on internal metabolic heat; ectotherms rely more on environmental heat exchange.", "[2] Basking and shade seeking can regulate heat gain/loss behaviourally.", "[1] The terms refer to dominant heat source, not a rule that one temperature is always constant and the other always variable."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q3"}, {"title": "Surface-area adaptation", "prompt": "Compare the heat-exchange consequences of a small compact body and a larger similarly shaped body, with other factors equal.", "steps": ["[2] The smaller body has more surface area per volume and potentially faster heat exchange relative to its mass.", "[2] The larger body has lower SA:V and can retain heat relatively better under the stated comparison.", "[1] Insulation, circulation, posture and environment can alter the actual outcome."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q4"}, {"title": "Structure versus physiology", "prompt": "Classify fur, vasoconstriction and moving into a burrow, explaining how each can reduce heat loss or excessive heat gain in an appropriate context.", "steps": ["[2] Fur is structural insulation that reduces heat transfer in suitable conditions.", "[2] Vasoconstriction is physiological reduction of skin blood flow, limiting peripheral heat transfer.", "[2] Burrowing is behavioural selection of a buffered microclimate, affecting exposure rather than creating metabolic heat itself."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q5"}, {"title": "Positive feedback", "prompt": "Contrast negative and positive feedback using body temperature and an amplifying physiological process. Explain why positive is not a synonym for beneficial.", "steps": ["[2] Negative feedback opposes the initiating deviation, such as increased heat loss after a temperature rise.", "[2] Positive feedback amplifies an initiating process, such as contractions promoting signals for stronger contractions until delivery ends the loop.", "[1] Labels describe direction of feedback rather than whether the outcome is good or bad."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q6"}, {"title": "Compare thermal survival", "prompt": "Compare an endotherm and an ectotherm facing a cool night followed by a hot dry day. Explain structural, physiological and behavioural responses and trade-offs. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] An endotherm can increase metabolic heat production in cold conditions but needs energy substrate and may lose water during cooling.", "[2] Insulation and reduced exposed area can limit heat loss, while circulation alters heat transfer.", "[2] An ectotherm depends more on external heat and may bask, seek shade or use a burrow to regulate exposure.", "[2] Evaporative cooling can remove heat where effective but costs water and is limited by humidity.", "[2] Both can alter activity/posture or microhabitat, with feeding and predator-exposure trade-offs.", "[2] Outcomes depend on species and environmental gradients; heat-source category alone does not determine a fixed body temperature."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-homeostasis-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What defines negative feedback?", "o": ["A response opposing the initiating deviation", "A harmful response only", "A response that always amplifies change", "Any process without receptors"], "a": 0, "why": "Negative describes the direction of correction relative to the deviation, not whether the outcome is undesirable."}, {"q": "What primarily distinguishes endothermy?", "o": ["Substantial internal metabolic heat production", "Guaranteed constant temperature at all times", "Always living in a hot place", "No environmental heat exchange"], "a": 0, "why": "Heat-source dependence defines the category; both endotherms and ectotherms interact with their environment."}, {"q": "Which heat-transfer process underlies effective sweating?", "o": ["Evaporation", "DNA replication", "Nitrogen fixation", "Only mass accumulation"], "a": 0, "why": "Water evaporation consumes heat from the surface; high humidity can reduce its effectiveness."}, {"q": "What can vasoconstriction reduce in cool conditions?", "o": ["Peripheral blood flow and heat transfer", "Every metabolic reaction", "The number of inherited alleles", "All need for insulation"], "a": 0, "why": "Reduced skin perfusion can limit heat transfer to the environment while other responses also matter."}, {"q": "Which is a behavioural response?", "o": ["Seeking a shaded burrow", "Growing fur as a structure", "Sweat-gland secretion alone", "Enzyme denaturation"], "a": 0, "why": "Behaviour changes exposure or activity, whereas anatomical structures and physiological responses are distinct categories."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-water-balance", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Surviving in a changing environment", "title": "Animal water, salts, excretion and environmental adaptations", "scope": "Osmoregulation; ammonia, urea and uric acid; kidney principles and ADH; freshwater, marine and terrestrial strategies.", "screens": [{"title": "Osmoregulation and water budgets", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water balance depends on inputs and losses through drinking, food, metabolism, urine, faeces, respiration and body surfaces. Salt balance depends on uptake, storage and removal of ions. Osmoregulation coordinates these processes to maintain conditions suitable for cells. A stable total body mass does not necessarily prove stable internal solute concentrations, and conserving water is not simply refusing all excretion. Different environments impose different osmotic challenges. Use a water budget to identify the largest loss pathway and the mechanism a proposed adaptation could change. Water generated by metabolic reactions can contribute to the budget, but its production also depends on substrate processing and may accompany respiratory water loss.", "diagram": "waterbudget"}, {"title": "Nitrogenous waste and the water trade-off", "kind": "learn", "text": "Breakdown of excess amino acids produces nitrogen-containing waste that must be removed or transformed. Ammonia is toxic and commonly needs substantial water for dilution and removal. Urea is less toxic and can be excreted in more concentrated form, while uric acid can conserve considerable water but requires greater energy investment. Many aquatic animals mainly release ammonia, many mammals mainly excrete urea, and birds and reptiles commonly excrete uric acid, with exceptions and context-dependent variation. The relationship reflects water availability, toxicity, energy and development rather than one universal rule. Explain why conserving water can favour a less soluble or less toxic form and acknowledge its cost. Nitrogenous waste removal differs from egestion of undigested food.", "diagram": "nitrogenwaste"}, {"title": "Kidney filtration and selective processing", "kind": "learn", "text": "A mammalian nephron provides a useful example of regulated waste removal. Pressure-driven filtration transfers water and small dissolved substances from blood into the initial filtrate while retaining blood cells and most large proteins. Subsequent processes selectively reabsorb useful substances and water, while secretion can add particular substances to the tubule. The final urine therefore differs substantially from the initial filtrate. The kidney does not merely strain out poisons while leaving everything useful automatically untouched. Blood supply, transport processes and tubular permeability help determine the outcome. This is an explanatory example for osmoregulation, rather than a requirement to memorise every detailed nephron transporter. Connect the processes to conservation of water and appropriate removal of nitrogenous waste and salts.", "diagram": "nephron"}, {"title": "ADH and a negative-feedback example", "kind": "learn", "text": "When blood osmotic conditions indicate reduced water availability, detection in the brain can increase release of antidiuretic hormone, also called vasopressin. In a simplified mammalian account, ADH increases water permeability in relevant kidney tubules and collecting ducts, allowing more water reabsorption when an appropriate gradient exists. Urine volume can fall and concentration can rise. As the osmotic disturbance is reduced, the signal for increased ADH is reduced, illustrating negative feedback. ADH does not create new water or remove the need for water intake. Several signals and mechanisms interact in actual regulation, so this model focuses on the central causal sequence. State the stimulus, hormonal change, tissue effect and consequence rather than saying a hormone tells the body to keep everything.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Freshwater animals and osmotic gain", "kind": "learn", "text": "A freshwater bony fish is commonly more concentrated in effective solutes than its surrounding water. Water tends to enter through permeable surfaces, while ions can be lost. Typical responses include limited drinking, production of relatively large volumes of dilute urine and active uptake of ions through specialised gill cells. These processes balance net tendencies rather than stopping all molecular movement. The gills provide both gas exchange and routes for osmotic interaction, illustrating a structural trade-off. Explain the direction of water movement first, then connect the excretory and ion-transport responses. Avoid assuming an animal in abundant water cannot face a water-balance challenge or that all aquatic animals use the same strategy regardless of salinity and internal chemistry.", "diagram": "fishwater"}, {"title": "Marine bony fish and osmotic loss", "kind": "learn", "text": "A typical marine bony fish has body fluids less concentrated than seawater, so it tends to lose water and gain salts across relevant surfaces. It commonly drinks seawater, absorbs water through the gut and removes excess ions through specialised gill processes and excretory routes, with relatively limited urine production. The exact handling differs among ions and species. Sharks and some other marine groups use different osmotic strategies, so a bony-fish model should not be applied indiscriminately to every marine vertebrate. Identify the group and the environment in the question. Compare net water movement, drinking, urine and ion regulation using the same categories rather than saying saltwater animals simply drink less to avoid salt.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Terrestrial water loss and behavioural strategies", "kind": "learn", "text": "Terrestrial animals can lose water through evaporation, breathing and excretion. Behavioural strategies include using humid or sheltered microhabitats, reducing daytime activity and timing feeding or movement to favourable conditions. These can lower exposure to high temperature and dry air but may alter food access or predation risk. A behavioural response is different from producing concentrated urine, which is physiological, or having a relatively impermeable covering, which is structural. The question's category determines which evidence is relevant. Water-saving behaviour works through changing exposure or activity, not by changing the laws of osmosis. Relate the strategy to a particular loss pathway and explain any trade-off rather than listing desert behaviours without connecting them to the water budget.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Structural and physiological water conservation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Less permeable body coverings, reduced exposed area and specialised respiratory structures can lower evaporative losses. Kidneys with suitable concentrating capacity can reduce urinary water loss, and some animals tolerate larger fluctuations in body temperature or water availability under particular conditions. Producing concentrated urine requires appropriate structures and gradients; it is not simply an act of choosing to urinate less. Metabolic water and water-rich food can contribute to intake, with the net benefit depending on associated costs. Distinguish immediate regulation from inherited adaptations shaped through selection. Not every desert animal has the same adaptations, and a feature useful in one setting may have disadvantages elsewhere. Explain how the chosen feature affects a specific flux of water or solute.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparing nitrogenous waste with habitat evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "A supplied table may compare waste type, water needed for excretion and energy cost among organisms. Interpret it as a trade-off rather than a ranking of which waste is best. An aquatic organism can benefit from direct ammonia release when water and exchange surfaces permit rapid dilution, while a terrestrial organism may conserve water by converting ammonia to urea or uric acid. Development within an egg can also influence the suitability of waste accumulation. Quantitative data should be used when available, with units and the basis of comparison made clear. Do not infer the whole animal's physiology from one waste measurement alone, because diet, age and conditions can affect excretion. State the hypothesis supported and the limitations of the evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Designing a comparative osmoregulation study", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fictional study compares water intake and urine concentration in animals under different ambient conditions. Ethical constraints mean the design should use approved observations or existing data rather than harmful dehydration. Control relevant differences such as body mass, activity, diet and measurement interval, and select independent individuals appropriate to the research question. Express loss rates relative to suitable units when comparing different sizes. A higher urine concentration alone does not establish lower total water loss because urine volume and other pathways matter. Measure the required variables and evaluate alternative explanations. A valid conclusion links the result to the mechanism and tested conditions, while avoiding generalisation to every species or an untested extreme environment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparative water and ion regulation", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Comparative water and ion regulation</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Organism/model</th><th scope=\"col\">Challenge</th><th scope=\"col\">Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Water-limited mammal</td><td>Loss exceeds intake; osmotic change</td><td>ADH, renal reabsorption and behaviour reduce losses</td></tr><tr><td>Freshwater bony fish</td><td>Water entry and ion loss</td><td>Dilute urine and active ion uptake</td></tr><tr><td>Marine bony fish</td><td>Water loss and ion gain</td><td>Drinking, ion removal and limited urine; not every marine vertebrate model</td></tr><tr><td>Ammonia excretor</td><td>Toxic soluble nitrogen</td><td>Plentiful water helps elimination; low synthesis cost</td></tr><tr><td>Uric-acid excretor</td><td>Nitrogen elimination with little water</td><td>Higher synthesis cost</td></tr><tr><td>Whole-organism budget</td><td>Drink+food+metabolic gains versus all losses</td><td>Conservation has limits and does not create unlimited water</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: ADH feedback", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: ADH feedback", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Freshwater fish", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Freshwater fish", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Marine bony fish", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Marine bony fish", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Nitrogen costs", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Water budget", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Kidney processes", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Compare water survival", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "ADH feedback", "prompt": "A mammal loses water and blood osmotic concentration rises. Explain an ADH-mediated correction without saying the hormone creates water.", "steps": ["[2] Osmoreceptors detect the osmotic change and coordinating pathways increase ADH release.", "[2] ADH increases appropriate kidney-tubule/collecting-duct water permeability, permitting greater reabsorption.", "[2] Less water is lost in a smaller, more concentrated urine output; correction depends on available water and reduces the initiating deviation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q1"}, {"title": "Freshwater fish", "prompt": "Explain osmotic and ion challenges for a typical freshwater bony fish and two compensating responses.", "steps": ["[2] Body fluids are more concentrated than freshwater, so water tends to enter while ions tend to be lost.", "[2] Excretion of substantial dilute urine removes excess water.", "[2] Active ion uptake at gills and other conservation processes replace or retain needed ions; passive water entry is not an ATP water pump."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q2"}, {"title": "Marine bony fish", "prompt": "Compare a typical marine bony fish with a freshwater bony fish, noting the scope of the model.", "steps": ["[2] A marine bony fish generally loses water osmotically to saltier surroundings and gains excess salts.", "[2] Drinking and ion removal help replace water and control salt, with relatively limited urine output.", "[2] A freshwater bony fish faces the opposite net water challenge; sharks and other organisms need not follow the same bony-fish model."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q3"}, {"title": "Nitrogen costs", "prompt": "Explain why uric-acid excretion can help an animal in a dry habitat but does not represent a cost-free improvement over ammonia.", "steps": ["[2] Uric acid can be eliminated with little water, reducing excretory water loss.", "[2] Its synthesis has a higher energetic cost than direct ammonia elimination.", "[1] The advantage depends on water availability, energy and the organism’s biology rather than a universal ranking of superiority."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q4"}, {"title": "Water budget", "prompt": "An animal gains 40 units from drinking,20 from food and10 from metabolism, while losing35 in evaporation,25 in urine and15 in faeces. Calculate its balance and identify two possible compensations.", "steps": ["[2] Gains=70 and losses=75 units, so net balance=−5 units.", "[2] Increasing appropriate water intake or reducing evaporative exposure can improve balance.", "[2] Physiological reduction of urine water loss can help within limits; the animal cannot conserve indefinitely without replacing necessary losses."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q5"}, {"title": "Kidney processes", "prompt": "Distinguish filtration, reabsorption and secretion and explain why glucose in a filtrate need not all appear in urine.", "steps": ["[2] Filtration transfers selected components from blood into the nephron filtrate.", "[2] Reabsorption returns substances from filtrate to blood; normally much filtered glucose is reabsorbed.", "[2] Secretion adds selected substances from blood/tissues into the tubule; final urine reflects all three processes, within transport limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q6"}, {"title": "Compare water survival", "prompt": "Compare mammalian water conservation, freshwater bony-fish osmoregulation and a terrestrial uric-acid excretor. Link environmental challenge to mechanism and trade-off. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A mammal facing water shortage can increase ADH-mediated renal water reabsorption and produce more concentrated urine.", "[2] Behavioural avoidance of heat/activity timing can reduce evaporation, with feeding/activity costs.", "[2] Freshwater bony fish gain water osmotically and lose ions, so dilute urine and active ion uptake address different parts of the challenge.", "[2] Uric-acid excretion reduces water requirement but costs more energy than ammonia elimination.", "[2] Water gains include drinking, food and metabolic production, and all routes of loss must be considered.", "[2] Adaptations have finite limits and differ among species; mechanisms conserve or replace water rather than creating unlimited water or abolishing all ion exchange."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-water-balance-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What does increased ADH normally promote in the simplified mammalian model?", "o": ["Greater renal water reabsorption", "Creation of new water atoms", "Always more dilute urine", "Complete absence of filtration"], "a": 0, "why": "Increased appropriate tubule permeability permits more water return to blood, reducing water loss in urine."}, {"q": "What is a freshwater bony fish’s net osmotic challenge?", "o": ["Water entry and ion loss", "Water loss only and no ion movement", "No exchange at all", "Identical to every shark"], "a": 0, "why": "Body fluids are generally more concentrated than freshwater, requiring removal of excess water and replacement/conservation of ions."}, {"q": "Why is uric acid helpful in dry conditions?", "o": ["Elimination uses little water", "It costs no energy", "It is always more toxic than ammonia", "It makes water intake unnecessary"], "a": 0, "why": "The water-saving benefit has an energetic cost and does not eliminate every route of water loss."}, {"q": "Which process returns filtered substances to blood?", "o": ["Reabsorption", "Filtration into the tubule", "Egestion", "Transcription"], "a": 0, "why": "Reabsorption acts from filtrate toward blood, while filtration and secretion have different directions/roles."}, {"q": "Which gain can contribute to an animal’s water budget?", "o": ["Water formed in metabolism", "Only sunlight entering the skin", "Only salt intake", "Only chromosome separation"], "a": 0, "why": "Metabolic water can contribute alongside food/drinking, but whole-organism net balance includes all losses and costs."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-plant-survival", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Surviving in a changing environment", "title": "Xerophytes, halophytes and plant survival", "scope": "Water-potential challenges; leaf and root adaptations; stomatal regulation; salt tolerance; physiological trade-offs and investigations.", "screens": [{"title": "Plant water stress and environmental gradients", "kind": "learn", "text": "Plants face a balance between acquiring water, maintaining internal conditions and exchanging gases. Water uptake depends on favourable water-potential relationships between soil and plant, while evaporative loss depends on leaf conditions and the atmosphere. Dry soil can limit supply, and saline soil can lower water potential even when physically wet. High temperature, low humidity and wind can raise water demand or alter stomatal behaviour. A xerophyte is adapted to relatively dry conditions, while a halophyte tolerates saline conditions; these categories describe environmental adaptations and can overlap. Explain the particular challenge before listing features. A thick leaf in saline ground and a deep root in dry ground solve different parts of the problem.", "diagram": "plantstress"}, {"title": "Cuticles, leaf area and surface structures", "kind": "learn", "text": "A thick waxy cuticle can reduce water loss through the leaf surface. Reduced leaf area, leaves modified into spines or seasonal leaf shedding can reduce evaporative exposure, while also affecting light capture and growth. Hairs or rolled leaves can influence the boundary layer and shelter stomata in humid air. Sunken stomata can reduce exposure to moving dry air under appropriate conditions. These structures do not prevent all transpiration, and their effects depend on anatomy and environment. Label the structure and explain how it changes the loss pathway. Do not describe all water-saving structures as stomata closing: closing is a physiological response, whereas the location of a pore or thickness of the cuticle is structural.", "diagram": "xerophyte"}, {"title": "Root distribution and access to water", "kind": "learn", "text": "Deep root systems can reach water below drying surface layers, while extensive shallow systems can capture brief surface rainfall over a broad area. Different arrangements may suit different soils, rainfall patterns and groundwater conditions. Root hair surface and membrane transport contribute to uptake, but having more roots does not guarantee uptake from soil with an unfavourable water potential. A structural root adaptation improves access or contact, while uptake still depends on gradients and tissue function. Compare the strategy with the water source in the scenario rather than assuming every desert plant must have deeper roots than every other plant. Root construction and maintenance also require resources, creating trade-offs with above-ground growth and reproduction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Stomatal closure and carbon uptake", "kind": "learn", "text": "Guard-cell changes regulate stomatal opening. When water availability declines, signals can reduce aperture and lower transpiration, helping conserve water. However, closure also restricts carbon dioxide entry and can limit photosynthesis. The plant therefore balances water conservation with carbon acquisition rather than maximising either indefinitely. Stomatal responses interact with light, humidity and internal conditions, and their pattern differs among species. A fall in photosynthetic rate under drought may arise through reduced carbon dioxide supply as well as other cellular effects. Explain the sequence from environmental condition to aperture change, water loss and photosynthetic consequence. Do not equate a completely closed pore with immediate death or claim closure leaves carbon fixation completely unaffected.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Water storage and osmotic adjustment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Succulent tissues can store water for later use, supported by structures that maintain storage and reduce loss. Osmotic adjustment can involve accumulating appropriate solutes that help maintain favourable water relations without excessively disrupting cellular function. Both strategies have limits and costs. Storage is a structural and physiological capacity, while simply being located in shade is a different strategy. A plant can contain a large water store yet still need to manage stomatal loss and salt concentrations. Explain how the stored water or solute conditions support turgor and function during a shortage. Avoid assuming a high total solute concentration always benefits the plant, because excessive or inappropriate ions can damage proteins and alter metabolic processes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "CAM as an extension example", "kind": "learn", "text": "Crassulacean acid metabolism, or CAM, provides an extension example of separating some carbon acquisition from the hottest part of the day. Many CAM plants open stomata at night and initially store carbon in organic acids, then use released carbon dioxide during the day while limiting stomatal opening. The light-dependent reactions still require light, so the plant does not perform all photosynthesis only at night. The strategy can improve water-use efficiency under suitable conditions but has limitations and is not a universal feature of xerophytes. Use it when a scenario or supplied data identifies that mechanism, rather than adding it automatically to every dry-habitat answer. Core responses should still explain the requested structural or physiological adaptation clearly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Salt exclusion and salt removal", "kind": "learn", "text": "Halophytes can maintain function in saline environments through several combinations of mechanisms. Some restrict salt entry at roots, some compartmentalise ions within tissues, and some have specialised structures for salt removal. Different species use different arrangements, so the presence of salt glands should not be assumed for every salt-tolerant plant. Excluding ions can reduce harmful internal accumulation, while secreting them requires appropriate transport and surfaces. The osmotic problem remains important because salty surroundings can make water uptake difficult. A complete explanation connects salt handling with water balance and cellular tolerance. A visible salt deposit can support a hypothesis of secretion, but controls are needed to distinguish it from salt left by external spray.", "diagram": "halophyte"}, {"title": "Compartmentalisation and leaf shedding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Compartmentalising salts in vacuoles can help limit high concentrations in sensitive parts of the cytoplasm while supporting osmotic conditions. Some plants accumulate salt in tissues that are later shed, reducing the retained load. These processes require transport, growth or replacement and therefore have resource costs. A plant that tolerates salt does not necessarily keep all internal fluids free of ions. Distinguish exclusion, secretion and storage as separate mechanisms, and link the claimed adaptation to the evidence. A comparison between young and old leaves may indicate different salt handling, but age, transpiration and exposure can also affect concentrations. The strongest conclusion combines measurements with an explicit biological explanation and acknowledges alternatives.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Water balance and gas exchange together", "kind": "learn", "text": "A xerophyte with a thick cuticle and sheltered stomata can reduce water loss while preserving some carbon dioxide access. A halophyte may combine such features with salt handling because both environmental dryness and salinity can lower effective water availability. Closing stomata can conserve water but reduce photosynthetic inputs, and concentrating solutes can support uptake while risking toxicity if poorly controlled. The adaptations therefore form an interacting system. When asked to discuss survival, include both the benefit and the trade-off, with distinct structural and physiological examples. Avoid repeating three versions of the same feature as if they were independent adaptations. Identify the water pathway, gas pathway and relevant cellular conditions explicitly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Investigating salinity and plant responses", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fictional experiment grows similar seedlings at several salt concentrations while controlling light, temperature, water volume, soil and starting size. Measure growth, survival, leaf water status or other defined outcomes with independent replicate plants and adequate time. Keep nutrient conditions comparable so differences are not simply due to a changed supply of essential ions. An apparent optimum in a salt-tolerant species does not establish that all plants need high salinity. A reduced growth rate can reflect osmotic effects, ion toxicity or other limitations, so identify what the measurement alone can show. Evaluate the evidence with clear units, treatment comparisons and relevant uncertainty. Use ethically appropriate plants and avoid transferring organisms or contaminated soil into natural habitats.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Dryness and salinity adaptations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Dryness and salinity adaptations</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Feature/process</th><th scope=\"col\">Mechanism</th><th scope=\"col\">Limit or trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thick cuticle/reduced leaves</td><td>Reduce evaporative pathway/area</td><td>Carbon uptake and growth can be affected</td></tr><tr><td>Sunken/rolled/hairy surfaces</td><td>Retain a humid boundary region</td><td>Effect depends on air movement and structure</td></tr><tr><td>Root architecture</td><td>Access particular water distributions</td><td>Deep/shallow advantages depend on site</td></tr><tr><td>Succulence</td><td>Store water</td><td>Finite storage and construction costs</td></tr><tr><td>Stomatal closure</td><td>Reduce vapour diffusion</td><td>Restricts CO₂ entry</td></tr><tr><td>Salt exclusion/sequestration/excretion</td><td>Control ion entry or cytosolic concentration</td><td>Species-specific and finite; water-potential challenge can remain</td></tr><tr><td>CAM extension</td><td>Separate initial night CO₂ uptake from daytime carbon use</td><td>Light reactions still require light</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Stomatal trade-off", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Stomatal trade-off", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Salinity challenge", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Salinity challenge", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: CAM model", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: CAM model", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Leaf structures", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Root comparisons", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Succulence and ions", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Salinity investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Stomatal trade-off", "prompt": "Explain why stomatal closure reduces water loss but can restrict photosynthesis.", "steps": ["[2] Closing pores lowers water-vapour diffusion from leaf air spaces to the atmosphere.", "[2] It also limits CO₂ entry required for carbon fixation.", "[1] Survival involves a water–carbon trade-off; closure does not necessarily stop every metabolic process."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q1"}, {"title": "Salinity challenge", "prompt": "Explain how saline soil can cause water stress even when the soil appears wet, including a separate ion-toxicity effect.", "steps": ["[2] Dissolved salts lower external water potential, reducing water uptake or causing net loss under relevant gradients.", "[2] Excess ions can damage cellular processes independently of the water-potential effect.", "[2] Salt exclusion, compartmentalisation or excretion address ion balance; stored water and reduced losses address additional challenges."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q2"}, {"title": "CAM model", "prompt": "For the optional extension model, explain how night-time CO₂ uptake can reduce water loss while light-dependent photosynthesis still requires daytime light.", "steps": ["[2] Opening stomata at cooler/more humid night times can reduce water cost relative to daytime opening.", "[2] CO₂ is initially fixed into stored organic acids and released for daytime carbon fixation.", "[2] Light-dependent reactions still require light; CAM does not mean all photosynthesis occurs only at night."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q3"}, {"title": "Leaf structures", "prompt": "Explain how a thick cuticle and sunken stomata can reduce water loss and give one limitation of inferring function from appearance alone.", "steps": ["[2] A thick cuticle can reduce diffusion across the leaf surface.", "[2] Sunken stomata can retain a more humid local boundary layer, lowering vapour gradients.", "[1] Effects depend on measured properties and conditions; a photograph alone does not quantify transpiration or prove the proposed adaptation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q4"}, {"title": "Root comparisons", "prompt": "Compare deep and widely spreading shallow roots as responses to different water distributions.", "steps": ["[2] Deep roots can access persistent deeper water where available.", "[2] Spreading shallow roots can rapidly capture brief surface rainfall across a wider area.", "[1] Neither is universally better; soil depth, rainfall pattern and competing vegetation influence effectiveness."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q5"}, {"title": "Succulence and ions", "prompt": "Explain why storing water and storing salts in vacuoles are different but potentially complementary survival strategies.", "steps": ["[2] Succulent tissues store water, buffering periods of limited uptake.", "[2] Vacuolar sequestration can reduce harmful ion concentrations in the cytosol while affecting osmotic balance.", "[2] Capacity is finite and species-specific; salt storage does not eliminate all water stress or permit unlimited accumulation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q6"}, {"title": "Salinity investigation", "prompt": "Design an investigation comparing growth of a halophyte and a non-halophyte over a salinity gradient. Include mechanisms, controls and limits of growth evidence. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Use a measured salinity series and define growth response, such as change in dry mass over a stated time.", "[2] Standardise initial stage/size, nutrient supply, light, temperature, soil volume and watering apart from the intended salinity.", "[2] Use independent plants, random allocation and enough replicates for means and variation.", "[2] High salinity can lower water potential and cause ion toxicity, so both are plausible growth mechanisms.", "[2] Halophyte ion exclusion/compartmentalisation/excretion or other relevant responses require separate measurements rather than assumption.", "[2] Compare interaction patterns across species and acknowledge mortality, developmental differences and assay limits; growth alone does not prove which salt-survival mechanism operates."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-plant-survival-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can wet saline soil still limit uptake?", "o": ["Salt lowers soil water potential", "Water has no potential in soil", "Salt always raises root photosynthesis", "All ions become proteins"], "a": 0, "why": "A sufficiently low external water potential reduces favourable water entry and may cause loss, independently of ion toxicity."}, {"q": "What is a stomatal-closure trade-off?", "o": ["Less water loss but restricted CO₂ entry", "More water loss with unlimited carbon uptake", "No gas-exchange effect", "All respiration instantly stops"], "a": 0, "why": "The shared pore serves gas exchange and vapour loss, so regulation trades water conservation against carbon supply."}, {"q": "What does vacuolar salt sequestration help protect?", "o": ["Cytosolic processes from excessive ions", "All leaves from every predator", "DNA from every possible mutation", "The soil from all salts permanently"], "a": 0, "why": "Compartmentalisation can limit harmful cytosolic concentrations but has finite capacity and does not remove every environmental challenge."}, {"q": "When can shallow spreading roots be useful?", "o": ["Brief surface rainfall across a wide area", "Only when no rain ever occurs", "Only in sealed dry rock", "When roots have no absorptive surface"], "a": 0, "why": "Root architecture benefits depend on where and when water is available, not a universal deep-root advantage."}, {"q": "In the CAM extension, when are light-dependent reactions supported?", "o": ["When suitable light is available", "Only in total darkness", "Never in a succulent", "Only while every pore is open"], "a": 0, "why": "CAM separates initial carbon uptake/storage in time, but light reactions still require photons."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-pathogens", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Surviving in a changing environment", "title": "Pathogen groups and prescribed disease examples", "scope": "Bacteria, fungi, protists and viruses; tuberculosis, crown gall, chytrid fungus, malaria, dieback, influenza, Ross River virus and honeybee viruses.", "screens": [{"title": "Infection, disease and host relationships", "kind": "learn", "text": "An infectious disease results from invasion or activity of a pathogen and can be transmitted between hosts through a suitable route. Infection and disease are related but not identical: an organism can carry a pathogen without showing the same severity or symptoms as another host. Non-infectious conditions can arise from inherited, environmental or other causes without pathogen transmission. Pathogen success depends on entry, multiplication or persistence, effects on the host and movement to another suitable host. Most microorganisms are not pathogens, and many contribute to essential ecological processes. Distinguish pathogen, host, vector and reservoir in a scenario. A vector transfers an agent, while a reservoir maintains it in a relevant population or environment.", "diagram": "pathogens"}, {"title": "Bacteria and fungi: structural comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bacteria are prokaryotic cells with membranes, ribosomes and genetic material but no membrane-bound nucleus. Pathogenic bacteria can harm hosts through tissue interactions, toxins or other processes, and not all harmful effects depend on the same mechanism. Fungi are eukaryotic organisms with nuclei and other organelles, often growing as hyphae or yeast forms and commonly having chitin-containing cell walls. Some cause disease by invading tissues or affecting host function. Structural differences can provide targets for selective control, but an effective treatment depends on the organism and conditions. Do not identify fungi as bacteria simply because both are microscopic. Classification, life cycle and transmission provide the basis for explaining why one control method suits one pathogen but not another.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Protists, oomycetes and viruses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Protists are a diverse set of eukaryotic organisms rather than one uniform shape or lifestyle. The Biology syllabus places Phytophthora with the protist examples and explicitly notes that oomycetes are not classified as true fungi. Viruses are non-cellular infectious agents with genetic material in a protective protein arrangement, sometimes with an envelope, and rely on host cells for replication. They do not have independent cellular metabolism or ribosomes equivalent to a bacterial cell. A virus can contain RNA or DNA, so all viruses should not be described as double-stranded DNA organisms. These distinctions explain why antibiotics targeting bacterial structures do not directly treat a viral infection. Use the particular pathogen's properties instead of assuming every infectious agent has a cell wall and nucleus.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Tuberculosis as a bacterial example", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tuberculosis is caused by bacteria in the Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex and commonly affects lungs, although other tissues can be involved. Transmission can occur through inhalation of airborne infectious particles from a person with infectious respiratory disease. Infection can persist without the same presentation as active disease, so exposure, infection and clinical illness should be distinguished. Damage and inflammation can impair function, while host and environmental conditions influence progression and spread. Antibiotics directed at the bacteria are used in clinical management, and resistance creates additional challenges. In an exam, explain pathogen group, entry route, relevant effect and transmission. This account supports syllabus learning and is not a basis for diagnosing an individual from symptoms or selecting a treatment regimen.", "diagram": "tb"}, {"title": "Crown gall and bacterial effects on plants", "kind": "learn", "text": "Crown gall is associated with pathogenic Agrobacterium-related bacteria, which can enter plant tissues through wounds and transfer genetic material that changes host-cell growth. The resulting galls illustrate how a pathogen can alter host development rather than only killing cells immediately. The same transfer system has informed applications in plant biotechnology, but a disease-producing interaction and a controlled engineered use have different aims and safeguards. Contaminated plant material, soil or tools can contribute to spread under suitable conditions. Management focuses on preventing introduction and limiting transmission, with methods matched to crop and setting. Identify the bacterial agent and host effect, and avoid treating a tumour-like plant growth as evidence that the pathogen must be an animal virus.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Chytridiomycosis in amphibians", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chytridiomycosis is caused by chytrid fungi, including Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis in a major amphibian disease context. Infection of skin can disrupt functions important to amphibian water and ion balance, contributing to severe outcomes in susceptible species. Motile spores and movement of infected hosts or contaminated material can contribute to transmission. Vulnerability differs among species and environmental conditions, so the same exposure need not produce identical effects everywhere. The case connects disease ecology with homeostasis and biodiversity decline. Explain why skin damage is especially important in the host rather than assuming skin only protects appearance. Management can include hygiene and control of movement, but evidence and local ecological conditions determine which interventions are practical and effective.", "diagram": "chytrid"}, {"title": "Malaria and its two-host life cycle", "kind": "learn", "text": "Malaria is caused by Plasmodium parasites, which are protists, and transmitted in the main natural cycle through infected female Anopheles mosquitoes. Infective stages enter a human during feeding, develop initially in liver cells and subsequently undergo stages involving red blood cells. Some stages taken up by another mosquito continue the cycle there. Red-cell infection and associated responses contribute to illness. The mosquito is not the malaria pathogen itself, and ordinary contact between people is not the main transmission route represented by this cycle. Identify the human and mosquito stages relevant to an intervention. Reducing mosquito contact or suitable breeding conditions affects a different part of the cycle from a medicine acting on parasite stages in a host.", "diagram": "malaria"}, {"title": "Phytophthora dieback in Western Australia", "kind": "learn", "text": "Phytophthora cinnamomi is an oomycete associated with dieback in many susceptible plants, including relevant Western Australian examples such as banksias, jarrah and grass trees. It affects roots and can impair water and nutrient uptake, leading to changes in plant condition and survival. Movement of contaminated soil, water, plant material or equipment can introduce it into new areas. Moist conditions can support relevant life-cycle and dispersal processes, while vulnerability depends on plant species and environmental conditions. The syllabus distinguishes it from true fungi, despite the historical use of fungus-like descriptions. Explain how root effects connect to the whole plant and ecosystem. Hygiene, clean movement pathways and appropriate access management aim to reduce spread rather than guaranteeing eradication of an already affected landscape.", "diagram": "dieback"}, {"title": "Influenza and zoonotic transmission", "kind": "learn", "text": "Influenza is caused by viruses that infect suitable host cells, commonly affecting respiratory tissues. Spread depends on infectious particles and contact patterns, with environmental and host factors influencing risk. The Biology syllabus defines a zoonosis as a disease that can be transmitted between vertebrate species; some influenza contexts illustrate this broader definition. A change in host range or genetic properties can affect transmission, but not every influenza infection represents a new cross-species event. The virus relies on host cellular machinery, so antibacterial antibiotics do not directly target it. Describe the agent, affected host system and transmission route with the required specificity. Avoid applying one case's host range to every influenza virus or confusing the pathogen with the symptoms it produces.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ross River virus and mosquito vectors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ross River virus is a mosquito-borne virus in an Australian context. Transmission involves suitable mosquitoes and vertebrate hosts, with reservoir and amplifying roles depending on the ecological setting. In humans, illness can include joint pain and other systemic effects, but the lesson's purpose is the transmission model rather than diagnosis. Rainfall, breeding habitat, temperature and human exposure can influence the opportunities for vector contact. The presence of mosquitoes alone does not prove transmission because the virus and susceptible hosts must also be involved. Explain the links among agent, vector, host and environment, then identify a control measure that breaks a particular link. A comparison with malaria should distinguish viral versus protist agents while recognising the shared importance of mosquito-mediated transfer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Viral diseases of honeybees", "kind": "learn", "text": "Honeybees can be affected by several viruses, including deformed wing virus in an important example. Transmission pathways can involve contact, contaminated materials and vector-associated movement, with Varroa mites playing a major role in many contexts. Viral infection can affect development or colony performance, while colony outcomes also depend on nutrition, other pathogens and environmental stresses. The virus is the pathogen and the mite can facilitate transmission; these roles should not be collapsed into one organism. Movement of colonies or equipment can connect populations, making biosecurity and monitoring relevant. Avoid claiming every poorly performing colony has one viral cause. A defensible explanation identifies the named example, route and host effect while considering interacting factors.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparing pathogen success and evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "For each prescribed example, organise the explanation around pathogen type, structure, entry, effect, transmission and an appropriate control link. Compare diseases using the same criteria rather than presenting disconnected facts. A pathogen's rapid replication, environmental persistence or host range can contribute to spread, but the outcome also depends on host density, defences and movement. Detecting genetic material can support identification but may require additional evidence to establish infectious viability or disease causation. Historical and current classifications can differ, as with oomycetes, so use the course convention and explain the distinction. A complete causal account links the life cycle to the intervention and identifies what observation would show that the proposed measure is working.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Required pathogen comparisons", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Required pathogen comparisons</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Named example</th><th scope=\"col\">Agent/group</th><th scope=\"col\">Route and relevant effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tuberculosis</td><td>Mycobacterium tuberculosis; bacterium</td><td>Airborne particles; often lung disease</td></tr><tr><td>Crown gall</td><td>Agrobacterium; bacterium</td><td>Plant wounds/DNA transfer; growth changes and galls</td></tr><tr><td>Chytridiomycosis</td><td>Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis; fungus</td><td>Skin infection in susceptible amphibians; water/ion disruption</td></tr><tr><td>Malaria</td><td>Plasmodium; protozoan/protist</td><td>Female Anopheles vector; liver and red-cell stages</td></tr><tr><td>Phytophthora dieback</td><td>P. cinnamomi; oomycete/water mould</td><td>Soil/water movement; susceptible plant-root damage</td></tr><tr><td>Influenza</td><td>Influenza virus</td><td>Respiratory route; host-dependent viral replication</td></tr><tr><td>Ross River disease</td><td>Ross River virus</td><td>Mosquito vectors; human illness including joint symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Deformed wing disease</td><td>Deformed wing virus</td><td>Honey bees; Varroa can vector; wing/colony effects</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Agent or vector", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Agent or vector", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Dieback pathway", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Dieback pathway", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Virus comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Virus comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Crown gall", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Chytrid mechanism", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Disease comparisons", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Outbreak evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Agent or vector", "prompt": "For malaria, name the pathogen group and vector and trace the key host stages without labelling the mosquito as the pathogen.", "steps": ["[2] Plasmodium is a protozoan/protist parasite; an infected female Anopheles mosquito is the vector.", "[2] In the human host, parasites first develop in the liver and then cycle through red blood cells.", "[2] A feeding mosquito can acquire transmissible stages and support further development before transmission; the vector and pathogen are distinct organisms."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q1"}, {"title": "Dieback pathway", "prompt": "Explain why soil on footwear can spread Phytophthora dieback and identify the pathogen’s group and plant effect.", "steps": ["[2] Phytophthora cinnamomi is an oomycete/water mould, included with protist-like pathogens in this course, not a true fungus.", "[2] Infected soil/water can transport the organism into susceptible areas; root damage disrupts water/nutrient uptake.", "[2] Appropriate soil-movement and equipment hygiene reduce transfer risk but do not prove eradication from an infected site."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q2"}, {"title": "Virus comparison", "prompt": "Compare influenza virus with a bacterial pathogen in cell organisation, reproduction and antibiotic response at a conceptual level.", "steps": ["[2] A bacterium is a prokaryotic cell with cellular machinery; a virus is non-cellular and depends on host machinery for replication.", "[2] Viruses carry genetic material but do not reproduce by their own independent cellular division.", "[2] Antibiotics acting on bacterial targets do not directly treat viral replication; interventions depend on the specific pathogen and evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q3"}, {"title": "Crown gall", "prompt": "Explain how Agrobacterium can cause crown gall and why this biological mechanism has relevance to gene technology.", "steps": ["[2] Bacteria entering suitable plant wounds can transfer DNA into plant cells.", "[2] Transferred functions can alter growth regulation and produce galls.", "[2] The transfer capacity can be adapted to introduce selected constructs in plant biotechnology, with the harmful functions modified/removed in suitable systems."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q4"}, {"title": "Chytrid mechanism", "prompt": "Explain how chytrid infection can harm amphibians and why skin hygiene and avoiding movement of infected material matter.", "steps": ["[2] Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis is a fungus infecting susceptible amphibian skin.", "[2] Damage disrupts important skin functions including water/ion balance and can contribute to mortality.", "[2] Infective stages/material can spread through contact and water; appropriate movement and equipment hygiene aim to reduce introduction without guaranteeing a cure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q5"}, {"title": "Disease comparisons", "prompt": "Compare Ross River virus, deformed wing virus and tuberculosis by agent, host/vector relationship and a distinct effect.", "steps": ["[2] Ross River virus is mosquito-borne and can cause illness including joint symptoms in people; the mosquito is a vector.", "[2] Deformed wing virus infects honey bees and can affect wings/colony health; Varroa mites can vector it.", "[2] Tuberculosis is bacterial, caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis in the standard example, transmitted through airborne particles and often affecting lungs."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q6"}, {"title": "Outbreak evidence", "prompt": "A fictional reserve records amphibian deaths, a positive chytrid DNA assay and recent movement of wet equipment between ponds. Evaluate the inference and propose an investigation and response. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Chytrid is a plausible fungal skin pathogen capable of impairing water/ion balance in susceptible amphibians.", "[2] Positive DNA supports genetic-material detection but alone does not prove organism viability or sole cause of deaths.", "[2] Equipment movement is a plausible transfer route but temporal association alone does not demonstrate causation.", "[2] Use validated assays/controls, comparable affected/unaffected-site observations and appropriate authorised diagnostic evidence.", "[2] Apply appropriate movement/equipment hygiene and avoid unnecessary handling/spread, following relevant conservation guidance.", "[2] Consider environmental stress and other agents, monitor outcomes and report limits rather than promising eradication or attributing every death to one test result."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-pathogens-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the malaria pathogen in the named example?", "o": ["Plasmodium", "Anopheles itself", "A phospholipid", "Mycobacterium tuberculosis"], "a": 0, "why": "Plasmodium is the parasite; infected female Anopheles mosquitoes provide the vector route."}, {"q": "How should Phytophthora be distinguished from chytrid?", "o": ["Oomycete versus true fungus", "Both are viruses", "Both are mammalian cells", "Chytrid is a bacterium"], "a": 0, "why": "Phytophthora is a water mould/oomycete, while Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis is a fungus affecting amphibians."}, {"q": "What limits independent viral reproduction?", "o": ["Dependence on host cellular machinery", "Presence of a complete bacterial cell", "Every virus containing ribosomes", "Every virus performing binary fission"], "a": 0, "why": "Viruses are non-cellular agents and rely on host processes for replication rather than independent cell division."}, {"q": "What is Agrobacterium’s relevant plant mechanism?", "o": ["Transfer of DNA contributing to gall formation", "Mosquito-vector transmission only", "Formation of mammalian alveoli", "Release of malaria parasites"], "a": 0, "why": "DNA transfer can alter plant growth and has been adapted in plant gene-technology systems."}, {"q": "What can a positive environmental pathogen-DNA assay alone fail to establish?", "o": ["Viability and sole cause of disease", "Detection of the matching target under the assay", "Any need for controls", "Any presence of genetic material"], "a": 0, "why": "DNA detection must be interpreted with assay controls, viability/diagnostic evidence and alternative explanations."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "24 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-disease-control", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Surviving in a changing environment", "title": "Disease transmission, immunity, prevention and population models", "scope": "Transmission pathways; host density and travel; vaccination and herd effects; antimicrobials, resistance, quarantine, vectors and model evaluation.", "screens": [{"title": "Transmission pathways and their control points", "kind": "learn", "text": "Pathogens can spread through routes including direct contact, infectious respiratory particles, contaminated food or water, contaminated objects, sexual transmission or vectors. The relevant route depends on the agent and its life cycle. A preventive measure should break a stated link, such as reducing contaminated-water exposure or contact with infectious mosquitoes. A single measure does not necessarily address every route. Movement of organisms, materials or people can connect formerly separated populations. Distinguish an agent entering a new area from sustained spread after entry, which requires suitable hosts and conditions. Explain the pathway from source to new host rather than simply saying disease is contagious. A control strategy can reduce risk without guaranteeing zero transmission under every circumstance.", "diagram": "transmission"}, {"title": "Host density, susceptibility and healthcare", "kind": "learn", "text": "Greater host density can increase opportunities for relevant contact, but transmission also depends on behaviour, pathogen survival, immunity and the environment. Variation in housing, water quality, sanitation and access to healthcare can alter exposure, detection and outcomes. Migration and international travel can move infectious agents between populations. The 2027 syllabus clarifies these interacting factors in urban susceptibility. Avoid attributing disease spread to a population's identity rather than the actual transmission and resource conditions. A comparison between cities needs comparable surveillance, denominators and time periods. A larger recorded number of cases can reflect a larger population or better detection rather than a higher individual risk. Explain which factor affects exposure, susceptibility, duration of infectiousness or the measurement itself.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Incidence, prevalence, epidemics and pandemics", "kind": "learn", "text": "Incidence concerns new cases over a defined interval in a defined population, while prevalence concerns existing cases at a particular time or over a stated period. State the denominator when comparing rates. An epidemic is an increase above the expected level in a population or area, while a pandemic involves widespread international or global spread in the relevant public-health context. Endemic occurrence does not necessarily mean low severity or constant low numbers. Different definitions and measurement systems must be considered when interpreting data. A decline in detected cases could reflect reduced transmission, changed testing or reporting delays. Use appropriate population and time comparisons, and distinguish a descriptive label from an explanation of why the pattern occurred.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Host barriers and immune responses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Host defences include physical and chemical barriers and responses that act after entry. Innate mechanisms provide relatively rapid broad responses, while adaptive responses recognise particular antigens and can generate immunological memory. B cells can contribute antibodies, and T-cell responses have complementary roles in a simplified vertebrate account. These mechanisms help explain immunisation and susceptibility, though the Biology syllabus does not require the full detail of every Human Biology immune pathway. Infection outcome depends on both agent and host, and an effective response may reduce severity without completely blocking every infection. Explain how a defence influences entry, replication or spread. Immunity is not one permanent on–off state identical for all pathogens and people.", "diagram": "immunity"}, {"title": "Vaccination and immune memory", "kind": "learn", "text": "Vaccination presents appropriate antigenic information or instructions that stimulate protective immune responses without requiring the same disease experience as natural infection. On later exposure, memory responses can improve the speed or effectiveness of defence against the relevant agent. Vaccines differ in their mechanisms, effectiveness, dosing and duration of protection, so avoid universal claims that every vaccine completely prevents infection forever. Protection against severe disease and protection against transmission are related but can differ. The educational account should link the stimulus, specific response, memory and population consequence. Current clinical schedules are outside the lesson's purpose and should be checked through appropriate health guidance. Use evidence for the particular vaccine and disease when evaluating a supplied claim or study.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Herd immunity and model assumptions", "kind": "learn", "text": "When enough people have effective immunity that reduces transmission, susceptible people can receive indirect protection because infectious chains are less likely to continue. This herd effect depends on transmission route, population contact structure, immunity effectiveness and how protection is distributed. It does not imply each unvaccinated person is guaranteed safe. A simple homogeneous model gives an illustrative threshold of approximately 1 − 1/R₀ for effective immunity, but real conditions and vaccine effectiveness can require different coverage. This numerical expression is an extension teaching model, not a universal policy target. A disease not transmitted person-to-person does not receive the same herd protection mechanism. Explain how reduced availability of susceptible hosts interrupts chains, and recognise that immunity can change over time.", "diagram": "herd"}, {"title": "Antibiotics, antivirals and selective action", "kind": "learn", "text": "Antibiotics target suitable bacterial processes or structures, while antivirals target particular stages of viral replication. They are not interchangeable categories, and effectiveness depends on the agent, susceptibility and biological setting. Fungal and protist infections can require other specific controls. A drug that disrupts a bacterial cell wall cannot directly act on a virus lacking that cellular structure. Treatment can reduce pathogen burden or infectious duration under appropriate clinical conditions, but it does not replace prevention of exposure. The lesson explains mechanisms rather than prescribing regimens. When evaluating a supplied intervention, identify its target and the evidence for effect. Distinguish failure due to resistant pathogens from lack of suitability for an entirely different pathogen group.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Resistance, selection and responsible interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Resistance arises when inherited properties allow pathogens to survive or reproduce despite a control measure. Mutation and, in some organisms, genetic exchange provide variation; selection changes its frequency when susceptible competitors are suppressed. The medicine does not necessarily cause the specific useful mutation in response to need. Resistant populations can spread between hosts or regions, linking evolution with public health and biosecurity. Surveillance and susceptibility evidence help identify the problem. A graph showing reduced effectiveness alone does not establish the exact genetic mechanism, because adherence, exposure and other factors can also matter. Explain the population process and avoid saying the person becomes genetically resistant to an antibiotic. Clinical decisions require professional guidance beyond an educational example.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Quarantine, physical prevention and vector control", "kind": "learn", "text": "Quarantine limits movement or contact to reduce introduction or spread of organisms and infectious agents. Biosecurity combines surveillance, movement rules, hygiene and targeted responses, supporting agriculture and natural ecosystems as well as human health. Physical preventive measures include appropriate sanitation, barriers, ventilation or reduction of vector exposure depending on the transmission route. Vector control can target breeding habitat or particular life-cycle stages but should account for non-target ecological effects and resistance. No method should be described as universally sufficient. Identify the control point and expected effect, then use monitoring to evaluate it. International communication matters because trade, travel and organism movement connect jurisdictions, and delayed information can reduce the effectiveness of coordinated action.", "diagram": "biosecurity"}, {"title": "Disease models, climate and uncertainty", "kind": "learn", "text": "Models can simulate pathogen spread and compare interventions using assumptions about contact, susceptibility, infectious duration and other processes. Increased processing capacity and data storage, emphasised in the 2027 syllabus, permit investigation of more variables but do not guarantee correct predictions. Calibration, validation and uncertainty analysis remain necessary. Climate change can alter mosquito habitat and activity, but disease distribution also depends on agents, hosts, infrastructure and control, so temperature alone is not a complete forecast. A model scenario is different from an observed outcome. Evaluate the data quality, assumptions, sensitivity and population to which the result applies. Explain how a proposed intervention changes a model parameter and what evidence would be needed to test its real-world effect.", "diagram": "diseasemodel"}, {"title": "Transmission, immunity and evidence", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Transmission, immunity and evidence</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th><th scope=\"col\">Exam caution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Incidence</td><td>New cases over specified interval</td><td>State denominator or person-time</td></tr><tr><td>Prevalence</td><td>Existing cases at specified time/interval</td><td>Distinct from newly arising cases</td></tr><tr><td>Endemic/epidemic/pandemic</td><td>Usual persistence / above-expected cases / widespread geographic epidemic</td><td>Extent is not identical to severity</td></tr><tr><td>Vaccination</td><td>Stimulates relevant adaptive response/memory</td><td>Protection and transmission effects vary</td></tr><tr><td>Antimicrobial resistance</td><td>Heritable pathogen variants survive/reproduce under relevant selection</td><td>Treatment failure has other causes; patients do not become resistant bacteria</td></tr><tr><td>Model</td><td>Conditional prediction using data and assumptions</td><td>Validate with independent observations and uncertainty</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Incidence or prevalence", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Incidence or prevalence", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Vaccination mechanism", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Vaccination mechanism", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Resistance selection", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Resistance selection", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transmission control", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Population immunity extension", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Model evaluation", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Disease management essay", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Incidence or prevalence", "prompt": "In a population of 1,000, 40 new cases occur during a month and 70 people have the disease on its final day. Distinguish the measures and calculate simple proportions.", "steps": ["[2] Monthly new-case proportion=40/1000=4%, using the stipulated denominator; incidence concerns new cases over time.", "[2] End-day prevalence=70/1000=7%, describing existing cases at that time.", "[1] Real incidence-rate calculations may require susceptible populations or person-time, and denominators/definitions must be stated."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q1"}, {"title": "Vaccination mechanism", "prompt": "Explain how vaccination can improve a later response to a pathogen without implying perfect protection in every person.", "steps": ["[2] Antigens or instructions producing antigens stimulate an adaptive immune response.", "[2] Appropriate memory cells permit a faster/stronger response on later relevant exposure.", "[2] Protection varies with pathogen, vaccine, time and host; reduced severe disease and reduced transmission are distinct outcomes."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q2"}, {"title": "Resistance selection", "prompt": "A resistant allele becomes more common after antibiotic exposure. Explain selection and give two reasons why treatment failure alone does not prove inherited resistance.", "steps": ["[2] Resistant bacteria survive/reproduce disproportionately, so their hereditary variants rise in frequency.", "[2] Inappropriate drug targeting or inadequate delivery/exposure can also cause failure.", "[2] A viral cause or other diagnostic issue may not respond to the antibiotic; laboratory/clinical evidence is needed to distinguish causes, without advising a personal regimen."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q3"}, {"title": "Transmission control", "prompt": "Match a justified control approach to contaminated drinking water, respiratory transmission and a mosquito vector, giving mechanisms rather than just names.", "steps": ["[2] Safe water treatment/handling interrupts ingestion of the relevant agent.", "[2] Appropriate ventilation and other route-specific measures reduce exposure to infectious respiratory particles.", "[2] Suitable vector-habitat management and bite protection reduce mosquito contact, with non-target/environmental effects assessed."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q4"}, {"title": "Population immunity extension", "prompt": "In an ideal homogeneous model with R₀=4 and perfect lasting transmission-blocking immunity, calculate 1−1/R₀ and state two limitations.", "steps": ["[2] Threshold fraction=1−1/4=0.75, or 75%.", "[2] Unequal contact patterns, imperfect/waning immunity and changing transmission violate the simplified assumptions.", "[1] This extension is not a universal clinical/policy threshold or a guarantee of no cases."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q5"}, {"title": "Model evaluation", "prompt": "An outbreak model predicts cases using population density and assumes every person has equal contact. Explain one use, two limitations and a way to check its predictions.", "steps": ["[2] It can compare conditional scenarios and examine effects of specified variables.", "[2] Equal-contact assumptions and omitted travel/healthcare/behaviour can distort transmission estimates.", "[2] Compare predictions with independent surveillance data, assess uncertainty and update parameters rather than treating computing power as proof of accuracy."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q6"}, {"title": "Disease management essay", "prompt": "For a fictional mosquito-borne outbreak, propose an integrated evidence-based response and explain why a single intervention or a rising case count is insufficient. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Identify the agent, vector and relevant reservoirs/host routes before choosing measures.", "[2] Reduce vector contact through appropriate habitat management and bite protection, while monitoring ecological effects.", "[2] Use surveillance with consistent case definitions, testing and denominators to distinguish true risk from altered detection.", "[2] Assess travel, living conditions, environmental variables and healthcare access rather than attributing risk solely to density.", "[2] Vaccination or other agent-specific measures require relevant evidence; antibiotics do not directly target a virus.", "[2] Compare intervention outcomes over time with justified controls/models and uncertainty, adapting the response while respecting consent and privacy."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-disease-control-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Which measure concerns new cases over time?", "o": ["Incidence", "Point prevalence", "Species richness", "Ploidy"], "a": 0, "why": "Incidence concerns newly arising cases during a defined interval, with an appropriate denominator or person-time definition."}, {"q": "What supports faster later adaptive responses?", "o": ["Relevant immune memory", "Every antibiotic creating antibodies", "All infections being identical", "A vector becoming a gene"], "a": 0, "why": "Memory from a suitable antigen exposure can improve later responses, with effectiveness and duration depending on context."}, {"q": "Why do antibacterial antibiotics not directly treat influenza virus?", "o": ["Their bacterial targets differ from viral replication machinery", "Viruses are always larger bacteria", "Influenza has a bacterial wall", "All drugs target the same pathway"], "a": 0, "why": "Pathogen biology determines targets; a viral agent is not a bacterium with the usual antibacterial target processes."}, {"q": "Which model assumption can undermine outbreak forecasts?", "o": ["Homogeneous contact despite real contact differences", "Explicitly stated uncertainty", "Checking independent data", "Calibrated parameter measurement"], "a": 0, "why": "Unequal contact, mobility and omitted variables can alter transmission, so models require evaluation against evidence."}, {"q": "What does pandemic primarily concern?", "o": ["Widespread geographic epidemic spread", "Necessarily maximal fatality", "Only one small local cluster", "Always a harmless infection"], "a": 0, "why": "Geographic extent and severity are different dimensions; endemic also does not mean invariably mild or rare."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}, {"id": "bio12-inquiry-exams", "year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Surviving in a changing environment", "title": "Biological inquiry, evidence and ATAR exam technique", "scope": "Investigation design, controls, data analysis, uncertainty, model evaluation, scientific reports and integrated Unit 3/4 examination practice.", "screens": [{"title": "A question, mechanism and testable prediction", "kind": "learn", "text": "A useful investigation starts with a clear question and a predicted relationship grounded in biology. Operational definitions state what will be changed or compared and what will be measured, including units, timing and scoring rules. A directional hypothesis predicts an increase, decrease or other defined change under specified conditions. For example, increasing a particular substrate concentration is predicted to raise initial enzyme activity over a range where substrate limits the reaction. The mechanism makes the prediction meaningful. A hypothesis is not a conclusion already known to be true, and a failed prediction can still provide useful evidence. Distinguish an experiment that manipulates a factor from a field or observational comparison measuring existing differences.", "diagram": "inquiry"}, {"title": "Controls, confounding and fair comparisons", "kind": "learn", "text": "Control relevant variables that could affect the dependent measure, and use a comparison condition suitable to the question. Standardising temperature, sample size, exposure time and preparation can improve the validity of an enzyme comparison. Random allocation can help distribute participant or specimen differences between conditions. A confound varies systematically with the explanatory variable, offering an alternative cause of the observed difference. A control group is not necessarily a group receiving no materials at all; it must address the relevant effect. A blank can identify background signal in an assay. Explain why a specific control matters biologically rather than listing generic controls that do not apply to the procedure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Replication, sampling and pseudoreplication", "kind": "learn", "text": "Independent replicates help estimate variation and assess how consistently a result occurs. Repeated measurements from one specimen can characterise that specimen or instrument but do not provide the same independence as multiple specimens. Treating several leaves from one plant as equivalent to several independently grown plants can overstate the biological replication in a treatment comparison. Sample selection also affects generalisability: one convenient strain, age class or habitat may not represent the wider population. Larger sample size can reduce sampling uncertainty but does not remove a systematic confound or miscalibration. Identify the experimental unit and explain which observations are independent. A strong evaluation distinguishes consistency, representativeness and causal validity rather than using reliability as a label for every improvement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Measurement error, accuracy and instrument resolution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random variation can scatter measurements, while systematic error can shift them in a consistent direction. Calibration, suitable blanks and validated methods address certain systematic problems. Repetition helps describe random variability but cannot by itself correct every bias. Instrument resolution is the smallest displayed or distinguishable increment and should guide sensible reporting, without guaranteeing that readings are accurate to all displayed digits. Sample preparation, timing and interpretation can create additional limits. An anomalous reading should be investigated and documented rather than automatically discarded because it weakens a preferred result. Explain how a proposed improvement addresses the named error source. This links data quality to the strength of the conclusion rather than treating extra decimal places as proof of precision or validity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Mean, median, range and probability", "kind": "learn", "text": "The mean is the sum divided by the number of observations; the median is the middle ordered value, or the mean of the two central values for an even-sized dataset. For 4, 6, 6, 8 and 16, the mean is 8 and the median 6. The minimum–maximum interval is 4–16 and its width is 12. State which meaning of range is requested. Probabilities in inheritance should identify the sample space and any conditions, such as affected among sons versus affected among all offspring. Add probabilities for mutually exclusive outcomes and multiply independent event probabilities where the model justifies independence. A calculated expected proportion does not guarantee an exact realised count in a small biological sample.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Graphs, tables and supporting evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Select a representation suited to the data: categories, continuous variables, paired observations or frequency distributions require different choices. Titles, axes, units and legends should make the figure interpretable on its own. Data-based comparisons need numerical evidence rather than saying one line is higher. Read scales carefully and distinguish absolute changes from percentage changes. Percentage change uses the initial value as the denominator, which can matter greatly when starting values differ. A table of means without sample sizes or variability can conceal important uncertainty. Explain what error bars represent if provided. A drawn biological model also needs labels and annotations identifying structures, processes and direction rather than relying on an attractive image to supply missing reasoning.", "diagram": "graphs"}, {"title": "Conclusions, correlations and models", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conclusion should answer the investigation question and match the strength and scope of the evidence. A correlation does not alone establish causation because confounding, reverse direction or other explanations may remain. Controlled manipulation can provide stronger causal evidence, but procedural limitations still matter. A model may explain a pattern and predict outcomes while simplifying mechanisms or assuming conditions not present in reality. Distinguish interpolation within tested values from extrapolation beyond them. Identify assumptions and propose observations that would test them. Evidence-based reasoning does not require certainty about every outcome, but it does require explaining why the observations support the claim and where confidence is limited.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ethics, safety and scientific communication", "kind": "learn", "text": "Biological research needs safe procedures, relevant permissions and ethical consideration of organisms, people and environments. Animal research principles include replacement, reduction and refinement. Avoid unnecessary harm, unsafe pathogen culturing or disclosure of sensitive personal information. A scientific report links aim, hypothesis, method, results, analysis, conclusion and evaluation with references to relevant sources. Separate measured observations from interpretation and use appropriate terminology and units. Evaluate sources by expertise, methods, transparency, currency and corroboration, rather than accepting an attractive graph or a prestigious label without scrutiny. An educational scenario using supplied fictional data can practise reasoning without requiring an unsafe experiment. Good communication allows another reader to understand both the finding and its limits.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Command words and structure of written answers", "kind": "learn", "text": "State asks for a concise fact; describe identifies features or patterns; explain connects the result to mechanisms; compare gives similarities and differences using common criteria; evaluate judges a claim or method with evidence and limitations. Follow the actual wording and mark allocation. A structure question needs physical features, while a physiological question needs processes, so avoid replacing one with the other. Distinct marks require distinct relevant points rather than restating the same idea in several sentences. For an extended answer, plan linked sections, use labelled diagrams when they clarify mechanisms and apply evidence to the scenario. Terms such as gene, allele, chromosome, genotype and phenotype should remain consistent throughout the response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The ATAR paper and a realistic preparation plan", "kind": "learn", "text": "The reviewed 2025 Biology paper has ten minutes reading time and three hours working time. It includes 30 multiple-choice questions, five compulsory structured short-answer questions and extended-answer choices requiring one Unit 3 answer and one Unit 4 answer. The section weightings are 30%, 50% and 20%, and the suggested times are 40, 90 and 50 minutes. Raw marks and weighted percentages are different: the short-answer section contains 100 raw marks, while each selected extended answer has 20. Check the official front cover for your cohort. The module's integrated practice includes original 20-mark Unit 3 and Unit 4 alternatives with suggested marking points. Use official past papers for full timed practice, then diagnose missing mechanisms, data reasoning and terminology rather than judging readiness from concept-check scores alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "2025 official paper structure and command words", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>2025 official paper structure and command words</h3><p>Use this reference to check distinctions after studying the explanations. It is not a substitute for a reasoned exam answer.</p><div class=\"bio-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Item</th><th scope=\"col\">Requirement</th><th scope=\"col\">Planning note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reading/working time</td><td>10 minutes reading; 3 hours working</td><td>Use official paper for a timed simulation</td></tr><tr><td>Section One</td><td>30 MC questions; 30 raw marks; 30%</td><td>Suggested 40 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Section Two</td><td>5 compulsory short-answer questions; 100 raw marks; 50%</td><td>Suggested 90 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Section Three</td><td>Choose 1 of 2 Unit 3 and 1 of 2 Unit 4 questions; each 20 raw marks; combined 20%</td><td>Suggested 50 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Explain</td><td>Link causes, mechanisms and outcomes</td><td>Apply the scenario, not just a definition</td></tr><tr><td>Compare</td><td>Relevant similarities and differences</td><td>Use the same dimensions for both items</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluate</td><td>Judge evidence and consequences</td><td>Discuss specific strengths, limitations and uncertainty</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Worked question: Interpret a mean", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Interpret a mean", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Pseudoreplication", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Pseudoreplication", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Exam planning", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Exam planning", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Unit 3 extended response A", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Unit 3 extended response B", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Unit 4 extended response A", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Unit 4 extended response B", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and full examination practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Official course versions and examination practice</h3><p>Currency checked 8 October 2026. Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the currently prescribed syllabus effective 2024. Published 2027 versions were also checked. The relevant 2027 changes to conservation, cellular technologies, bioinformatics and disease modelling are explained within this resource. Check the official course page for the version applying to your cohort.</p><p>These questions and suggested marking points are original ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official past examination and marking key for a full timed paper. Concept checks complement written answers and do not establish exam readiness by themselves.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/biology\">SCSA Biology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/1232615/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1089654/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus currently in force</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264876/Biology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1264878/Biology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232645/2025-BLY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0006/1232646/2025-BLY-Ratified-Marking-Key-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 ratified marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/1242526/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-BLY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.health.wa.gov.au/Articles/J_M/Mosquito-borne-diseases/Ross-River-virus-and-Barmah-Forest-virus\">WA Health: Ross River virus</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\">CDC: malaria life cycle</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis\">WHO: tuberculosis</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance\">WHO: antimicrobial resistance</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.wa.gov.au/service/environment/conservation/phytophthora-dieback\">WA Government: Phytophthora dieback</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.dcceew.gov.au/environment/invasive-species/diseases-fungi-and-parasites/chytrid-amphibian-fungus-chytridiomycosis\">DCCEEW: amphibian chytrid</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.agriculture.gov.au/pests-diseases-weeds/bees\">DAFF: honey-bee pests and diseases</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.rhs.org.uk/disease/crown-gall\">RHS: crown gall biology</a></li></ul><p>Repository Biology PowerPoints were used as supporting material and checked against the current syllabus. Text, exam examples and SVG teaching models are written for this student course rather than copied from publisher slides. Numerical scenarios labelled fictional are teaching datasets.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Interpret a mean", "prompt": "Five independent plant-growth measurements are 4,6,6,8,16 mm. Calculate mean, median and range width and explain why the largest value should not be deleted automatically.", "steps": ["[2] Mean=40/5=8 mm and median=6 mm.", "[1] Measurements span 4–16 mm, giving range width 12 mm.", "[2] The large value affects mean but may be real variation; check documented measurement issues rather than deleting it solely to improve a result."], "allocations": [2, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q1"}, {"title": "Pseudoreplication", "prompt": "A student tests salinity using one plant per treatment and measures 20 leaves on each. Explain the replication problem and a stronger design.", "steps": ["[2] Leaves share plant-level conditions and are not 20 independent treatment replicates.", "[2] One plant per treatment confounds treatment with individual plant differences.", "[2] Randomly allocate several independent comparable plants per treatment; leaf measurements can contribute within-plant information but not replace independent plants."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q2"}, {"title": "Exam planning", "prompt": "An original 8-mark question says “Explain two mechanisms reducing water loss and evaluate the investigation.” Outline a response plan distinguishing explanation from evaluation.", "steps": ["[2] Allocate space and time to both requested mechanisms and investigation evaluation according to marks.", "[2] For each mechanism link structure/process to water gradient or loss, applying the stated organism/scenario.", "[2] Evaluate validity, independent replication, controls and uncertainty with effects on conclusions.", "[2] Use distinct relevant points rather than repeating “saves water”; a labelled model supplements but does not replace required explanation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 8, "practice": false, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q3"}, {"title": "Unit 3 extended response A", "prompt": "Original 20-mark practice option: Explain continuity of hereditary information and generation of variation through DNA replication, meiosis, fertilisation and mutation. Evaluate how PCR and gel electrophoresis can provide evidence about a genetic difference.", "steps": ["[4] Explain complementary antiparallel semiconservative replication and correct copying before division, distinguishing chromosome number from DNA amount.", "[4] Explain homologous pairing/crossing over and independent assortment in meiosis I, homologues then sisters separating, and haploid products without a second replication.", "[3] Explain random fertilisation restoring diploidy and combining existing alleles, with mutation adding possible new alleles rather than directed useful change.", "[3] Link DNA sequence through transcription/translation to a potential protein/phenotype, acknowledging redundancy and environment.", "[3] PCR uses defined primers and denaturation/annealing/extension to amplify a selected target, requiring positive and no-template controls.", "[3] Gel separates fragments by migration/size toward the positive electrode; ladder and appropriate controls aid inference, but equal-size bands do not prove equal sequence or full phenotype."], "allocations": [4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3], "marks": 20, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q4"}, {"title": "Unit 3 extended response B", "prompt": "Original 20-mark practice option: A population becomes isolated and declines from 500 to 20 individuals. Explain how its gene pool may change and how reproductive isolation could develop. Evaluate genetic and ecological evidence for a conservation intervention.", "steps": ["[4] Define the gene pool and allele-frequency change, with drift/bottleneck potentially losing variants by chance in the small population.", "[4] Explain natural selection using heritable variation and differential reproductive success, contrasting it with chance drift and non-directed mutation.", "[3] Reduced gene flow permits divergence; reproductive isolation may develop but geographic separation alone is not instant speciation.", "[3] Genetic sequences/pedigree or breeding data help assess relatedness/diversity, with gene choice and sampling limits.", "[3] Appropriate immigrant breeding can restore some alleles while disease, compatibility/local adaptation and habitat risks require assessment.", "[3] Monitor independent ecological/genetic evidence over time and compare alternatives, reporting uncertainty and addressing the original threats."], "allocations": [4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3], "marks": 20, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q5"}, {"title": "Unit 4 extended response A", "prompt": "Original 20-mark practice option: Compare thermal and water-balance survival of an endothermic mammal, a freshwater bony fish and a salt-tolerant plant. Explain mechanisms, feedback where relevant, and energetic or carbon trade-offs.", "steps": ["[4] Explain mammalian thermal negative feedback with receptors, coordination and effectors opposing deviations, linking circulation/metabolic/evaporative responses to heat exchange.", "[4] Explain water-balance challenge and ADH-mediated permeability/reabsorption, conserving water through reduced urine losses rather than creating water.", "[3] Explain freshwater-fish net water gain and ion loss, with dilute urine and active ion uptake addressing different challenges.", "[3] Explain salt-lowered soil water potential and ion toxicity in plants, with appropriate salt exclusion/compartmentalisation/excretion mechanisms.", "[3] Explain cuticle/stomatal/root or storage responses and link reduced stomatal loss to restricted CO₂ uptake.", "[3] Compare costs of metabolic heat, evaporation, ion transport and reduced carbon fixation, noting environmental/species limits and avoiding universal adaptation claims."], "allocations": [4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3], "marks": 20, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q6"}, {"title": "Unit 4 extended response B", "prompt": "Original 20-mark practice option: Compare malaria, tuberculosis and Phytophthora dieback by pathogen, infection route and effect. Explain transmission-control principles and evaluate a computer model of changing disease risk.", "steps": ["[4] Malaria involves Plasmodium protists and female Anopheles vectors, with liver/blood stages and appropriate vector/host distinctions.", "[4] Tuberculosis involves Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacteria and airborne transmission, often affecting lungs; bacterial targets differ from viral ones.", "[3] Phytophthora cinnamomi is an oomycete damaging susceptible roots, moved in water/infected soil; equipment/soil hygiene targets spread.", "[3] Match controls to routes and explain why no single measure guarantees elimination, with non-target and practical limits.", "[3] Model risk using relevant contact density, travel, environment, surveillance/healthcare and pathogen variables; computational capacity aids analysis but does not validate assumptions.", "[3] Compare predictions with independent data, consistent denominators and uncertainty, respecting ethical handling, privacy and conservation needs."], "allocations": [4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 3], "marks": 20, "practice": true, "id": "bio12-inquiry-exams-q7"}], "bank": [{"q": "Which is independent replication for a plant treatment?", "o": ["Several independently allocated plants", "Twenty leaves from one treated plant only", "One plant photographed twenty times", "One reading copied twenty times"], "a": 0, "why": "Within-plant observations do not replace independent treatment units, and pseudoreplication can inflate confidence."}, {"q": "What does calibration mainly address?", "o": ["Systematic measurement error", "Every ecological confounder", "Only increasing sample count", "Automatic proof of causation"], "a": 0, "why": "Calibration against an appropriate standard addresses consistent offsets, while replication and design address other concerns."}, {"q": "For values 4,6,6,8,16, what is the median?", "o": ["6", "8", "16", "4"], "a": 0, "why": "The middle of the five ordered values is 6; the mean is 8 and is more influenced by the largest observation."}, {"q": "What best fulfils an evaluate instruction?", "o": ["Judge evidence with specific strengths, limits and consequences", "Only recite a definition", "Only state a preference", "Repeat the title"], "a": 0, "why": "Evaluation weighs relevant evidence and explains how design, assumptions and uncertainty affect conclusions."}, {"q": "What is the 2025 official extended-response selection pattern?", "o": ["One Unit 3 option and one Unit 4 option", "All four options compulsory", "Only one option from either unit", "Thirty essays"], "a": 0, "why": "The 2025 paper requires one of two Unit 3 options and one of two Unit 4 options; each chosen response is worth 20 raw marks."}], "icon": "🌿", "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written exam tasks"}];
